window.PHYSICS_REVIEW_LESSONS=[{"id":"physics-measurement-units","title":"1. Measurements, physical quantities and SI units","body":"1. WHY PHYSICS BEGINS WITH MEASUREMENT\nPhysics studies matter, energy and the laws governing their behaviour. A measurement compares an unknown quantity with an agreed standard. Saying a desk is “long” is vague; saying it is 1.20 m long allows another person to check it. Every measured answer needs a number and a unit.\n\n2. FUNDAMENTAL AND DERIVED QUANTITIES\nFundamental quantities are independent starting quantities: length (metre, m), mass (kilogram, kg), time (second, s), electric current (ampere, A), temperature (kelvin, K), amount of substance (mole, mol) and luminous intensity (candela, cd).\nDerived quantities are formed from fundamental quantities. Area = length × breadth, so its unit is m². Volume is measured in m³. Speed = distance/time, so its unit is m/s. Density = mass/volume, so its unit is kg/m³. Force has unit newton (N), equal to kg m/s².\nMass is the amount of matter; weight is gravitational force. A balance measures mass, while a spring balance measures force. Their units are kg and N respectively.\n\n3. PREFIXES AND UNIT CONVERSION\nkilo (k) means 1,000; centi (c) means 1/100; milli (m) means 1/1,000; micro (μ) means 1/1,000,000.\nTo convert centimetres to metres divide by 100; to convert kilometres to metres multiply by 1,000. Remember that area and volume conversion factors must be squared or cubed: 1 m² = 10,000 cm² and 1 m³ = 1,000,000 cm³.\n\nExample (i)\nConvert 250 cm to metres.\nSolution\nSince 100 cm = 1 m, 250 ÷ 100 = 2.5 m.\n\nExample (ii)\nA rectangular sheet is 40 cm long and 25 cm wide. Find its area in square metres.\nSolution\nFormula: Area = length × breadth.\nConvert first: 40 cm = 0.40 m; 25 cm = 0.25 m.\nArea = 0.40 × 0.25 = 0.10 m².\n\n4. MEASURING INSTRUMENTS AND PRECISION\nA metre rule measures length; a stopwatch measures time; a measuring cylinder measures liquid volume. A vernier caliper measures external/internal diameters and depths; a micrometer screw gauge measures very small thicknesses. Read a scale at eye level to avoid parallax error. Check for zero error before measuring.\nAccuracy means closeness to the true value. Precision means repeated measurements are close to one another. A measurement can be precise but inaccurate if the instrument has a systematic zero error.\n\n5. DIMENSIONS AS A CHECK\nThe dimension of speed is [L T⁻¹]; acceleration is [L T⁻²]; force is [M L T⁻²]. Both sides of a physically correct equation must have the same dimensions. This helps detect a wrong formula, although it cannot prove a formula is fully correct.\n\nExample (iii)\nFind the density of a 600 g block occupying 200 cm³.\nSolution\nFormula: Density = mass ÷ volume.\nDensity = 600 ÷ 200 = 3 g/cm³.\nIn SI units, 3 g/cm³ = 3,000 kg/m³.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nDo not confuse mass with weight, m² with m³, accuracy with precision, or a unit prefix with a power of ten that has not been squared for area.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is the SI unit of mass? Answer: Kilogram (kg).\n2. Why must a physical measurement have a unit? Answer: The unit states the standard used to interpret the number.\n3. Convert 2.5 km to metres. Answer: 2,500 m.\n4. What causes parallax error? Answer: Reading a scale from the wrong viewing angle.\n5. What is the SI unit of density? Answer: kg/m³.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nTry converting measurements before substituting into formulas. Future uploaded JAMB questions on instruments, units, prefixes and dimensions should map to this lesson.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"1. Measurements, physical quantities and SI units","body":"1. WHAT A MEASUREMENT REALLY MEANS\nPhysics explains how matter and energy behave by observing, measuring and testing. A physical quantity is something measurable, such as length, mass or time. A measurement always combines a NUMBER with a UNIT: 2.5 m describes length, while “2.5” alone does not tell us what was measured.\nMeasurements are not perfectly exact. The instrument and the smallest marked division limit how precisely we can report them. In a practical question, write down the measured value, its unit and the instrument used.\n\n2. FUNDAMENTAL QUANTITIES, DERIVED QUANTITIES AND THEIR UNITS\nThe seven SI base quantities and units are length—metre (m); mass—kilogram (kg); time—second (s); electric current—ampere (A); thermodynamic temperature—kelvin (K); amount of substance—mole (mol); luminous intensity—candela (cd). These are starting quantities: their units are not defined by multiplying other SI base units.\nDerived quantities are obtained by combining base quantities. Area = length × breadth, so its unit is m². Volume = length × breadth × height, so its unit is m³. Speed = distance ÷ time, so its unit is m/s. Density = mass ÷ volume, so its unit is kg/m³. Force = mass × acceleration, so 1 newton (N) = 1 kg m/s².\nMass describes the amount of matter in a body, whereas weight is the gravitational force acting on it. Mass is measured in kg with a balance; weight is measured in N with a force meter or spring balance. Formula: W = mg. A body's mass does not change simply because it moves to the Moon, but its weight changes because g changes.\n\nExample (i)\nA block has mass 4 kg. Find its weight where g = 10 N/kg.\nSolution:\nFormula: W = mg.\nW = 4 × 10 = 40 N. Do not report 40 kg: weight is a force.\n\nExample (ii)\nA rectangular floor is 3 m long and 2 m wide. Find its area.\nSolution:\nFormula: Area = length × breadth.\nArea = 3 × 2 = 6 m². Area uses square metres because two lengths are multiplied.\n\n3. PREFIXES, CONVERSIONS AND POWERS OF TEN\nA prefix changes the size of a unit: kilo (k) = 10³; centi (c) = 10⁻²; milli (m) = 10⁻³; micro (μ) = 10⁻⁶. For length, 1 km = 1,000 m, 1 m = 100 cm and 1 m = 1,000 mm.\nTo convert from a smaller length unit to a larger one, divide by the conversion factor. To convert to a smaller unit, multiply. For AREA square the length conversion factor; for VOLUME cube it. This is why 1 m² = (100 cm)² = 10,000 cm² and 1 m³ = (100 cm)³ = 1,000,000 cm³.\n\nExample (iii)\nConvert 250 cm to metres.\nSolution:\nFormula: Metres = centimetres ÷ 100.\n250 ÷ 100 = 2.5 m.\n\nExample (iv)\nA rectangular sheet is 40 cm long and 25 cm wide. Find its area in m².\nSolution:\nFormula: Area = length × breadth.\nConvert lengths first: 40 cm = 0.40 m and 25 cm = 0.25 m.\nArea = 0.40 × 0.25 = 0.10 m².\n\nExample (v)\nConvert 0.020 m³ to cm³.\nSolution:\nFormula: 1 m³ = (100 cm)³ = 1,000,000 cm³.\n0.020 × 1,000,000 = 20,000 cm³.\n\n4. MEASURING INSTRUMENTS AND PRECISION\nChoose an instrument according to the size and shape of what you measure. A metre rule measures ordinary lengths; a stopwatch measures time; a measuring cylinder measures liquid volume. A vernier caliper measures outside diameters, inside diameters and depths. A micrometer screw gauge is useful for very small diameters or thicknesses, such as a thin wire.\nHOW TO READ A VERNIER CALIPER: First close the jaws gently and check whether the zeros coincide. Then place the object between the correct jaws. Read the MAIN SCALE just to the left of the vernier zero. Find the vernier division that lines up exactly with a main-scale mark. Multiply that division number by the instrument's LEAST COUNT, then add it to the main-scale reading. Apply a zero correction if necessary.\nFormula: Observed reading = main-scale reading + (coinciding vernier division × least count).\nFormula: Correct reading = observed reading − signed zero error.\nThe least count is the smallest measurement increment the instrument can distinguish. Different vernier calipers may have different least counts; use the value shown or specified, not a guessed one. A positive zero error is subtracted; a negative zero error is added.\nFor a micrometer, read the sleeve/main scale and the thimble divisions, then correct any zero error. Never squeeze the object so tightly that it changes shape.\nPARALLAX ERROR occurs when you view a scale from an angle instead of directly in front of it. RANDOM ERROR varies from reading to reading; repeat and average measurements to reduce its effect. SYSTEMATIC ERROR, such as a consistent zero error, shifts readings in the same direction and must be corrected. Accuracy means closeness to the true value; precision means repeated values are close together.\n\nExample (vi)\nA vernier caliper has main-scale reading 2.30 cm. Its 6th vernier division aligns and its least count is 0.01 cm. There is no zero error. Find the reading.\nSolution:\nFormula: Reading = main-scale reading + (aligned division × least count).\nReading = 2.30 + (6 × 0.01) = 2.36 cm.\n\nExample (vii)\nA caliper shows 3.48 cm but has a positive zero error of +0.02 cm. Find the corrected reading.\nSolution:\nFormula: Correct reading = observed reading − signed zero error.\nCorrect reading = 3.48 − 0.02 = 3.46 cm.\n\nExample (viii)\nA student measures a rod three times: 12.2 cm, 12.4 cm and 12.3 cm. Find the mean.\nSolution:\nFormula: Mean = sum of readings ÷ number of readings.\nMean = (12.2 + 12.4 + 12.3)/3 = 12.3 cm.\n\n5. DENSITY AND DIMENSIONAL CHECKS\nDensity describes how much mass is packed into a given volume. Formula: ρ = m/V. Two objects of the same volume may have different masses because their densities differ.\nDimensions describe a quantity in terms of mass [M], length [L] and time [T]. Speed has dimension [L T⁻¹], acceleration [L T⁻²], and force [M L T⁻²]. The dimensions on both sides of a correct physical equation must match. This can reveal a wrong formula, though matching dimensions alone cannot guarantee the formula is correct.\n\nExample (ix)\nA block has mass 600 g and volume 200 cm³. Find its density in g/cm³ and kg/m³.\nSolution:\nFormula: Density = mass ÷ volume.\nρ = 600 ÷ 200 = 3 g/cm³.\nSince 1 g/cm³ = 1,000 kg/m³, ρ = 3,000 kg/m³.\n\nExample (x)\nExplain why distance = speed × time is dimensionally consistent.\nSolution:\nFormula: [speed] × [time] = [L T⁻¹] × [T] = [L].\nThe result has the dimension of distance, so the equation passes this dimensional check.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nA numerical answer without a unit is incomplete. Do not confuse mass (kg) with weight (N), or area (m²) with volume (m³). Square or cube unit conversion factors when necessary. On a vernier, read the main scale BEFORE the vernier zero and use the specified least count. A positive zero error must be subtracted, not added. Precision is not the same as accuracy.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is the SI unit of mass? Answer: Kilogram (kg).\n2. Why does 1 m² equal 10,000 cm² rather than 100 cm²? Answer: The length conversion factor 100 must be squared.\n3. A caliper reads 2.50 cm and has a +0.03 cm zero error. What is the corrected reading? Answer: 2.47 cm.\n4. What is parallax error? Answer: An error caused by reading a scale from the wrong viewing angle.\n5. What is the SI unit of density? Answer: kg/m³.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nPractise selecting an instrument, reading the main and vernier scales, correcting zero error, and converting the final answer to the requested unit."},"old":{"title":"1. Measurements, physical quantities and SI units","body":"New syllabus-first Physics lesson; historical year uploads pending."}},{"id":"physics-scalars-vectors","title":"2. Scalars, vectors and vector addition","body":"1. THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN SCALARS AND VECTORS\nA scalar has magnitude only. Examples include mass, time, distance, speed, energy and temperature. A vector has both magnitude and direction. Examples include displacement, velocity, acceleration, force and momentum.\nA car travelling 20 m/s tells us its speed; 20 m/s east tells us its velocity. Distance is the total path travelled, while displacement is the straight-line change from starting point to finishing point with direction.\n\n2. DRAWING VECTORS\nRepresent a vector by an arrow. The arrow length, when drawn to scale, represents magnitude; the arrowhead represents direction. State the scale, such as 1 cm represents 5 N. Two vectors are equal when their magnitudes and directions are equal, even if drawn at different positions.\n\n3. ADDITION BY THE HEAD-TO-TAIL METHOD\nTo add two vectors, draw the second arrow starting at the head of the first. The resultant runs from the first arrow's tail to the last arrow's head. The order of addition does not change the resultant.\nIf two forces act in the same direction, add their magnitudes. If they act in opposite directions, subtract the smaller from the larger and give the direction of the larger.\n\nExample (i)\nTwo horizontal forces of 12 N east and 7 N west act on an object. Find the resultant.\nSolution\nFormula: Resultant for opposite directions = larger force − smaller force.\nR = 12 − 7 = 5 N east.\n\n4. PERPENDICULAR VECTORS\nFor two vectors at right angles, use Pythagoras' theorem: R = √(A² + B²). The direction can be found from tan θ = opposite component ÷ adjacent component.\n\nExample (ii)\nA student walks 3 m east and then 4 m north. Find the displacement.\nSolution\nFormula: R = √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5 m.\nDirection: tan θ = 4/3, so θ ≈ 53.1° north of east.\nDistance travelled = 3 + 4 = 7 m; displacement = 5 m. They are not the same.\n\n5. RESOLVING A VECTOR\nA vector F at angle θ above the positive horizontal has horizontal component Fₓ = F cos θ and vertical component Fᵧ = F sin θ. The horizontal component is adjacent to θ; the vertical is opposite. Always check which axis the angle is measured from.\n\nExample (iii)\nResolve a 10 N force acting at 30° above the horizontal.\nSolution\nFormulas: Fₓ = F cos θ; Fᵧ = F sin θ.\nFₓ = 10 cos 30° ≈ 8.66 N; Fᵧ = 10 sin 30° = 5.0 N.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nDo not add perpendicular vectors as ordinary numbers. Do not report displacement without direction. Distinguish speed from velocity and distance from displacement.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Is temperature a scalar or vector? Answer: Scalar.\n2. What two properties define a vector? Answer: Magnitude and direction.\n3. What is the resultant of 8 N east and 3 N east? Answer: 11 N east.\n4. A person walks 6 m north and 8 m east. What is the displacement magnitude? Answer: 10 m.\n5. Which component of F at angle θ above the horizontal is F cos θ? Answer: The horizontal component.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw arrows and label their directions before calculating. Later JAMB vector questions should be mapped here.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"2. Scalars, vectors and vector addition","body":"1. SCALARS AND VECTORS: WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE?\nA SCALAR tells us how much of a quantity there is. Examples: mass 5 kg, time 10 s, temperature 30 °C, distance 100 m, speed 20 m/s and energy 50 J.\nA VECTOR tells us both how much AND in which direction. Examples: displacement 5 m east, velocity 20 m/s north, acceleration 3 m/s² downward and force 10 N west.\nDirection is essential: two equal forces in opposite directions can cancel, while two equal forces in the same direction reinforce each other. This is why vectors cannot always be added like ordinary numbers.\nDISTANCE is total path length. DISPLACEMENT is the straight-line change from start to finish, with direction. SPEED is distance per time; VELOCITY is displacement per time. Distance and speed are scalars; displacement and velocity are vectors.\n\nExample (i)\nA pupil walks 4 m east and 4 m west. Find the distance and displacement.\nSolution:\nFormula: Distance = total path = 4 + 4 = 8 m.\nFormula: Displacement = final position − starting position.\nThe pupil returns to the start, so displacement = 0 m.\n\n2. HOW TO REPRESENT A VECTOR\nDraw a vector as an arrow. Its LENGTH shows magnitude according to a chosen scale, and its ARROWHEAD shows direction. If 1 cm represents 5 N, a 15 N force is represented by a 3 cm arrow.\nVectors can be equal even if drawn at different places, provided magnitude and direction are equal. A negative sign on a one-dimensional vector indicates direction relative to a chosen positive axis, not a negative physical length.\n\nExample (ii)\nUsing 1 cm to represent 4 N, how long should an arrow representing 12 N be?\nSolution:\nFormula: Arrow length = vector magnitude ÷ scale value per centimetre.\nLength = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 cm, with the arrow pointing in the specified direction.\n\n3. ADDING VECTORS: SAME, OPPOSITE AND DIFFERENT DIRECTIONS\nFor vectors pointing the SAME way, add magnitudes. For OPPOSITE directions, subtract magnitudes and use the direction of the larger vector. If equal and opposite, the resultant is zero.\nFor other directions, use HEAD-TO-TAIL addition: draw the first arrow; place the tail of the second at the head of the first; draw the resultant from the first tail to the final head. The resultant represents the single vector equivalent to both.\n\nExample (iii)\nTwo forces of 12 N east and 7 N west act on a box. Find the resultant.\nSolution:\nFormula: Resultant for opposite forces = larger − smaller.\nR = 12 − 7 = 5 N east.\n\nExample (iv)\nTwo forces of 8 N north and 5 N north act together. Find the resultant.\nSolution:\nFormula: Resultant for same-direction forces = sum of magnitudes.\nR = 8 + 5 = 13 N north.\n\n4. RIGHT-ANGLE VECTORS AND THE RESULTANT DIRECTION\nWhen two vectors are perpendicular, the resultant is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle. Formula: R = √(A² + B²). To state direction, use tan θ = opposite side ÷ adjacent side. Check which direction the angle is measured FROM.\nDo not use ordinary addition A + B for perpendicular vectors: 3 m east plus 4 m north gives a 5 m displacement, not 7 m. The 7 m is the distance walked along the two legs.\n\nExample (v)\nA student walks 3 m east, then 4 m north. Find total distance and displacement.\nSolution:\nFormula: Distance = 3 + 4 = 7 m.\nFormula: R = √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5 m.\nFormula: tan θ = 4/3, so θ ≈ 53.1° north of east.\nDisplacement = 5 m at 53.1° north of east.\n\nExample (vi)\nTwo forces of 6 N east and 8 N north act at right angles. Find the resultant magnitude.\nSolution:\nFormula: R = √(6² + 8²).\nR = √100 = 10 N.\n\n5. RESOLVING A VECTOR INTO COMPONENTS\nSometimes one diagonal vector is easier to work with as two perpendicular COMPONENTS. These are not additional forces: they are horizontal and vertical parts of the same vector.\nIf a vector F makes angle θ ABOVE THE HORIZONTAL, its horizontal component is ADJACENT to θ and its vertical component is OPPOSITE. Therefore Fₓ = F cos θ and Fᵧ = F sin θ.\nIf θ is measured from the vertical, sine and cosine exchange roles. If the vector points left or down, its relevant component is negative when right and up are chosen positive.\n\nExample (vii)\nResolve a 10 N force at 30° above the positive horizontal.\nSolution:\nFormulas: Fₓ = F cos θ; Fᵧ = F sin θ.\nFₓ = 10 cos 30° ≈ 8.66 N right.\nFᵧ = 10 sin 30° = 5.00 N up.\n\nExample (viii)\nA 20 N force makes 60° with the positive horizontal. Find its components.\nSolution:\nFormulas: Fₓ = F cos θ; Fᵧ = F sin θ.\nFₓ = 20 cos 60° = 10 N.\nFᵧ = 20 sin 60° ≈ 17.32 N.\n\n6. RESULTANT OF VECTORS AT ANY ANGLE\nFor two vectors A and B with included angle φ between their directions, the resultant magnitude is found from R² = A² + B² + 2AB cos φ. At φ = 0°, the magnitudes add; at φ = 180°, they subtract; at φ = 90°, the cosine term is zero and the Pythagoras formula appears.\nThis formula gives magnitude. A separate component calculation or trigonometry is needed if the question also asks for direction.\n\nExample (ix)\nTwo forces of 5 N and 5 N act at 60° to each other. Find the resultant magnitude.\nSolution:\nFormula: R² = A² + B² + 2AB cos φ.\nR² = 5² + 5² + 2 × 5 × 5 × cos 60° = 25 + 25 + 25 = 75.\nR = √75 ≈ 8.66 N.\n\n7. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nSpeed is not velocity; distance is not displacement. Never omit a vector's direction when it is required. Do not add perpendicular magnitudes directly. Check whether θ is measured from the horizontal or vertical. The sign of a component depends on your chosen axes.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Is temperature a scalar or a vector? Answer: Scalar.\n2. What two properties define a vector? Answer: Magnitude and direction.\n3. What is the resultant of 8 N east and 3 N east? Answer: 11 N east.\n4. A pupil walks 6 m north and 8 m east. What is the displacement magnitude? Answer: 10 m.\n5. For an angle θ measured from the horizontal, what is the horizontal component of F? Answer: F cos θ.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nBefore calculating, draw the arrows, mark their directions, identify the angle reference axis, and decide whether to add, subtract or use a right-angle triangle."},"old":{"title":"2. Scalars, vectors and vector addition","body":"New syllabus-first Physics lesson; historical year uploads pending."}},{"id":"physics-motion","title":"3. Motion, speed, velocity and acceleration","body":"1. DESCRIBING MOTION\nAn object is in motion when its position changes relative to a reference point over time. A passenger seated in a moving bus is at rest relative to the bus but moving relative to the roadside. Always identify the reference point.\n\n2. DISTANCE, DISPLACEMENT, SPEED AND VELOCITY\nDistance is total path length (scalar). Displacement is change in position with direction (vector). Average speed = total distance ÷ total time. Average velocity = displacement ÷ total time.\nA runner who completes one full lap and returns to the start has travelled a distance but has zero displacement. The runner's average velocity over the lap is zero, even though average speed is not.\n\nExample (i)\nA cyclist travels 120 m in 15 s. Find the average speed.\nSolution\nFormula: Average speed = total distance ÷ total time.\nSpeed = 120 ÷ 15 = 8 m/s.\n\n3. ACCELERATION\nAcceleration is the rate of change of velocity: a = (v − u)/t. Its unit is m/s². Positive acceleration increases velocity in the chosen positive direction; negative acceleration indicates acceleration in the opposite direction. Deceleration describes slowing down, which depends on the direction of velocity and acceleration.\n\nExample (ii)\nA vehicle speeds up uniformly from 5 m/s to 25 m/s in 4 s. Find its acceleration.\nSolution\nFormula: a = (v − u)/t.\na = (25 − 5)/4 = 5 m/s².\n\n4. EQUATIONS FOR UNIFORM ACCELERATION\nFor motion in a straight line with constant acceleration:\nv = u + at\ns = ut + ½at²\nv² = u² + 2as\ns = (u + v)t/2.\nHere u = initial velocity, v = final velocity, a = acceleration, t = time and s = displacement. These equations do not apply unchanged when acceleration varies.\n\nExample (iii)\nA car starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 2 m/s² for 6 s. Find its final velocity and displacement.\nSolution\nFormulas: v = u + at and s = ut + ½at².\nSince it starts from rest, u = 0.\nv = 0 + 2 × 6 = 12 m/s.\ns = 0 × 6 + ½ × 2 × 6² = 36 m.\n\n5. MOTION GRAPHS\nOn a displacement–time graph, gradient gives velocity. A horizontal line means zero velocity; a steeper straight line means greater speed magnitude.\nOn a velocity–time graph, gradient gives acceleration. The signed area between the graph and time axis gives displacement; add absolute areas for total distance. A horizontal velocity–time line means constant velocity, not rest unless the line lies at zero.\nOn a speed–time graph, the area under the curve gives distance travelled.\n\nExample (iv)\nVelocity increases uniformly from 0 to 10 m/s over 5 s. Find displacement.\nSolution\nFormula: Displacement = area under velocity–time graph.\nThe area is a triangle: ½ × base × height = ½ × 5 × 10 = 25 m.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nRead graph axes before calculating. Do not confuse gradient with area. Use displacement rather than total distance for average velocity. Write units on all answers.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is the SI unit of acceleration? Answer: m/s².\n2. What does the gradient of a displacement–time graph give? Answer: Velocity.\n3. What does the area under a velocity–time graph give? Answer: Displacement.\n4. A body starts from rest. What is u? Answer: 0 m/s.\n5. A runner returns to the starting point. What is the displacement? Answer: Zero.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nSketch and label graph axes, then identify whether the question asks for gradient or area before solving.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"3. Motion, speed, velocity and acceleration","body":"1. WHAT DOES IT MEAN TO BE IN MOTION?\nMotion means a change of position with time RELATIVE TO A REFERENCE POINT. A passenger seated in a moving bus is at rest relative to the seat but moving relative to a tree by the roadside. The reference point matters.\nWe describe motion using distance, displacement, speed, velocity and acceleration. They answer different questions: how far along the path, how far from the starting point, how quickly, in which direction, and how quickly velocity changes.\n\n2. DISTANCE AND DISPLACEMENT\nDISTANCE is the entire path travelled, regardless of direction. It is a scalar and cannot be negative. DISPLACEMENT is the straight-line change in position from start to finish, with direction. It is a vector; along a chosen line it may be positive, negative or zero.\nIf someone walks 100 m east and then 100 m west, distance = 200 m but displacement = 0 m. Displacement magnitude never exceeds total distance.\n\nExample (i)\nA student walks 70 m east, then 30 m west. Find distance and displacement.\nSolution:\nFormula: Distance = sum of path lengths = 70 + 30 = 100 m.\nFormula: Displacement = final position − initial position = 70 − 30 = 40 m east.\n\n3. SPEED AND VELOCITY\nSPEED is the rate at which distance is covered. Formula: Average speed = total distance ÷ total time. VELOCITY is the rate of change of displacement. Formula: Average velocity = total displacement ÷ total time.\nBoth have unit m/s, but only velocity includes direction. A runner returning to the starting point has zero average velocity over the complete trip, although average speed is greater than zero.\nINSTANTANEOUS speed or velocity describes motion at one moment; average values describe the whole interval. A speedometer indicates instantaneous speed, not average speed for the entire journey.\n\nExample (ii)\nA cyclist covers 120 m in 15 s. Find average speed.\nSolution:\nFormula: Average speed = total distance ÷ total time.\nAverage speed = 120 ÷ 15 = 8 m/s.\n\nExample (iii)\nA car moves 90 m east in 10 s, then 30 m west in 5 s. Find average speed and average velocity.\nSolution:\nFormula: Average speed = total distance ÷ total time.\nDistance = 90 + 30 = 120 m; total time = 10 + 5 = 15 s.\nAverage speed = 120/15 = 8 m/s.\nFormula: Average velocity = displacement ÷ total time.\nDisplacement = 90 − 30 = 60 m east.\nAverage velocity = 60/15 = 4 m/s east.\n\n4. ACCELERATION AND DECELERATION\nACCELERATION is the rate of change of VELOCITY, not just speed. Formula: a = (v − u)/t, where u is initial velocity, v final velocity and t elapsed time. Unit: m/s².\nA change in speed OR direction changes velocity, so an object turning at constant speed can still accelerate. For straight-line motion, choose a positive direction and keep it throughout the calculation.\nIf an object moves in the positive direction and slows down, acceleration is negative. A negative acceleration does not ALWAYS mean slowing down: an object moving in the negative direction can speed up while acceleration is negative.\n\nExample (iv)\nA car speeds up uniformly from 5 m/s to 25 m/s in 4 s. Find acceleration.\nSolution:\nFormula: a = (v − u)/t.\na = (25 − 5)/4 = 5 m/s².\n\nExample (v)\nA vehicle moving forward at 20 m/s stops uniformly in 5 s. Find acceleration.\nSolution:\nFormula: a = (v − u)/t.\na = (0 − 20)/5 = −4 m/s².\nThe negative sign means acceleration opposes the chosen forward direction.\n\n5. EQUATIONS OF UNIFORMLY ACCELERATED MOTION\nWhen acceleration is CONSTANT and motion is along a straight line, use:\nv = u + at\ns = ut + ½at²\nv² = u² + 2as\ns = (u + v)t/2.\nHere s is displacement, not necessarily total path distance. Choose the formula containing the quantities you know and the one unknown you need. The formulas do not apply unchanged when acceleration varies.\n“Starts from rest” means u = 0; “comes to rest” means v = 0. Check units before substitution.\n\nExample (vi)\nA car starts from rest and accelerates at 2 m/s² for 6 s. Find final velocity and displacement.\nSolution:\nFormula: v = u + at.\nSince u = 0, v = 0 + 2 × 6 = 12 m/s.\nFormula: s = ut + ½at².\ns = 0 × 6 + ½ × 2 × 6² = 36 m.\n\nExample (vii)\nA car moving at 10 m/s accelerates uniformly at 3 m/s² over 50 m. Find its final speed.\nSolution:\nFormula: v² = u² + 2as.\nv² = 10² + 2 × 3 × 50 = 400.\nv = 20 m/s in the original forward direction.\n\n6. HOW TO READ MOTION GRAPHS\nFirst look at the AXES. On a DISPLACEMENT–TIME graph, the gradient (rise ÷ run) gives velocity. A horizontal line means no change in position, hence zero velocity. A straight sloping line means constant velocity; a curve means changing velocity. The gradient of a tangent to a curved graph gives instantaneous velocity.\nOn a VELOCITY–TIME graph, the gradient gives acceleration. The SIGNED area between the line and the time axis gives displacement. An area above the axis counts positive; an area below counts negative. For total distance, add the magnitudes of the areas. A horizontal line above zero means constant positive velocity, NOT rest.\nOn a SPEED–TIME graph, the area under the graph gives total distance. The gradient gives the rate of change of speed, which is not always the full vector acceleration if direction changes.\n\nExample (viii)\nVelocity increases uniformly from 0 to 10 m/s in 5 s. Find acceleration and displacement.\nSolution:\nFormula: Acceleration = gradient = (10 − 0)/(5 − 0) = 2 m/s².\nFormula: Displacement = triangular area under velocity–time graph.\ns = ½ × base × height = ½ × 5 × 10 = 25 m.\n\nExample (ix)\nA velocity–time graph increases uniformly from 4 m/s to 12 m/s over 4 s. Find displacement.\nSolution:\nFormula: Displacement = trapezium area = ½(u + v)t.\ns = ½(4 + 12) × 4 = 32 m.\n\n7. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nDistance and displacement are different. Average speed and average velocity need different numerators. A horizontal line on a velocity–time graph means constant velocity, not necessarily zero velocity. Do not confuse gradient with area. A negative acceleration does not automatically mean slowing down. Uniform-acceleration formulas require constant acceleration.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is the SI unit of acceleration? Answer: m/s².\n2. What does the gradient of a displacement–time graph give? Answer: Velocity.\n3. What does the signed area under a velocity–time graph give? Answer: Displacement.\n4. A body starts from rest. What is its initial velocity? Answer: 0 m/s.\n5. A runner returns to the starting point. What is the displacement? Answer: Zero.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nFor every graph, label the axes, identify whether the question needs gradient or area, and include units. For equations of motion, list u, v, a, s and t before choosing a formula."},"old":{"title":"3. Motion, speed, velocity and acceleration","body":"New syllabus-first Physics lesson; historical year uploads pending."}},{"id":"physics-projectiles","title":"4. Motion under gravity and projectiles","body":"1. FREE FALL AND GRAVITY\nA body in free fall moves under gravity alone, ignoring air resistance. Near Earth's surface, acceleration due to gravity g is approximately 9.8 m/s² downward; some JAMB questions specify 10 m/s². Always use the value given in the question.\nA dropped stone begins with zero initial vertical velocity. A stone thrown upward has upward initial velocity but downward acceleration throughout its flight. At its highest point its vertical velocity is zero for an instant, but acceleration is still g downward.\n\n2. VERTICAL MOTION WITH A SIGN CONVENTION\nIf upward is positive, acceleration a = −g. Then v = u − gt and s = ut − ½gt². For downward-positive motion, acceleration is +g. Be consistent with the chosen direction.\n\nExample (i)\nA ball is thrown vertically upward at 20 m/s. Find its time to maximum height, taking g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nAt maximum height, v = 0.\nFormula: v = u − gt.\n0 = 20 − 10t, so t = 2 s.\n\nExample (ii)\nFind the maximum height reached by the same ball.\nSolution\nFormula: v² = u² − 2gh.\nAt the highest point, v = 0.\n0 = 20² − 2 × 10 × h; h = 20 m.\n\n3. PROJECTILE MOTION\nA projectile is an object launched into the air and moving under gravity, with air resistance neglected. Horizontal motion has constant velocity because horizontal acceleration is zero. Vertical motion has constant downward acceleration g. These two components occur simultaneously.\nFor initial speed u at angle θ above the horizontal:\nuₓ = u cos θ; uᵧ = u sin θ.\nHorizontal position x = (u cos θ)t.\nVertical position y = (u sin θ)t − ½gt².\nFor launch and landing at the same height:\nTime of flight T = 2u sin θ/g.\nMaximum height H = u²sin²θ/(2g).\nHorizontal range R = u²sin(2θ)/g.\nThese last three formulas assume level launch and landing, constant g and negligible air resistance.\n\nExample (iii)\nA projectile is launched at 20 m/s at 30° above the horizontal. Find the time of flight, taking g = 10 m/s² and equal launch/landing heights.\nSolution\nFormula: T = 2u sin θ/g.\nT = (2 × 20 × sin 30°)/10 = 2 s.\n\n4. HORIZONTAL LAUNCH\nIf an object leaves a horizontal platform, initial vertical velocity is zero. Its vertical drop is h = ½gt² and its horizontal distance is x = ut, where u is horizontal launch speed.\n\nExample (iv)\nA ball rolls horizontally off a platform 20 m high at 5 m/s. Find time to reach the ground and horizontal distance, taking g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: h = ½gt².\n20 = ½ × 10 × t², so t² = 4 and t = 2 s.\nFormula: x = ut = 5 × 2 = 10 m.\n\n5. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nAt the top of a flight, vertical velocity is zero but gravity is not. Do not use the equal-height range formula for a projectile landing on a different level. Distinguish horizontal from vertical components.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is the direction of gravitational acceleration near Earth's surface? Answer: Downward.\n2. What is the vertical velocity at maximum height? Answer: Zero momentarily.\n3. Is horizontal acceleration zero in ideal projectile motion? Answer: Yes.\n4. What value of g should be used when the question specifies 10 m/s²? Answer: 10 m/s².\n5. What is the horizontal component of launch speed u at angle θ? Answer: u cos θ.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw separate horizontal and vertical directions, write the sign convention and identify whether launch and landing heights are equal.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"4. Motion under gravity and projectiles","body":"1. WHAT GRAVITY DOES TO A MOVING OBJECT\nImagine throwing a ball straight up. The ball rises, slows down, stops rising for an instant, then falls. Gravity pulls it downward throughout the journey, even while the ball is travelling upward.\nFree fall means gravity is the only force acting on the object. In school calculations we usually ignore air resistance. Near Earth's surface, the acceleration due to gravity is g ≈ 9.8 m/s² downward. If a question specifies g = 10 m/s², use 10 m/s² instead.\nAcceleration is not the same as velocity: a ball can be moving upward while accelerating downward. At the highest point of a vertical throw, its velocity is zero for an instant, but its acceleration is still g downward.\nMass is measured in kilograms (kg); weight is the force of gravity, measured in newtons (N). Formula: Weight W = mg.\n\nExample (i)\nA 3 kg object is held near Earth's surface. Find its weight when g = 10 N/kg.\nSolution:\nFormula: W = mg.\nW = 3 × 10 = 30 N. Its mass remains 3 kg.\n\n2. VERTICAL MOTION: DROPPED, THROWN UP, OR THROWN DOWN\nFirst decide which direction is positive. If UP is positive, acceleration a = −g; if DOWN is positive, a = +g. Do not change signs halfway through a question.\nSymbols: u = initial velocity (m/s); v = final velocity (m/s); t = time (s); s = signed vertical displacement (m); a = acceleration (m/s²).\nFor constant acceleration, the equations from the previous motion lesson still work:\nv = u + at\ns = ut + ½at²\nv² = u² + 2as.\nWhen upward is positive, substitute a = −g:\nv = u − gt\ns = ut − ½gt²\nv² = u² − 2gs.\nFor a body DROPPED from rest, u = 0. For a body THROWN upward, u is not zero. At maximum height, v = 0, but only at that instant.\nFor a body thrown vertically upward and returning to its original height (ignoring air resistance), time up = time down, and total flight time = 2u/g. This symmetry does not apply when the final height differs from the starting height.\n\nExample (ii)\nA stone is dropped from a 45 m building. How long does it take to reach the ground? Take g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nChoose downward as positive. Since it is dropped, u = 0.\nFormula: s = ut + ½gt².\n45 = 0 + ½ × 10 × t² = 5t².\nt² = 9; therefore t = 3 s. Use the positive time.\n\nExample (iii)\nA ball is thrown vertically upward at 20 m/s. Find the time to reach its highest point when g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nChoose upward as positive. At the highest point, v = 0.\nFormula: v = u − gt.\n0 = 20 − 10t.\n10t = 20; therefore t = 2 s.\n\nExample (iv)\nFor the same ball, find its maximum height above the release point.\nSolution:\nFormula: v² = u² − 2gh, because upward is positive.\nAt the highest point, v = 0.\n0 = 20² − 2 × 10 × h.\n20h = 400; therefore h = 20 m.\n\nExample (v)\nThe ball in Examples (iii) and (iv) returns to the same release height. Find the total time in the air.\nSolution:\nFormula: Total flight time = 2u/g, only for return to the same height.\nT = 2 × 20/10 = 4 s. It takes 2 s to rise and another 2 s to fall.\n\n3. PROJECTILE MOTION\nA projectile is an object launched into the air and then moving under gravity alone, such as a kicked ball after it leaves the foot. Its path is curved because it has TWO motions happening at the same time.\nHORIZONTAL MOTION: Without air resistance, horizontal acceleration is zero. Horizontal velocity stays constant.\nVERTICAL MOTION: Gravity acts downward, so vertical velocity changes every second.\nBoth motions share the same time t. We can solve them separately and then combine the answers.\n\nSuppose a ball is launched with speed u at angle θ ABOVE THE HORIZONTAL. We split its initial velocity into:\nHorizontal component uₓ = u cos θ.\nVertical component uᵧ = u sin θ.\nThe angle is measured from the horizontal, which is why cosine gives the horizontal component. If the angle is measured from the vertical, the roles of sine and cosine change.\nFor a launch at angle θ, with upward positive and the starting point at x = 0, y = 0:\nHorizontal displacement x = (u cos θ)t.\nVertical displacement y = (u sin θ)t − ½gt².\nVertical velocity at time t: vᵧ = u sin θ − gt.\nAt the highest point vᵧ = 0, but horizontal velocity u cos θ generally remains nonzero. The ball does not come to a complete stop.\n\nExample (vi)\nA ball leaves the ground at 10 m/s, 60° above the horizontal. Find its initial horizontal and vertical velocities.\nSolution:\nFormulas: uₓ = u cos θ; uᵧ = u sin θ.\nuₓ = 10 cos 60° = 5 m/s.\nuᵧ = 10 sin 60° ≈ 8.66 m/s.\nThe horizontal and vertical components describe ONE initial velocity, not two separate launches.\n\n4. PROJECTILES THAT LAND AT THE SAME HEIGHT\nWhen a projectile lands at the SAME vertical level from which it was launched, and air resistance is ignored, we can use three shortcut formulas:\nTime of flight T = 2u sin θ/g.\nMaximum height H = u²sin²θ/(2g).\nHorizontal range R = u²sin(2θ)/g.\nHere T is total time in the air, H is the greatest height above launch level, and R is horizontal distance from launch to landing. All three formulas assume constant g, no air resistance and equal starting/landing heights.\nWhy does the time formula work? Initial vertical speed is u sin θ. The time to rise is (u sin θ)/g; the time to fall back to the same height is equal, so total time is twice that.\nWhy does the range formula work? Horizontal distance = horizontal velocity × total time. Thus R = (u cos θ) × (2u sin θ/g), which simplifies to u²sin(2θ)/g.\n\nExample (vii)\nA ball is projected at 20 m/s at 30° and lands at its original height. Find the time of flight using g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: T = 2u sin θ/g.\nT = (2 × 20 × sin 30°)/10.\nSince sin 30° = 0.5, T = 2 s.\n\nExample (viii)\nUsing the same ball, find its maximum height and horizontal range.\nSolution:\nFormula for height: H = u²sin²θ/(2g).\nH = 20² × (0.5)²/(2 × 10) = 400 × 0.25/20 = 5 m.\nFormula for range: R = u²sin(2θ)/g.\nR = 20² × sin 60°/10 ≈ 34.6 m.\nThe maximum height is 5 m, while the range is about 34.6 m. They measure different directions.\n\n5. HORIZONTAL PROJECTION FROM A HEIGHT\nA ball rolling off a table is not thrown upward: it leaves horizontally. Therefore its INITIAL VERTICAL VELOCITY is zero, but its horizontal velocity is not zero.\nChoose downward as positive for the vertical fall:\nVertical drop h = ½gt².\nHorizontal distance x = uₓt.\nFirst use the height to find time. Then use that SAME time to find the horizontal distance. Do not use the equal-height range formula R = u²sin(2θ)/g here: the ball lands BELOW its launch point.\n\nExample (ix)\nA ball rolls off a platform 20 m high with horizontal speed 5 m/s. Find its time to hit the ground and how far from the base it lands. Take g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula for vertical fall: h = ½gt².\n20 = ½ × 10 × t² = 5t².\nt² = 4; therefore t = 2 s.\nFormula for horizontal distance: x = uₓt.\nx = 5 × 2 = 10 m.\nThe ball takes 2 s to land and travels 10 m horizontally.\n\n6. CHOOSING THE RIGHT METHOD IN A JAMB QUESTION\nAsk these questions before calculating:\nWas the object DROPPED? Then its initial velocity is zero.\nWas it THROWN VERTICALLY? Choose a sign convention and use vertical-motion equations.\nWas it launched AT AN ANGLE? Resolve initial velocity into horizontal and vertical components.\nWas it launched HORIZONTALLY FROM A HEIGHT? Vertical initial velocity is zero; find falling time from the height.\nDoes it LAND AT THE SAME HEIGHT? Only then consider the three shortcut projectile formulas.\nIs the question asking for HEIGHT, TIME, RANGE or VELOCITY? Identify the quantity before selecting a formula.\nAlways draw a small sketch showing the launch point, direction, θ if present, and downward gravity. Write the formula before inserting values and include the correct units.\n\n7. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nAt maximum height, vertical velocity is zero but gravitational acceleration is not.\nA projectile at maximum height may still have horizontal velocity.\nHorizontal velocity remains constant only when horizontal forces such as air resistance are ignored.\nThe equal-height time, height and range shortcuts cannot be used unchanged for landing at a different height.\nDo not confuse maximum height (vertical distance) with range (horizontal distance).\nDo not substitute the full launch speed u for a horizontal or vertical component when the question requires u cos θ or u sin θ.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What direction does gravitational acceleration act near Earth's surface? Answer: Downward, even when the object moves upward.\n2. What is the vertical velocity at the highest point of a projectile? Answer: Zero momentarily; horizontal velocity may still be nonzero.\n3. Why do we use u cos θ for the horizontal component when θ is measured above the horizontal? Answer: The horizontal component is adjacent to θ.\n4. A ball rolls horizontally off a table. What is its initial vertical velocity? Answer: Zero.\n5. When may R = u²sin(2θ)/g be used? Answer: For ideal projectile motion with equal launch and landing heights, constant g and negligible air resistance.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nBefore checking an answer, sketch the motion, separate horizontal from vertical quantities, state the sign convention, and decide whether the launch and landing heights match."},"old":{"title":"4. Motion under gravity and projectiles","body":"New syllabus-first Physics lesson; historical year uploads pending."}},{"id":"physics-newtons-laws","title":"5. Newton's Laws of Motion and Momentum","body":"1. WHY OBJECTS CHANGE THEIR MOTION\nA force is a push or pull that can change an object's velocity or shape. Force is a vector: it has size and direction. Its SI unit is the newton (N). An object does not need a continuous forward force to keep moving at constant velocity on a frictionless straight path. A NET or RESULTANT force is needed to change velocity.\nAlways draw a FREE-BODY DIAGRAM: show the object and every external force acting on it. For a block on a horizontal table, weight mg acts down and the normal reaction N acts up. If there is no vertical acceleration, N = mg; this equality is not automatic on a slope or when other vertical forces act.\n\n2. NEWTON'S FIRST LAW — INERTIA\nA body remains at rest or moves with constant velocity in a straight line unless acted on by a nonzero resultant external force. This is the law of inertia. Inertia is resistance to a change of velocity; greater mass means greater inertia. A passenger lurches forward when a moving bus brakes because the passenger's body tends to continue moving.\nBalanced forces mean zero acceleration, NOT necessarily zero velocity. A car travelling steadily on a straight road can have balanced driving and resistive forces.\n\nExample (i)\nA box has a 20 N push east and a 20 N frictional force west. Find its resultant force and acceleration.\nSolution:\nFormula: Resultant force = 20 − 20 = 0 N.\nFormula: F = ma, so a = 0/m = 0 m/s² for nonzero mass.\nThe box could remain at rest OR move at constant velocity.\n\n3. NEWTON'S SECOND LAW — FORCE, MASS AND ACCELERATION\nThe net force equals the rate of change of momentum. For constant mass, F = ma. Acceleration has the direction of the resultant force, not necessarily the direction of motion. The larger the net force for a fixed mass, the larger the acceleration; the larger the mass for a fixed force, the smaller the acceleration.\nSymbols: F = resultant force in N; m = mass in kg; a = acceleration in m/s². Convert grams to kilograms before using the formula.\n\nExample (ii)\nA 5 kg trolley experiences a resultant horizontal force of 15 N. Find its acceleration.\nSolution:\nFormula: F = ma, therefore a = F/m.\na = 15/5 = 3 m/s² in the direction of the force.\n\nExample (iii)\nA 2 kg body accelerates at 4 m/s² to the right. What resultant force acts on it?\nSolution:\nFormula: F = ma.\nF = 2 × 4 = 8 N to the right.\n\nExample (iv)\nA 10 kg crate is pushed rightward with 50 N while friction acts leftward with 20 N. Find its acceleration.\nSolution:\nFormula: Net force = applied force − friction = 50 − 20 = 30 N.\nFormula: a = Fnet/m = 30/10 = 3 m/s² rightward.\n\n4. NEWTON'S THIRD LAW — ACTION AND REACTION\nWhen object A exerts a force on B, B simultaneously exerts an equal and opposite force on A. These two forces act on DIFFERENT objects, so they do not cancel when analysing just one object. A swimmer pushes water backward; water pushes the swimmer forward. The Earth pulls an apple down, and the apple pulls Earth up with an equal gravitational force, though Earth's acceleration is tiny because its mass is enormous.\nDo not confuse third-law force pairs with balanced forces acting on the SAME object.\n\nExample (v)\nA student pushes a wall with a force of 60 N eastward. State the reaction.\nSolution:\nNewton's third law: the wall pushes the student with 60 N westward.\nThe forces are equal and opposite, but act on different bodies.\n\n5. MOMENTUM AND IMPULSE\nLinear momentum is the quantity of motion of a body: p = mv. It is a vector and has unit kg m/s. Impulse is force multiplied by the time it acts: J = FΔt for constant force. It equals change in momentum: J = Δp = mv − mu for constant mass.\nIncreasing collision time can reduce average impact force for the same change in momentum. This is why airbags and padding help protect passengers.\n\nExample (vi)\nA 0.5 kg ball moves east at 12 m/s. Find its momentum.\nSolution:\nFormula: p = mv.\np = 0.5 × 12 = 6 kg m/s east.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 0.2 kg ball moving right at 10 m/s is brought to rest in 0.5 s. Find the average force on it.\nSolution:\nFormula: Faverage = Δp/Δt = m(v − u)/t.\nF = 0.2(0 − 10)/0.5 = −4 N.\nThe force is 4 N to the left, opposite the initial motion.\n\n6. CONSERVATION OF LINEAR MOMENTUM\nIn an isolated system with no net external impulse, total momentum before an interaction equals total momentum after. Internal forces between colliding objects cancel in the system total, but kinetic energy is not necessarily conserved.\nFor two objects moving on a straight line, choose right as positive: m₁u₁ + m₂u₂ = m₁v₁ + m₂v₂. Velocities to the left are negative.\nIf objects stick together after collision, they share one final velocity. Such a collision is perfectly inelastic: momentum is conserved but kinetic energy is generally lost.\n\nExample (viii)\nA 2 kg trolley moving right at 4 m/s collides with a stationary 2 kg trolley. They stick together. Find their common velocity.\nSolution:\nFormula: m₁u₁ + m₂u₂ = (m₁ + m₂)v.\n2 × 4 + 2 × 0 = (2 + 2)v.\n8 = 4v, so v = 2 m/s rightward.\n\nExample (ix)\nA 3 kg trolley moves right at 2 m/s and a 1 kg trolley moves left at 2 m/s. They stick. Find their final velocity.\nSolution:\nChoose right positive; left is negative.\nFormula: (m₁u₁ + m₂u₂)/(m₁ + m₂) = v.\nv = [3(2) + 1(−2)]/4 = 4/4 = 1 m/s rightward.\n\n7. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nUse RESULTANT force, not one selected force, in F = ma. Balanced forces can accompany constant nonzero velocity. Action–reaction forces act on different bodies. Momentum is a vector: include signs and direction. Momentum may be conserved even when kinetic energy is not.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What does zero resultant force tell us about acceleration? Answer: Acceleration is zero; velocity may be constant and nonzero.\n2. What is the SI unit of force? Answer: Newton (N).\n3. A 4 kg body experiences 12 N net force. What is its acceleration? Answer: 3 m/s².\n4. On which objects do action and reaction forces act? Answer: On two different interacting objects.\n5. What is conserved in an isolated collision? Answer: Total linear momentum.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw each force with its direction; find the resultant before using F = ma. In collision questions, choose a positive direction and write momentum before = momentum after.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"5. Newton's Laws of Motion and Momentum","body":"1. FORCES: WHY OBJECTS START, STOP OR CHANGE DIRECTION\nThink about a football lying on the field. It stays still until a player kicks it. The kick makes it move. Later, friction with the ground and air resistance slow it down. When another player kicks it sideways, its direction changes.\nA FORCE is a push or pull that can change an object's motion or shape. A force can start a stationary object moving, slow a moving object, change its direction, or deform it. We measure force in newtons (N).\nForce is a VECTOR quantity: it has both magnitude (how strong it is) and direction (where it acts). For example, 10 N east is different from 10 N west.\nSometimes several forces act at once. A person may push a box to the right while friction pulls against its motion to the left. The single force that has the same overall effect as all these forces is called the RESULTANT or NET FORCE.\nIf 30 N acts right and 10 N acts left, the resultant is 20 N right. If two equal opposite forces act, their resultant is zero. Zero resultant force does not necessarily mean the object is at rest: it can continue moving steadily in a straight line.\nTo understand how forces affect motion, Newton described three laws. Each answers a different question: what happens when forces balance, how an unbalanced force changes motion, and how interacting bodies exert forces on each other.\n\n2. NEWTON'S FIRST LAW: WHY MOTION DOES NOT CHANGE BY ITSELF\nImagine standing inside a bus. When the bus suddenly starts moving, you may lean backward. When it brakes sharply, you tend to lurch forward. Your body resists a sudden change in its motion. This resistance is called INERTIA.\nNewton's first law states that an object remains at rest, or continues moving at constant velocity in a straight line, unless acted on by a nonzero resultant external force.\nAn object at rest stays at rest if forces balance. An object already moving continues at the same speed and in the same direction if forces balance. A moving object does not need a continuous resultant forward force to keep moving at constant velocity; in real life, a car's engine often has to balance friction and air resistance.\nMass measures inertia: the more massive the body, the harder it is to change its velocity. A loaded trolley is generally harder to start or stop than an empty one.\nA FREE-BODY DIAGRAM helps you identify forces before calculating. Draw only the chosen object and arrows for forces acting ON it. For a box on a level floor, weight mg acts down, the floor's normal reaction N acts up, a push may act right, and friction may act left. If no other vertical forces act and vertical acceleration is zero, N = mg.\nA key distinction: BALANCED forces mean zero acceleration, not necessarily zero speed.\n\nExample (i)\nA box has a 20 N push east and a 20 N frictional force west. Find its resultant force and acceleration.\nSolution:\nFormula: Resultant force = 20 − 20 = 0 N.\nFormula: F = ma, so a = 0/m = 0 m/s² for nonzero mass.\nThe box could remain at rest OR move at constant velocity.\n\n3. NEWTON'S SECOND LAW: HOW MUCH MOTION CHANGES\nSuppose you push an empty shopping trolley and then a heavily loaded one with the same force. The empty trolley gains speed more quickly. Now imagine pushing the same trolley harder: it accelerates more.\nACCELERATION means the rate at which velocity changes. It may mean speeding up, slowing down, or changing direction. Newton's second law explains how the resultant force produces acceleration.\nFor a body of constant mass, the formula is Fnet = ma, where Fnet is the resultant force in newtons, m is mass in kilograms, and a is acceleration in metres per second squared (m/s²).\nRearrange it to find acceleration: a = Fnet/m. Thus, doubling the net force doubles acceleration when mass stays constant; doubling the mass halves acceleration when force stays constant.\nAlways combine forces with their directions BEFORE using F = ma. For example, a 50 N push to the right against 20 N friction to the left gives 30 N net force to the right, not 50 N. The acceleration points in the direction of the net force, even when the object is initially moving in another direction.\nThe general statement of the second law is that resultant force equals the rate of change of momentum. We will define momentum later in this lesson.\n\nExample (ii)\nA 5 kg trolley experiences a resultant horizontal force of 15 N. Find its acceleration.\nSolution:\nFormula: F = ma, therefore a = F/m.\na = 15/5 = 3 m/s² in the direction of the force.\n\nExample (iii)\nA 2 kg body accelerates at 4 m/s² to the right. What resultant force acts on it?\nSolution:\nFormula: F = ma.\nF = 2 × 4 = 8 N to the right.\n\nExample (iv)\nA 10 kg crate is pushed rightward with 50 N while friction acts leftward with 20 N. Find its acceleration.\nSolution:\nFormula: Net force = applied force − friction = 50 − 20 = 30 N.\nFormula: a = Fnet/m = 30/10 = 3 m/s² rightward.\n\n4. NEWTON'S THIRD LAW: FORCES ALWAYS INVOLVE TWO BODIES\nWhen you walk, your foot pushes the ground backward. At the same time, the ground pushes your foot forward. That forward force helps you walk. When a swimmer pushes water backward, the water pushes the swimmer forward.\nNewton's third law says: if object A exerts a force on object B, object B exerts an equal force in the opposite direction on object A. The two forces act at the same time and on DIFFERENT bodies.\nThese are called ACTION and REACTION forces. They are equal in magnitude, opposite in direction and of the same type, but they do not cancel each other when you draw the forces acting on only one body.\nFor example, the Earth pulls a falling apple downward. The apple pulls the Earth upward with an equal gravitational force. Their accelerations differ because the Earth has vastly more mass.\nDo not confuse a third-law pair with two balanced forces on one object. The weight of a book downward and the table's support force upward both act ON THE BOOK, so they are not a third-law pair. The book pushes down on the table, and the table pushes up on the book: THAT is a third-law pair.\n\nExample (v)\nA student pushes a wall with a force of 60 N eastward. State the reaction.\nSolution:\nNewton's third law: the wall pushes the student with 60 N westward.\nThe forces are equal and opposite, but act on different bodies.\n\n5. MOMENTUM AND IMPULSE: WHY STOPPING TIME MATTERS\nA slowly rolling football is easy to stop, but a fast-moving heavy trolley is much harder. Both mass and velocity determine how much MOMENTUM a moving body has.\nLinear momentum is mass multiplied by velocity: p = mv. Here p is momentum in kg m/s, m is mass in kg and v is velocity in m/s. Because velocity has direction, momentum also has direction. A stationary body has zero momentum.\nA force acting for some time changes momentum. The effect of the force over that time is called IMPULSE.\nFormula: Impulse J = Faverage × Δt = Δp. For a body of constant mass, Δp = m(v − u), where u is initial velocity and v is final velocity.\nFor the same change in momentum, a longer stopping time produces a smaller average force. A car's airbag increases the time over which a passenger is brought to rest; a padded landing surface helps in a similar way.\nWhen the question involves opposite directions, choose one direction as positive and treat the other as negative. The sign of the answer tells you the direction.\n\nExample (vi)\nA 0.5 kg ball moves east at 12 m/s. Find its momentum.\nSolution:\nFormula: p = mv.\np = 0.5 × 12 = 6 kg m/s east.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 0.2 kg ball moving right at 10 m/s is brought to rest in 0.5 s. Find the average force on it.\nSolution:\nFormula: Faverage = Δp/Δt = m(v − u)/t.\nF = 0.2(0 − 10)/0.5 = −4 N.\nThe force is 4 N to the left, opposite the initial motion.\n\n6. CONSERVATION OF MOMENTUM: WHAT HAPPENS IN A COLLISION\nImagine two trolleys moving on a smooth track. When they collide, each exerts a force on the other. These internal forces are equal and opposite, so they change the trolleys' individual momenta but not the total momentum of the pair if there is no net external impulse.\nThis is the PRINCIPLE OF CONSERVATION OF LINEAR MOMENTUM: in an isolated system, total momentum before an interaction equals total momentum afterward.\nFor two bodies moving along one straight line:\nFormula: m₁u₁ + m₂u₂ = m₁v₁ + m₂v₂.\nThe subscripts 1 and 2 identify the bodies; u means velocity before collision and v means velocity after collision.\nA useful method is to choose right as positive, write each body's initial momentum with its sign, and equate their total to the final momentum.\nIf the trolleys stick together after colliding, they move with one common final velocity v, so:\nFormula: m₁u₁ + m₂u₂ = (m₁ + m₂)v.\nMomentum can be conserved even when kinetic energy is not. When objects stick together, some kinetic energy is usually converted into heat, sound or deformation.\n\nExample (viii)\nA 2 kg trolley moving right at 4 m/s collides with a stationary 2 kg trolley. They stick together. Find their common velocity.\nSolution:\nFormula: m₁u₁ + m₂u₂ = (m₁ + m₂)v.\n2 × 4 + 2 × 0 = (2 + 2)v.\n8 = 4v, so v = 2 m/s rightward.\n\nExample (ix)\nA 3 kg trolley moves right at 2 m/s and a 1 kg trolley moves left at 2 m/s. They stick. Find their final velocity.\nSolution:\nChoose right positive; left is negative.\nFormula: (m₁u₁ + m₂u₂)/(m₁ + m₂) = v.\nv = [3(2) + 1(−2)]/4 = 4/4 = 1 m/s rightward.\n\n7. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nUse RESULTANT force, not one selected force, in F = ma. Balanced forces can accompany constant nonzero velocity. Action–reaction forces act on different bodies. Momentum is a vector: include signs and direction. Momentum may be conserved even when kinetic energy is not.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What does zero resultant force tell us about acceleration? Answer: Acceleration is zero; velocity may be constant and nonzero.\n2. What is the SI unit of force? Answer: Newton (N).\n3. A 4 kg body experiences 12 N net force. What is its acceleration? Answer: 3 m/s².\n4. On which objects do action and reaction forces act? Answer: On two different interacting objects.\n5. What is conserved in an isolated collision? Answer: Total linear momentum.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw each force with its direction; find the resultant before using F = ma. In collision questions, choose a positive direction and write momentum before = momentum after."},"old":{"title":"5. Newton's Laws of Motion and Momentum","body":"New curriculum-first Physics lesson; verified year-by-year past-question mapping pending."}},{"id":"physics-equilibrium-friction","title":"6. Equilibrium of Forces, Friction and Inclined Planes","body":"1. WHAT EQUILIBRIUM MEANS\nA body is in translational equilibrium when the vector sum of all forces on it is zero: ΣF = 0. It then has zero acceleration; it may be stationary or moving at constant velocity. Resolve forces into horizontal and vertical directions and check ΣFₓ = 0 and ΣFᵧ = 0 separately.\nA FREE-BODY DIAGRAM shows every external force acting on the selected body, not forces that the body exerts on something else.\n\n2. WEIGHT, NORMAL REACTION AND TENSION\nWeight W = mg always acts vertically downward near Earth's surface. Normal reaction N is the contact force perpendicular to a surface. Tension T is the pulling force transmitted by a taut string; in simple questions we assume a light inextensible string and ideal pulley, so the tension is the same along that string.\nOn a horizontal surface with no other vertical forces or acceleration, N = mg. If a body hangs at rest from a vertical string, T = mg. Do not assume these equalities in every arrangement.\n\nExample (i)\nA 6 kg lamp hangs motionless from a vertical string. Find the tension when g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: Vertical equilibrium means T − mg = 0.\nT = mg = 6 × 10 = 60 N.\n\nExample (ii)\nA 4 kg block rests on a horizontal table. Find its normal reaction if no other vertical force acts and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: N = mg for this particular horizontal equilibrium.\nN = 4 × 10 = 40 N upward.\n\n3. FRICTION: WHY SURFACES RESIST SLIDING\nFriction acts along contacting surfaces and opposes actual or impending RELATIVE sliding. Static friction prevents sliding and adjusts up to a maximum value: 0 ≤ fₛ ≤ μₛN. At the point of impending motion, limiting friction fₛ,max = μₛN. Once sliding occurs, a simple model gives kinetic friction fₖ = μₖN.\nHere μ is the coefficient of friction and has no unit. Do not use f = μN for EVERY stationary object: static friction may be smaller than its limiting value. Rougher contact usually increases friction; lubrication often reduces it.\n\nExample (iii)\nA 10 kg block rests on a horizontal rough surface. Its coefficient of limiting static friction is 0.4. Find the greatest horizontal force that can be balanced by static friction. Take g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: N = mg = 10 × 10 = 100 N.\nFormula: Limiting friction = μₛN = 0.4 × 100 = 40 N.\nA horizontal push of 20 N can be balanced by 20 N static friction; 40 N is the maximum.\n\nExample (iv)\nA 5 kg block slides horizontally with coefficient of kinetic friction 0.2. Find friction when g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: N = mg = 5 × 10 = 50 N.\nFormula: fₖ = μₖN = 0.2 × 50 = 10 N opposite sliding.\n\n4. INCLINED PLANES: RESOLVING WEIGHT\nOn a slope at angle θ to the horizontal, weight mg still points vertically down. Resolve it into two components relative to the plane:\nAlong the slope downward: mg sin θ.\nPerpendicular into the slope: mg cos θ.\nIf there is no acceleration perpendicular to the plane and no other perpendicular forces, N = mg cos θ. The steeper the slope, the greater the downslope component of weight and the smaller the normal reaction.\nUse a sketch showing the slope, weight vertically downward, reaction perpendicular to the plane, and friction along the plane. Friction opposes the direction the object slides or would slide.\n\nExample (v)\nA 2 kg block rests on a smooth 30° slope. Find the component of its weight down the slope and its normal reaction. Take g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: W = mg = 2 × 10 = 20 N.\nFormula: Downslope component = mg sin θ = 20 sin 30° = 10 N.\nFormula: N = mg cos θ = 20 cos 30° ≈ 17.32 N.\nBecause the slope is smooth, there is no friction.\n\nExample (vi)\nFind the acceleration of the same 2 kg block released on the smooth 30° slope.\nSolution:\nFormula: Fnet = mg sin θ and Fnet = ma.\nTherefore a = g sin θ = 10 × 0.5 = 5 m/s² down the slope.\n\n5. BALANCING FORCES AT ANGLES\nWhen several forces meet at a point, choose axes and resolve every force. For equilibrium, total rightward components equal total leftward components, and total upward components equal total downward components.\nFor three concurrent coplanar forces in equilibrium, a correctly drawn closed triangle of vectors represents zero resultant. Do not confuse the diagram of forces with the physical positions of the objects.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 50 N horizontal force acts right and a 20 N force acts left on a box. What additional horizontal force would keep it in equilibrium?\nSolution:\nFormula: ΣFₓ = 0.\n50 − 20 − F = 0, so F = 30 N leftward.\n\nExample (viii)\nTwo horizontal pulls act on a ring: 12 N east and 5 N north. Find the magnitude and direction of a third force needed for equilibrium.\nSolution:\nFormula: Resultant of first two = √(12² + 5²) = 13 N.\nThe balancing force, called the equilibrant, is equal and opposite to this resultant: 13 N toward southwest.\nDirection: tan θ = 5/12, so θ ≈ 22.6° south of west.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nEquilibrium means zero resultant force, not necessarily zero speed. Friction is not always μN: static friction adjusts until its limit. Weight is vertical; normal reaction is perpendicular to the surface. On a slope, mg sin θ is parallel to the plane and mg cos θ is perpendicular when θ is the plane's angle to horizontal.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is the resultant force on a body in translational equilibrium? Answer: Zero.\n2. In what direction does weight act? Answer: Vertically downward.\n3. Does static friction always equal μₛN? Answer: No; it can take any value up to μₛN.\n4. What is the downslope component of weight on a slope at angle θ? Answer: mg sin θ.\n5. What is the equilibrant of a 10 N force east? Answer: 10 N west.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw a free-body diagram before writing equations. Label weight, normal reaction, tension and friction correctly, and resolve forces along and perpendicular to any slope.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"6. Equilibrium of Forces, Friction and Inclined Planes","body":"1. EQUILIBRIUM: WHEN FORCES BALANCE\nImagine a book resting on a table. Gravity pulls the book downward, but the table pushes it upward. The book does not fall through the table because these forces balance.\nNow imagine a car moving along a straight road at a steady speed. Its engine drives it forward while resistive forces act backward. When the forward and backward forces balance, the car continues at constant velocity.\nBoth situations illustrate TRANSLATIONAL EQUILIBRIUM: the resultant force on a body is zero, so its acceleration is zero. The body may be at rest or moving at constant velocity in a straight line.\nFormula: ΣF = 0. The symbol Σ (sigma) means 'add all'. For problems with forces in different directions, add horizontal components separately from vertical components: ΣFₓ = 0 and ΣFᵧ = 0.\nBefore calculating, draw a FREE-BODY DIAGRAM. Represent the body simply and draw arrows for all external forces acting ON it. Label the directions and magnitudes when known. This prevents you from missing a force or counting a force on another object.\n\n2. THE FORCES THAT KEEP OBJECTS SUPPORTED\nThree forces appear repeatedly in equilibrium questions: WEIGHT, NORMAL REACTION and TENSION.\nWEIGHT is the gravitational force pulling an object toward Earth. It acts vertically downward. Formula: W = mg, where m is mass in kilograms and g is gravitational field strength (often 10 N/kg in JAMB questions when stated).\nNORMAL REACTION is the push exerted by a surface on an object touching it. It acts PERPENDICULAR to the surface. On a horizontal table it points vertically up; on a slope it points away from the sloping surface, not vertically upward.\nTENSION is the pulling force transmitted through a taut rope or string. It acts along the string and pulls the object toward the string. In simple questions with a light inextensible string and ideal pulley, tension can be treated as equal throughout the same string.\nFor a book resting on a horizontal table, with no other vertical forces, the upward reaction balances the downward weight: N = mg. For a lamp hanging motionless from a single vertical string, T = mg. These equalities depend on the situation and must not be assumed for every slope, lift or pulley.\n\nExample (i)\nA 6 kg lamp hangs motionless from a vertical string. Find the tension when g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: Vertical equilibrium means T − mg = 0.\nT = mg = 6 × 10 = 60 N.\n\nExample (ii)\nA 4 kg block rests on a horizontal table. Find its normal reaction if no other vertical force acts and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: N = mg for this particular horizontal equilibrium.\nN = 4 × 10 = 40 N upward.\n\n3. FRICTION: WHY A PUSH DOES NOT ALWAYS MAKE SOMETHING MOVE\nTry pushing a heavy cupboard gently across a floor. At first, it may not move. The floor produces a force that opposes the cupboard's tendency to slide. This force is called FRICTION.\nFriction acts along the contact surface and opposes relative sliding or the tendency to slide. It helps us walk without slipping and allows brakes to slow a vehicle, but it can also waste energy as heat and cause wear.\nSTATIC FRICTION acts while the surfaces are not sliding past each other. It adjusts to match a small applied force until it reaches its greatest possible value, called LIMITING FRICTION. That is why a cupboard can stay still under a gentle push but move when the push becomes strong enough.\nFormula: 0 ≤ fₛ ≤ μₛN; maximum static friction fₛ,max = μₛN.\nOnce the surfaces are sliding, KINETIC (SLIDING) FRICTION acts. In the usual simple model: fₖ = μₖN.\nHere μ is the coefficient of friction, a number without units; N is the normal reaction. The coefficient depends on the surfaces in contact. Lubrication can reduce friction, while rough or gripping surfaces can increase it.\nImportant: do not set static friction equal to μₛN automatically. That formula gives its MAXIMUM value, not the value for every stationary object.\n\nExample (iii)\nA 10 kg block rests on a horizontal rough surface. Its coefficient of limiting static friction is 0.4. Find the greatest horizontal force that can be balanced by static friction. Take g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: N = mg = 10 × 10 = 100 N.\nFormula: Limiting friction = μₛN = 0.4 × 100 = 40 N.\nA horizontal push of 20 N can be balanced by 20 N static friction; 40 N is the maximum.\n\nExample (iv)\nA 5 kg block slides horizontally with coefficient of kinetic friction 0.2. Find friction when g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: N = mg = 5 × 10 = 50 N.\nFormula: fₖ = μₖN = 0.2 × 50 = 10 N opposite sliding.\n\n4. INCLINED PLANES: WHY AN OBJECT SLIDES DOWN A SLOPE\nImagine placing a toy car on a ramp. Even without pushing it, the car may roll downward. Gravity pulls vertically downward, but part of that pull acts along the ramp and makes the car accelerate.\nA sloping surface is an INCLINED PLANE. To solve force questions on a slope, we split the object's weight mg into two components: one PARALLEL to the slope and one PERPENDICULAR to it. This process is called RESOLVING A FORCE.\nIf the slope makes angle θ with the horizontal:\n• Down the slope: mg sin θ. This component tends to make the body slide down.\n• Into the slope: mg cos θ. This component presses the body against the surface.\nThe normal reaction points outward, perpendicular to the slope. If there is no acceleration perpendicular to the surface and no other perpendicular forces, N = mg cos θ.\nOn a SMOOTH slope, friction is neglected. If the body is released without a supporting force, the downslope component produces acceleration. On a ROUGH slope, friction acts along the surface and opposes the actual or impending sliding.\nAlways draw the slope first, show weight VERTICALLY downward, then label its two components. A steeper slope gives a larger downslope component.\n\nExample (v)\nA 2 kg block is placed on a smooth 30° slope. Find the component of its weight down the slope and its normal reaction. Take g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: W = mg = 2 × 10 = 20 N.\nFormula: Downslope component = mg sin θ = 20 sin 30° = 10 N.\nFormula: N = mg cos θ = 20 cos 30° ≈ 17.32 N.\nBecause the slope is smooth, there is no friction.\n\nExample (vi)\nFind the acceleration of the same 2 kg block released on the smooth 30° slope.\nSolution:\nFormula: Fnet = mg sin θ and Fnet = ma.\nTherefore a = g sin θ = 10 × 0.5 = 5 m/s² down the slope.\n\n5. BALANCING FORCES THAT POINT IN DIFFERENT DIRECTIONS\nSuppose two students pull a ring, one toward the east and the other toward the north. These forces do not cancel directly because they point in different directions. We first find their combined effect, called the RESULTANT.\nTo keep the ring in equilibrium, a third force must be equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to that resultant. This balancing force is called the EQUILIBRANT.\nFor forces along a single line, choose one direction as positive and add the signed forces. For forces at angles, resolve them into horizontal (x) and vertical (y) components. In equilibrium, the total force in each direction is zero:\nFormula: ΣFₓ = 0 and ΣFᵧ = 0.\nFor perpendicular components of 12 N east and 5 N north, the resultant magnitude is √(12² + 5²) = 13 N. The equilibrant has magnitude 13 N but points in the opposite direction.\nFor three forces acting at one point in a plane, a correctly drawn head-to-tail triangle that closes represents zero resultant force. The triangle is a diagram of VECTORS, not necessarily the physical shape of the ropes.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 50 N horizontal force acts right and a 20 N force acts left on a box. What additional horizontal force would keep it in equilibrium?\nSolution:\nFormula: ΣFₓ = 0.\n50 − 20 − F = 0, so F = 30 N leftward.\n\nExample (viii)\nTwo horizontal pulls act on a ring: 12 N east and 5 N north. Find the magnitude and direction of a third force needed for equilibrium.\nSolution:\nFormula: Resultant of first two = √(12² + 5²) = 13 N.\nThe balancing force, called the equilibrant, is equal and opposite to this resultant: 13 N toward southwest.\nDirection: tan θ = 5/12, so θ ≈ 22.6° south of west.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nEquilibrium means zero resultant force, not necessarily zero speed. Friction is not always μN: static friction adjusts until its limit. Weight is vertical; normal reaction is perpendicular to the surface. On a slope, mg sin θ is parallel to the plane and mg cos θ is perpendicular when θ is the plane's angle to horizontal.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is the resultant force on a body in translational equilibrium? Answer: Zero.\n2. In what direction does weight act? Answer: Vertically downward.\n3. Does static friction always equal μₛN? Answer: No; it can take any value up to μₛN.\n4. What is the downslope component of weight on a slope at angle θ? Answer: mg sin θ.\n5. What is the equilibrant of a 10 N force east? Answer: 10 N west.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw a free-body diagram before writing equations. Label weight, normal reaction, tension and friction correctly, and resolve forces along and perpendicular to any slope."},"old":{"title":"6. Equilibrium of Forces, Friction and Inclined Planes","body":"New curriculum-first Physics lesson; verified year-by-year past-question mapping pending."}},{"id":"physics-work-energy-power","title":"7. Work, Energy, Power and Conservation of Energy","body":"1. WORK: WHEN A FORCE TRANSFERS ENERGY\nIn physics, work is done when a force causes displacement and has a component along that displacement. Formula: W = Fs cos θ for a constant force F acting at angle θ to displacement s. The SI unit is joule (J), where 1 J = 1 N m.\nIf force acts in the direction of motion, θ = 0° and W = Fs. If force is perpendicular to displacement, θ = 90° and W = 0. Friction generally does negative work because it opposes sliding. A person holding a heavy bag motionless becomes tired, but does zero mechanical work ON THE BAG because its displacement is zero.\n\nExample (i)\nA 20 N horizontal force moves a box 5 m in the same direction. Find the work done by the force.\nSolution:\nFormula: W = Fs cos θ.\nW = 20 × 5 × cos 0° = 100 J.\n\nExample (ii)\nA person carries a bag horizontally for 10 m while exerting a vertical upward force. Find the work done by that upward force on the bag.\nSolution:\nFormula: W = Fs cos θ.\nSince force is perpendicular to horizontal displacement, θ = 90°.\nW = Fs cos 90° = 0 J.\n\nExample (iii)\nA 10 N force acts at 60° above the horizontal and moves a box 4 m horizontally. Find its work.\nSolution:\nFormula: W = Fs cos θ.\nW = 10 × 4 × cos 60° = 20 J.\n\n2. ENERGY: CAPACITY TO DO WORK\nEnergy is the capacity to do work and is measured in joules. KINETIC ENERGY is energy of motion: KE = ½mv². The square on velocity means doubling speed makes kinetic energy four times as large, when mass stays constant.\nGRAVITATIONAL POTENTIAL ENERGY is energy due to height in a gravitational field: PE = mgh near Earth's surface, where h is height relative to a chosen reference level. An object can have both kinetic and potential energy at once.\n\nExample (iv)\nFind the kinetic energy of a 4 kg body moving at 3 m/s.\nSolution:\nFormula: KE = ½mv².\nKE = ½ × 4 × 3² = 18 J.\n\nExample (v)\nA 2 kg stone is lifted 5 m vertically. Find its gain in gravitational potential energy when g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: ΔPE = mgΔh.\nΔPE = 2 × 10 × 5 = 100 J.\n\n3. WORK–ENERGY PRINCIPLE\nThe NET work done on a body equals the change in its kinetic energy: Wnet = KEfinal − KEinitial. This explains why a positive net work increases speed and negative net work reduces speed.\nIf a force of friction removes mechanical energy, that energy is transformed into heat and other forms; total energy is not destroyed.\n\nExample (vi)\nA 2 kg body speeds up from 3 m/s to 5 m/s. Find the net work done.\nSolution:\nFormula: Wnet = ½m(v² − u²).\nWnet = ½ × 2 × (25 − 9) = 16 J.\n\n4. CONSERVATION OF MECHANICAL ENERGY\nWhen only conservative forces such as gravity do work and air resistance is neglected, the sum KE + PE remains constant. As a falling object loses gravitational potential energy, it gains kinetic energy. Total energy is conserved even when mechanical energy is converted into heat through friction.\nFor a body dropped from rest through height h with no air resistance, mgh = ½mv², so v = √(2gh). Mass cancels in this ideal model.\n\nExample (vii)\nA stone falls freely from rest through 20 m. Find its speed after falling, taking g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: mgh = ½mv², so v = √(2gh).\nv = √(2 × 10 × 20) = √400 = 20 m/s.\n\nExample (viii)\nA 1 kg object falls from 8 m to 3 m above the ground. Find its loss of gravitational potential energy when g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: Loss of PE = mg(hinitial − hfinal).\nLoss = 1 × 10 × (8 − 3) = 50 J.\n\n5. POWER AND EFFICIENCY\nPOWER is the rate of doing work or transferring energy: P = W/t or P = E/t. Its SI unit is watt (W), where 1 W = 1 J/s. For a constant force acting along the velocity, P = Fv.\nEFFICIENCY compares useful output energy or power with total input: efficiency = (useful output/input) × 100%. Because of energy losses, real machines have efficiencies less than 100%.\n\nExample (ix)\nA machine does 600 J of work in 20 s. Find its average power.\nSolution:\nFormula: P = W/t.\nP = 600/20 = 30 W.\n\nExample (x)\nA motor takes in 500 J and delivers 350 J as useful work. Find its efficiency.\nSolution:\nFormula: Efficiency = useful output/input × 100%.\nEfficiency = 350/500 × 100% = 70%.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nWork requires displacement and a force component along it. Do not confuse work (J) with power (W). Kinetic energy depends on the SQUARE of speed. State the height reference for potential energy. Conservation of mechanical energy requires no non-conservative work; conservation of total energy still holds.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is the SI unit of work? Answer: Joule (J).\n2. What work is done by a force perpendicular to displacement? Answer: Zero.\n3. What happens to kinetic energy if speed doubles and mass stays constant? Answer: It becomes four times as large.\n4. What is the SI unit of power? Answer: Watt (W).\n5. What is the efficiency of a machine delivering 80 J from 100 J input? Answer: 80%.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nIdentify the energy store before and after a process. Decide whether to use W = Fs cos θ, KE = ½mv², PE = mgh, P = W/t or the efficiency formula, and check units.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"7. Work, Energy, Power and Conservation of Energy","body":"1. UNDERSTANDING WORK IN PHYSICS\nImagine pushing a shopping trolley across a supermarket. Your push makes the trolley move, so you are doing work on it. Now imagine pushing a wall with all your strength. The wall does not move, so in physics you do no mechanical work on the wall, even though your muscles become tired.\nThis gives us the first idea: work is done ON AN OBJECT when a force acts on it and the object moves through a distance in the direction of that force, or partly in that direction.\nA force is a push or pull, measured in newtons (N). Displacement is the change in position, measured in metres (m). Work is energy transferred by a force and is measured in joules (J).\nWhen the force and displacement point in the same direction, work = force × displacement, or W = Fs. For example, a 10 N force moving a box 2 m does 20 J of work.\nBut forces do not always point in the direction of movement. When a force makes an angle θ with the displacement, only the component along the displacement does work. The general formula is W = Fs cos θ, where θ is the angle BETWEEN the force and the displacement. One joule equals one newton metre (1 J = 1 N m).\nThree cases help you understand the formula:\n• Force along movement (θ = 0°): positive work, W = Fs.\n• Force perpendicular to movement (θ = 90°): zero work, because cos 90° = 0.\n• Force opposite movement (θ = 180°): negative work, because cos 180° = −1.\nFor example, friction usually does negative work on a sliding box because it acts against the movement. The person carrying a bag horizontally does zero work on the bag BY THE VERTICAL SUPPORTING FORCE; that does not mean the person's body uses no energy.\n\nExample (i)\nA 20 N horizontal force moves a box 5 m in the same direction. Find the work done by the force.\nSolution:\nFormula: W = Fs cos θ.\nW = 20 × 5 × cos 0° = 100 J.\n\nExample (ii)\nA person carries a bag horizontally for 10 m while exerting a vertical upward force. Find the work done by that upward force on the bag.\nSolution:\nFormula: W = Fs cos θ.\nSince force is perpendicular to horizontal displacement, θ = 90°.\nW = Fs cos 90° = 0 J.\n\nExample (iii)\nA 10 N force acts at 60° above the horizontal and moves a box 4 m horizontally. Find its work.\nSolution:\nFormula: W = Fs cos θ.\nW = 10 × 4 × cos 60° = 20 J.\n\nBefore moving on, notice that work can be calculated for one force at a time. The NET work is the sum of the work done by all the forces. A forward push may do positive work while friction does negative work.\nExample (iv)\nA frictional force of 6 N opposes a sliding box over 3 m. Find work done by friction.\nSolution:\nFormula: W = Fs cos θ.\nThe angle between friction and displacement is 180°.\nW = 6 × 3 × cos 180° = −18 J. Friction does negative work.\nExample (v)\nA student pushes with 50 N at 37° above the horizontal, moving a box 4 m horizontally. Use cos 37° = 0.8. Find the work done by the push.\nSolution:\nFormula: W = Fs cos θ.\nW = 50 × 4 × 0.8 = 160 J. The upward component does no work along the horizontal displacement.\n\n2. UNDERSTANDING ENERGY: MOTION AND POSITION\nThink about a bicycle moving along a road and a stone resting on a high shelf. The bicycle can do work because it is moving. The stone can do work if it falls because of its position above the ground. Both have energy, but in different forms.\nEnergy is the capacity to do work, measured in joules (J). The energy of motion is called KINETIC ENERGY (KE). A heavier object at the same speed has more kinetic energy; a faster object of the same mass also has more kinetic energy.\nFormula: KE = ½mv², where m is mass in kilograms and v is speed in metres per second. Because speed is squared, doubling speed makes kinetic energy four times as large, not twice.\nEnergy stored because of position or arrangement is POTENTIAL ENERGY. A raised object has gravitational potential energy because gravity can pull it downward. Near Earth's surface, gravitational potential energy relative to a chosen reference height is PE = mgh, where g is gravitational field strength and h is height above that reference. A stretched spring also stores elastic potential energy.\nA moving object above the ground can have BOTH kinetic and gravitational potential energy at the same time. Always identify which energy the question asks about before selecting a formula.\n\nExample (vi)\nFind the kinetic energy of a 4 kg body moving at 3 m/s.\nSolution:\nFormula: KE = ½mv².\nKE = ½ × 4 × 3² = 18 J.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 2 kg stone is lifted 5 m vertically. Find its gain in gravitational potential energy when g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: ΔPE = mgΔh.\nΔPE = 2 × 10 × 5 = 100 J.\n\nAs an object moves or changes position, energy can move between these forms. Energy may be transferred between stores. A raised ball has gravitational potential energy because of its position; while falling, its potential energy decreases and its kinetic energy increases. A moving bicycle has kinetic energy; braking changes much of that mechanical energy into thermal energy in the brakes and surroundings.\nElastic potential energy is stored when a spring is stretched or compressed. For an ideal spring obeying Hooke's law, F = kx and elastic energy E = ½kx², where k is spring constant (N/m) and x is extension (m). This is useful for connecting force, extension and stored energy.\nExample (viii)\nAn ideal spring of stiffness 200 N/m is stretched by 0.10 m. Find the energy stored.\nSolution:\nFormula: E = ½kx².\nE = ½ × 200 × (0.10)² = 1 J.\n\n3. HOW WORK CHANGES AN OBJECT'S SPEED\nSuppose you push a stationary trolley and it begins moving. Your force transfers energy to the trolley. If the trolley speeds up, its kinetic energy increases. If friction slows it down, kinetic energy decreases.\nThe WORK–ENERGY PRINCIPLE connects these ideas: the NET work done on an object equals the CHANGE in its kinetic energy.\nFormula: Wnet = KEfinal − KEinitial = ½m(v² − u²).\nHere u is the initial speed and v is the final speed. A positive net work increases kinetic energy; a negative net work decreases it. This method is useful when you know work or distance but not the time taken.\nWhen friction removes mechanical energy, that energy is transferred to the surroundings, mainly as thermal energy. Energy is transformed, not destroyed.\n\nExample (ix)\nA 2 kg body speeds up from 3 m/s to 5 m/s. Find the net work done.\nSolution:\nFormula: Wnet = ½m(v² − u²).\nWnet = ½ × 2 × (25 − 9) = 16 J.\n\nIn a question involving several forces, first find the net work done by ALL of them. Only then equate that result to the change in kinetic energy.\nExample (x)\nA 2 kg trolley starts from rest. Net work of 36 J is done on it. Find its final speed.\nSolution:\nFormula: Wnet = ½m(v² − u²).\n36 = ½ × 2 × (v² − 0).\nv² = 36, so v = 6 m/s.\n\n4. WHY ENERGY IS CONSERVED\nImagine holding a ball high above the ground. Before you release it, the ball has gravitational potential energy because of its height. As it falls, its height decreases and it gains speed. Its potential energy decreases while its kinetic energy increases.\nIf we ignore air resistance, the energy lost from one form equals the energy gained by the other. This is CONSERVATION OF MECHANICAL ENERGY:\nFormula: KEinitial + PEinitial = KEfinal + PEfinal.\nFor a ball dropped from rest through vertical height h, mgh = ½mv². Cancelling m gives v = √(2gh). This result assumes that only gravity does work.\nIn real situations, air resistance and friction can transfer some mechanical energy into heat and sound. Mechanical energy then need not remain constant, but TOTAL energy is still conserved.\n\nExample (xi)\nA stone falls freely from rest through 20 m. Find its speed after falling, taking g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: mgh = ½mv², so v = √(2gh).\nv = √(2 × 10 × 20) = √400 = 20 m/s.\n\nExample (xii)\nA 1 kg object falls from 8 m to 3 m above the ground. Find its loss of gravitational potential energy when g = 10 m/s².\nSolution:\nFormula: Loss of PE = mg(hinitial − hfinal).\nLoss = 1 × 10 × (8 − 3) = 50 J.\n\nAn ideal spring can also store elastic potential energy. When a question says a surface is “smooth” or asks you to “neglect air resistance,” it often means you may treat mechanical energy as conserved.\nExample (xiii)\nA 2 kg ball is dropped from rest 5 m above the ground. Find its speed just before impact when g = 10 m/s² and air resistance is neglected.\nSolution:\nFormula: Initial PE = final KE, so mgh = ½mv².\n2 × 10 × 5 = ½ × 2 × v².\n100 = v², so v = 10 m/s.\n\n5. POWER: HOW FAST WORK IS DONE\nTwo people carry the same load up the same stairs. They do approximately the same useful work against gravity, but one finishes in 10 seconds while the other takes 20 seconds. The faster person develops greater average POWER because the same work was done in less time.\nPower is the RATE of doing work or transferring energy.\nFormula: P = W/t or P = E/t, where W or E is in joules and t is in seconds.\nIts unit is the watt (W): 1 W = 1 J/s. One kilowatt (kW) is 1,000 W. If a force acts along the direction of movement, instantaneous power can also be found using P = Fv.\nEFFICIENCY is a different idea. It tells us how much of the energy supplied becomes useful output, rather than being transferred to unwanted forms such as heat or sound.\nFormula: Efficiency = (useful output energy ÷ input energy) × 100%. The same ratio can be formed using useful output power and input power measured over the same process. Real machines are generally less than 100% efficient.\n\nExample (xiv)\nA machine does 600 J of work in 20 s. Find its average power.\nSolution:\nFormula: P = W/t.\nP = 600/20 = 30 W.\n\nExample (xv)\nA motor takes in 500 J and delivers 350 J as useful work. Find its efficiency.\nSolution:\nFormula: Efficiency = useful output/input × 100%.\nEfficiency = 350/500 × 100% = 70%.\n\nFor a force at an angle θ to the direction of velocity, the instantaneous power transferred by that force is P = Fv cos θ.\nExample (xvi)\nA motor lifts a 100 N load vertically by 6 m in 3 s. Find its useful average power.\nSolution:\nFormula: Work = force × displacement = 100 × 6 = 600 J.\nFormula: P = W/t = 600/3 = 200 W.\nExample (xvii)\nA machine receives 1,000 W input power and provides 750 W useful output. Find efficiency and wasted power.\nSolution:\nFormula: Efficiency = useful output power/input power × 100%.\nEfficiency = 750/1,000 × 100% = 75%.\nFormula: Wasted power = input − useful output = 1,000 − 750 = 250 W.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nWork requires displacement and a force component along it. Do not confuse work (J) with power (W). Kinetic energy depends on the SQUARE of speed. State the height reference for potential energy. Conservation of mechanical energy requires no non-conservative work; conservation of total energy still holds.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is the SI unit of work? Answer: Joule (J).\n2. What work is done by a force perpendicular to displacement? Answer: Zero.\n3. What happens to kinetic energy if speed doubles and mass stays constant? Answer: It becomes four times as large.\n4. What is the SI unit of power? Answer: Watt (W).\n5. What is the efficiency of a machine delivering 80 J from 100 J input? Answer: 80%.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nIdentify the energy store before and after a process. Decide whether to use W = Fs cos θ, KE = ½mv², PE = mgh, P = W/t or the efficiency formula, and check units."},"old":{"title":"7. Work, Energy, Power and Conservation of Energy","body":"New curriculum-first Physics lesson; verified year-by-year past-question mapping pending."}},{"id":"physics-simple-machines","title":"8. Simple Machines, Moments and Centre of Gravity","body":"1. WHY MACHINES ARE USEFUL\nA simple machine makes work easier by changing the magnitude or direction of an applied force. Examples include levers, pulleys, inclined planes, wheel and axle, screw jacks and wedges. A machine does NOT create energy. In a real machine, friction means the work put in exceeds the useful work obtained.\nEFFORT is the force applied to a machine; LOAD is the resisting force the machine moves. Both are measured in newtons.\n\n2. MECHANICAL ADVANTAGE, VELOCITY RATIO AND EFFICIENCY\nMechanical advantage MA = Load/Effort. It has no unit. Velocity ratio VR = distance moved by effort/distance moved by load, also unitless. Efficiency η = useful work output/work input × 100%.\nFor constant forces along their respective displacements, η = (Load × load distance)/(Effort × effort distance) × 100% = (MA/VR) × 100%.\nAn ideal frictionless machine has efficiency 100% and MA = VR. A real machine generally has MA < VR. High MA does not automatically mean high efficiency.\n\nExample (i)\nA machine lifts a 300 N load using an effort of 75 N. Find its mechanical advantage.\nSolution:\nFormula: MA = Load/Effort.\nMA = 300/75 = 4.\n\nExample (ii)\nThe effort moves 2 m while the load rises 0.4 m. Find the velocity ratio.\nSolution:\nFormula: VR = distance moved by effort/distance moved by load.\nVR = 2/0.4 = 5.\n\nExample (iii)\nUsing the previous results, find the machine's efficiency.\nSolution:\nFormula: η = MA/VR × 100%.\nη = 4/5 × 100% = 80%.\n\n3. MOMENT OF A FORCE AND TURNING EFFECT\nA force can cause rotation about a pivot or fulcrum. The MOMENT (torque) of a force about a pivot equals force multiplied by the PERPENDICULAR distance from the pivot to the force's line of action.\nFormula: Moment = Fd⊥. Unit: N m. Do not use the sloping length of a lever unless it is perpendicular to the force.\nFor rotational equilibrium about a pivot, total clockwise moments equal total anticlockwise moments. This is the principle of moments. Translational equilibrium also requires zero net force.\n\nExample (iv)\nA 10 N downward force acts 0.4 m from a pivot, perpendicular to the lever. Find its moment.\nSolution:\nFormula: Moment = Fd⊥.\nMoment = 10 × 0.4 = 4 N m.\n\nExample (v)\nA metre rule is supported at a pivot. A 20 N force acts 0.3 m to the left of the pivot. What downward force 0.5 m to the right balances its moment? Ignore the rule's weight.\nSolution:\nFormula: Anticlockwise moment = clockwise moment.\n20 × 0.3 = F × 0.5.\nF = 6/0.5 = 12 N.\n\n4. LEVERS: THREE CLASSES\nA lever consists of a rigid bar turning about a fulcrum.\nFIRST-CLASS lever: fulcrum between effort and load, such as a seesaw or scissors.\nSECOND-CLASS lever: load between fulcrum and effort, such as a wheelbarrow or nutcracker. This arrangement commonly provides mechanical advantage greater than one.\nTHIRD-CLASS lever: effort between fulcrum and load, such as the human forearm when lifting a weight. It usually trades force advantage for speed or range of movement.\nIn a lever question, locate FULCRUM, EFFORT and LOAD before deciding its class.\n\nExample (vi)\nA lever has effort arm 0.8 m and load arm 0.2 m. Assuming an ideal lever, find its mechanical advantage.\nSolution:\nFormula: Effort × effort arm = Load × load arm.\nTherefore Load/Effort = effort arm/load arm.\nMA = 0.8/0.2 = 4.\n\n5. PULLEYS AND INCLINED PLANES\nA fixed ideal pulley changes the direction of the effort; its velocity ratio is 1. A movable pulley can reduce effort. In an ideal pulley arrangement, VR equals the number of rope segments supporting the moving load, provided the rope and pulley configuration matches that count. Count the SUPPORTING strands, not every visible strand.\nFor an inclined plane, VR = length of slope/vertical height. A longer ramp reduces the required effort in the ideal case, but the effort must move a greater distance. Friction reduces efficiency.\n\nExample (vii)\nA ramp is 5 m long and raises a load by 1 m. Find its velocity ratio.\nSolution:\nFormula: VR = slope length/vertical height.\nVR = 5/1 = 5.\n\nExample (viii)\nA frictionless pulley system has four supporting rope segments and lifts a 200 N load. Find the ideal effort.\nSolution:\nFormula: Ideal MA = VR = 4.\nFormula: Effort = Load/MA = 200/4 = 50 N.\n\n6. CENTRE OF GRAVITY AND STABILITY\nThe centre of gravity is the point through which the resultant weight of a body acts. In a uniform gravitational field it coincides with the centre of mass. A uniform symmetrical ruler has its centre of gravity at its midpoint.\nAn object is more stable when it has a low centre of gravity and a wide base. It begins to topple when the vertical line through its centre of gravity falls outside its base of support. Stability depends on both height of centre of gravity and base width, not simply on mass.\n\nExample (ix)\nA uniform metre rule is balanced on a knife edge at its midpoint. Where is its centre of gravity?\nSolution:\nFor a uniform ruler in a uniform gravitational field, its weight acts at the geometric midpoint.\nCentre of gravity = 50 cm mark.\n\n7. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nMechanical advantage and velocity ratio are different quantities. MA and VR have no units. Real machines cannot exceed 100% efficiency. Moment uses PERPENDICULAR distance. A fixed pulley changes direction but does not ideally multiply force. Identify lever class from the order of effort, load and fulcrum.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is mechanical advantage? Answer: Load divided by effort.\n2. What is velocity ratio? Answer: Distance moved by effort divided by distance moved by load.\n3. What distance is used in calculating moment? Answer: Perpendicular distance from pivot to the force's line of action.\n4. Which part lies between effort and load in a first-class lever? Answer: Fulcrum.\n5. What helps make a standing object more stable? Answer: A lower centre of gravity and wider base of support.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nSketch the machine and label load, effort, pivot and distances. Calculate MA and VR separately before efficiency; use perpendicular arms for moments.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"8. Simple Machines, Moments and Centre of Gravity","body":"1. HOW A SIMPLE MACHINE MAKES A TASK EASIER\nImagine trying to lift a heavy drum straight onto the back of a truck. You might struggle to raise it vertically. Now imagine rolling the same drum up a long ramp. You use a smaller force, but you push it over a longer distance.\nThat is what a SIMPLE MACHINE does: it helps us apply a force more conveniently, perhaps by reducing the effort needed, changing the direction of a force, or changing how far and how quickly something moves.\nEveryday examples include a ramp, wheelbarrow, bottle opener, pulley, scissors, screw jack and wheel-and-axle arrangement. These devices do not create energy. They change how force and movement are used.\nBefore studying formulas, learn two important words. The LOAD is the resisting force the machine must overcome, such as the weight of an object being lifted. The EFFORT is the force we apply to operate the machine. Both are forces measured in newtons (N).\nA machine may make the effort smaller, but it usually requires that effort to move a greater distance. Real machines also lose some input energy through friction.\n\nFor example, a fixed pulley allows you to pull downward to lift an object upward. It changes the direction of effort without ideally multiplying the force. The useful work a machine produces cannot exceed the energy supplied to it.\nExample (i)\nA machine raises a 200 N load through 1 m while the effort of 50 N moves 5 m. Find input and useful output work.\nSolution:\nFormula: Work = force × distance along the force.\nInput work = 50 × 5 = 250 J.\nUseful output work = 200 × 1 = 200 J. The difference, 50 J, is lost to other forms such as heat.\n\n2. THREE WAYS TO DESCRIBE A MACHINE'S PERFORMANCE\nConsider a machine that lifts a 300 N load when you pull with only 75 N. It multiplies the force you apply. We measure this force advantage using MECHANICAL ADVANTAGE (MA).\nFormula: MA = Load ÷ Effort. In this example, MA = 300/75 = 4. It means the load force is four times the effort force.\nBut there is another question: how far must you pull? If you pull 2 m of rope to raise the load by 0.4 m, your effort travels five times the load distance. We call this VELOCITY RATIO (VR).\nFormula: VR = distance moved by effort ÷ distance moved by load.\nFinally, some energy supplied to a real machine is lost to friction and other unwanted transfers. EFFICIENCY tells us what proportion becomes useful output work.\nFormula: Efficiency = useful output work ÷ input work × 100%.\nFor a machine with forces acting along their respective displacements, efficiency = MA/VR × 100%. An ideal machine without losses has MA = VR and 100% efficiency; a real machine has efficiency below 100%.\nRemember the distinction: MA compares FORCES, VR compares DISTANCES, and efficiency compares WORK or ENERGY. All three ratios are unitless, though efficiency is commonly stated as a percentage.\n\nExample (ii)\nA machine lifts a 300 N load using an effort of 75 N. Find its mechanical advantage.\nSolution:\nFormula: MA = Load/Effort.\nMA = 300/75 = 4.\n\nExample (iii)\nThe effort moves 2 m while the load rises 0.4 m. Find the velocity ratio.\nSolution:\nFormula: VR = distance moved by effort/distance moved by load.\nVR = 2/0.4 = 5.\n\nExample (iv)\nUsing the previous results, find the machine's efficiency.\nSolution:\nFormula: η = MA/VR × 100%.\nη = 4/5 × 100% = 80%.\n\nTo turn MA/VR into a percentage, multiply the ratio by 100. Do not confuse a high mechanical advantage with high efficiency: a machine can multiply force greatly yet lose considerable energy.\nExample (v)\nA machine has MA = 3 and VR = 5. Find efficiency.\nSolution:\nFormula: Efficiency = MA/VR × 100%.\nEfficiency = 3/5 × 100% = 60%.\nExample (vi)\nA machine has VR = 4 and efficiency = 75%. Find its mechanical advantage.\nSolution:\nFormula: Efficiency = MA/VR × 100%.\n0.75 = MA/4, so MA = 0.75 × 4 = 3.\n\n3. WHY A LONG HANDLE MAKES TURNING EASIER\nThink about opening a door. Pushing near the hinges is difficult, but pushing near the handle is easier. You can use the same force in both places; the difference is how far the force acts from the turning point.\nThe turning point is the PIVOT or FULCRUM. The turning effect of a force is its MOMENT, also called torque.\nFormula: Moment = force × PERPENDICULAR distance from the pivot to the force's line of action.\nThe unit is newton metre (N m). The perpendicular distance is important: it is not always the length of the bar or handle. If the force's line of action passes through the pivot, the perpendicular distance is zero, so it produces no moment about that pivot.\nA force may turn an object clockwise or anticlockwise. When a body is balanced against rotation, the total clockwise moment equals the total anticlockwise moment. This is the PRINCIPLE OF MOMENTS. For complete equilibrium, the resultant force must also be zero.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 10 N downward force acts 0.4 m from a pivot, perpendicular to the lever. Find its moment.\nSolution:\nFormula: Moment = Fd⊥.\nMoment = 10 × 0.4 = 4 N m.\n\nExample (viii)\nA metre rule is supported at a pivot. A 20 N force acts 0.3 m to the left of the pivot. What downward force 0.5 m to the right balances its moment? Ignore the rule's weight.\nSolution:\nFormula: Anticlockwise moment = clockwise moment.\n20 × 0.3 = F × 0.5.\nF = 6/0.5 = 12 N.\n\nWhen working out moments, first mark the pivot and decide which force tends to turn the object clockwise and which tends to turn it anticlockwise.\nExample (ix)\nA 15 N force acts perpendicularly 0.20 m from a pivot. Find its moment.\nSolution:\nFormula: Moment = force × perpendicular distance.\nMoment = 15 × 0.20 = 3 N m.\nExample (x)\nA 30 N downward force acts 0.4 m left of a pivot. What downward force 0.6 m right of the pivot balances it? Ignore the beam's weight.\nSolution:\nFormula: Clockwise moment = anticlockwise moment.\nF × 0.6 = 30 × 0.4.\nF = 12/0.6 = 20 N.\n\n4. LEVERS: HOW THE POSITION OF THREE PARTS CHANGES THEIR ACTION\nA LEVER is a rigid bar that turns about a pivot called the fulcrum. Every lever has a FULCRUM, a LOAD and an EFFORT. The arrangement of these three parts determines the lever's class.\nFIRST-CLASS LEVER: the FULCRUM is between the effort and the load, as in a seesaw. It may multiply force or increase movement depending on the arm lengths.\nSECOND-CLASS LEVER: the LOAD is between fulcrum and effort, as in a wheelbarrow. The effort arm is usually longer than the load arm, making it easier to lift a heavy load.\nTHIRD-CLASS LEVER: the EFFORT is between fulcrum and load, as in the human forearm when the biceps lifts a weight. It generally needs more effort force but allows the load to move farther or faster.\nTo classify any lever, locate the pivot first, then the load, then where the effort acts. Ask which one lies in the middle. Do not memorise examples without understanding their arrangements.\n\nExample (xi)\nA lever has effort arm 0.8 m and load arm 0.2 m. Assuming an ideal lever, find its mechanical advantage.\nSolution:\nFormula: Effort × effort arm = Load × load arm.\nTherefore Load/Effort = effort arm/load arm.\nMA = 0.8/0.2 = 4.\n\nThe force advantage of a lever depends on the lengths of its effort and load arms. In an ideal balanced lever, effort × effort arm = load × load arm.\nExample (xii)\nA lever has an effort arm of 1.2 m and load arm of 0.3 m. For an ideal lever, find the effort needed to lift 120 N.\nSolution:\nFormula: Effort × effort arm = load × load arm.\nE × 1.2 = 120 × 0.3.\nE = 36/1.2 = 30 N.\n\n5. PULLEYS AND INCLINED PLANES: SMALLER EFFORT, LONGER DISTANCE\nA PULLEY is a wheel with a rope running around it. A FIXED pulley is attached to a support and mainly changes the direction of pulling. An ideal fixed pulley has VR = 1. A MOVABLE pulley rises with the load. In a common arrangement, two rope sections support the load, so an ideal effort of half the load can lift it.\nTo calculate an ideal pulley's velocity ratio, count the rope segments supporting the moving load. Do not simply count every visible rope segment; the arrangement matters.\nAn INCLINED PLANE is a sloping surface such as a ramp. Instead of lifting a heavy load straight up, you move it over a longer sloping distance using a smaller force.\nFormula: VR of inclined plane = length of slope ÷ vertical height. A longer slope generally gives a larger ideal VR. Friction makes the actual required effort larger than in the ideal case.\n\nExample (xiii)\nA ramp is 5 m long and raises a load by 1 m. Find its velocity ratio.\nSolution:\nFormula: VR = slope length/vertical height.\nVR = 5/1 = 5.\n\nExample (xiv)\nA frictionless pulley system has four supporting rope segments and lifts a 200 N load. Find the ideal effort.\nSolution:\nFormula: Ideal MA = VR = 4.\nFormula: Effort = Load/MA = 200/4 = 50 N.\n\nFor example, when an ideal movable pulley has two supporting rope sections, pulling 2 m of rope lifts the load by 1 m. This shows why a smaller effort is balanced by a greater distance moved.\nExample (xv)\nA frictionless movable pulley is supported by two rope segments and lifts a 160 N load. Find effort and the rope pulled to lift it by 0.5 m.\nSolution:\nFormula: Ideal MA = VR = number of supporting segments = 2.\nEffort = 160/2 = 80 N.\nFormula: Effort distance = VR × load distance = 2 × 0.5 = 1.0 m.\nExample (xvi)\nA ramp is 6 m long and rises 1.5 m. Find its ideal velocity ratio.\nSolution:\nFormula: VR = slope length/vertical rise.\nVR = 6/1.5 = 4.\n\n6. CENTRE OF GRAVITY: WHY SOME OBJECTS TOPPLE EASILY\nImagine balancing a uniform metre rule on one finger. There is one point near its middle where it can balance without turning. We call the point through which the object's total weight effectively acts its CENTRE OF GRAVITY. In a nearly uniform gravitational field, it coincides with the centre of mass.\nNow think about two stools: one tall and narrow, the other low and broad. The low, broad stool is harder to topple because its centre of gravity is lower and its base of support is wider.\nA body remains stable while the vertical line through its centre of gravity falls inside its base of support. When that line moves outside the base, gravity can cause the body to tip.\nStability depends on the height of the centre of gravity and the width of the base, not simply on how heavy an object is.\n\nExample (xvii)\nA uniform metre rule is balanced on a knife edge at its midpoint. Where is its centre of gravity?\nSolution:\nFor a uniform ruler in a uniform gravitational field, its weight acts at the geometric midpoint.\nCentre of gravity = 50 cm mark.\n\nFor example, putting heavy luggage on the roof of a bus raises its centre of gravity and can make it less stable than placing the same luggage low inside the vehicle.\nExample (xviii)\nTwo identical bottles stand upright. One is placed on a broad base and the other on a narrow base. Which is generally more stable, all else equal?\nSolution:\nPrinciple: A wider base of support makes tipping less likely before the vertical line through the centre of gravity passes outside the base.\nThe bottle on the broad base is generally more stable.\n\n7. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nMechanical advantage and velocity ratio are different quantities. MA and VR have no units. Real machines cannot exceed 100% efficiency. Moment uses PERPENDICULAR distance. A fixed pulley changes direction but does not ideally multiply force. Identify lever class from the order of effort, load and fulcrum.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is mechanical advantage? Answer: Load divided by effort.\n2. What is velocity ratio? Answer: Distance moved by effort divided by distance moved by load.\n3. What distance is used in calculating moment? Answer: Perpendicular distance from pivot to the force's line of action.\n4. Which part lies between effort and load in a first-class lever? Answer: Fulcrum.\n5. What helps make a standing object more stable? Answer: A lower centre of gravity and wider base of support.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nSketch the machine and label load, effort, pivot and distances. Calculate MA and VR separately before efficiency; use perpendicular arms for moments."},"old":{"title":"8. Simple Machines, Moments and Centre of Gravity","body":"New curriculum-first Physics lesson; verified year-by-year past-question mapping pending."}},{"id":"physics-elasticity-hooke-s-law-and-elastic-potential-energy","title":"9. Elasticity, Hooke's Law and Elastic Potential Energy","body":"1. ELASTICITY: WHAT HAPPENS WHEN A FORCE CHANGES SHAPE?\nPull a rubber band gently, then release it. It stretches while you pull and returns towards its original length afterwards. Press a spring and release it: it also returns. These are familiar illustrations of elasticity.\nDEFINITION: Elasticity is the ability of a material to regain its original shape and size after the deforming force is removed, provided the elastic limit has not been exceeded.\nA deforming force is a force that changes a body's shape or size. It may stretch, compress, bend or twist a body. Elastic does not mean 'soft': a steel wire can be elastic even though it is difficult to stretch. If a force is too large, a material may remain permanently stretched; that is plastic deformation.\n\n2. EXTENSION, HOOKE'S LAW AND SPRING CONSTANT\nImagine hanging a spring beside a ruler. Its length without a load is its ORIGINAL LENGTH. When a load is added, it has a NEW LENGTH. The increase in length is called EXTENSION:\nextension e = stretched length − original length.\nDo not confuse extension with the total stretched length. A spring 10 cm long that becomes 14 cm long has extended by 4 cm, not 14 cm.\nDEFINITION: Hooke's law states that the extension of an elastic spring is directly proportional to the applied force, provided the limit of proportionality is not exceeded.\nF ∝ e, so F = ke.\nHere F = applied force (N), e = extension (m), and k = spring constant (N/m). The spring constant measures stiffness: a larger k means more force is required for the same extension. If a spring extends 2 cm under 4 N, it should extend 4 cm under 8 N only while Hooke's law holds.\nHOW TO INVESTIGATE IT: Clamp a spring vertically with a ruler beside it. Record its original length. Add known masses one at a time, wait for the spring to settle, record its new length, and subtract the original length to find each extension. Convert each mass to force using F = mg. Repeat readings and avoid overloading. Plot force F on the vertical axis and extension e on the horizontal axis.\nThe straight portion of a force–extension graph passes through the origin; its gradient is k. If the axes are reversed, the gradient is 1/k. At the limit of proportionality the graph stops being straight. The elastic limit concerns whether the material can still return to its original dimensions; the two limits need not be identical.\n\nExample (i)\nA spring has original length 12 cm and stretched length 17 cm. Find its extension.\nSolution\nFormula: e = final length − original length.\ne = 17 − 12 = 5 cm = 0.05 m.\n\nExample (ii)\nA force of 8 N produces an extension of 4 cm. Calculate the spring constant.\nSolution\nFormula: k = F/e.\nConvert 4 cm = 0.04 m.\nk = 8/0.04 = 200 N/m.\n\nExample (iii)\nA spring of constant 150 N/m carries a load of mass 0.30 kg. Calculate its extension. Take g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: F = mg; e = F/k.\nF = 0.30 × 10 = 3 N.\ne = 3/150 = 0.02 m = 2 cm.\n\nExample (iv)\nOn a force–extension graph, force increases from 2 N to 6 N while extension increases from 0.01 m to 0.03 m. Find the gradient.\nSolution\nFormula: gradient = change in force/change in extension.\nk = (6 − 2)/(0.03 − 0.01) = 4/0.02 = 200 N/m.\n\n3. ELASTIC POTENTIAL ENERGY AND WORK DONE\nWhen you stretch a spring, you do work against its restoring force. If it returns elastically, energy is stored in the spring and can be released when you let go. This stored energy is called ELASTIC POTENTIAL ENERGY.\nFor a spring obeying Hooke's law, force increases steadily from zero to its final value. The average force during gradual stretching is therefore half the final force. Work done equals the area under the force–extension graph:\nE = ½Fe = ½ke².\nE is measured in joules (J). The triangular area formula applies to the straight-line Hooke's-law region. If the graph is curved, find the actual area under the curve instead.\n\nExample (v)\nA spring of constant 100 N/m is extended by 0.20 m. Find the stored energy.\nSolution\nFormula: E = ½ke².\nE = ½ × 100 × (0.20)² = 2 J.\n\nExample (vi)\nA spring is gradually stretched through 0.10 m until the force reaches 20 N. Find the work done.\nSolution\nFormula: E = ½Fe.\nE = ½ × 20 × 0.10 = 1 J.\nThe factor ½ is essential because the force rises gradually from zero.\n\nExample (vii)\nIf the extension of a Hooke's-law spring doubles, what happens to its elastic energy?\nSolution\nFormula: E = ½ke².\nWith the same k, E is proportional to e². Doubling e gives 2² = 4 times the energy.\n\n4. STRESS, STRAIN AND YOUNG'S MODULUS\nA long thin wire stretches more easily than a short thick wire made of the same material. To compare materials fairly, we must account for the wire's dimensions.\nDEFINITION: Tensile stress is the stretching force divided by the wire's cross-sectional area.\nStress = F/A, unit pascal (Pa) or N/m².\nDEFINITION: Tensile strain is the extension divided by the original length.\nStrain = e/L; strain has no unit because it is a ratio of lengths.\nDEFINITION: Young's modulus is tensile stress divided by tensile strain in the linear elastic region.\nY = stress/strain = (F/A)/(e/L) = FL/(Ae).\nIts unit is Pa. A high Young's modulus means the material is stiff and resists stretching. Stiffness is not the same as breaking strength: a material may resist extension yet still fail under a sufficiently large force.\nThink of stress as how concentrated the pull is, strain as the fraction by which the wire lengthens, and Young's modulus as how much stress is needed to produce a given strain.\n\nExample (viii)\nA wire 2 m long extends by 1 mm. Find its tensile strain.\nSolution\nFormula: strain = e/L.\nConvert 1 mm = 0.001 m.\nStrain = 0.001/2 = 0.0005.\n\nExample (ix)\nA wire of cross-sectional area 2 × 10⁻⁶ m² is pulled by 40 N. Find its tensile stress.\nSolution\nFormula: stress = F/A.\nStress = 40/(2 × 10⁻⁶) = 2 × 10⁷ Pa.\n\nExample (x)\nA wire 2 m long with area 1 × 10⁻⁶ m² extends 1 mm under a force of 50 N. Find Young's modulus.\nSolution\nFormula: Y = FL/(Ae).\nConvert 1 mm = 0.001 m.\nY = (50 × 2)/[(1 × 10⁻⁶) × 0.001] = 1 × 10¹¹ Pa.\n\n5. ELASTIC AND PLASTIC BEHAVIOUR IN PRACTICE\nAn elastic deformation disappears after the load is removed; a plastic deformation remains. The limit of proportionality marks the end of a straight force–extension relationship. The elastic limit marks the greatest deformation from which the material can still recover fully. Stretching beyond the elastic limit may permanently lengthen a spring; a still greater force may cause breaking.\nExamples of elastic applications include spring balances, vehicle suspension and vibration absorbers. Springs should not be overloaded. In a laboratory graph, label both axes and units, mark the straight-line region, and do not assume that a curved region obeys F = ke.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nExtension is not stretched length. Convert cm and mm to m before calculating k, E or Y. A spring constant has units N/m, while Young's modulus has units Pa. Strain has no unit. A force–extension gradient is k only when force is on the vertical axis. Hooke's law applies within the proportional region, not indefinitely.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Define elasticity. Answer: The ability to regain original shape and size after unloading within the elastic limit.\n2. A spring changes from 8 cm to 11 cm. Find its extension. Answer: 3 cm.\n3. What does a large spring constant mean? Answer: A stiffer spring.\n4. Why is the elastic-energy graph area triangular in the Hooke's-law region? Answer: Force rises linearly from zero.\n5. What is the SI unit of Young's modulus? Answer: Pascal (Pa).\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw the force–extension graph with both axes and units. Explain its straight section, gradient and curved region. Then practise finding extension, spring constant, energy and Young's modulus without confusing their formulas.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"9. Elasticity, Hooke's Law and Elastic Potential Energy","body":"1. ELASTICITY: WHAT HAPPENS WHEN A FORCE CHANGES SHAPE?\nPull a rubber band gently, then release it. It stretches while you pull and returns towards its original length afterwards. Press a spring and release it: it also returns. These are familiar illustrations of elasticity.\nDEFINITION: Elasticity is the ability of a material to regain its original shape and size after the deforming force is removed, provided the elastic limit has not been exceeded.\nA deforming force is a force that changes a body's shape or size. It may stretch, compress, bend or twist a body. Elastic does not mean 'soft': a steel wire can be elastic even though it is difficult to stretch. If a force is too large, a material may remain permanently stretched; that is plastic deformation.\n\n2. EXTENSION, HOOKE'S LAW AND SPRING CONSTANT\nImagine hanging a spring beside a ruler. Its length without a load is its ORIGINAL LENGTH. When a load is added, it has a NEW LENGTH. The increase in length is called EXTENSION:\nextension e = stretched length − original length.\nDo not confuse extension with the total stretched length. A spring 10 cm long that becomes 14 cm long has extended by 4 cm, not 14 cm.\nDEFINITION: Hooke's law states that the extension of an elastic spring is directly proportional to the applied force, provided the limit of proportionality is not exceeded.\nF ∝ e, so F = ke.\nHere F = applied force (N), e = extension (m), and k = spring constant (N/m). The spring constant measures stiffness: a larger k means more force is required for the same extension. If a spring extends 2 cm under 4 N, it should extend 4 cm under 8 N only while Hooke's law holds.\nHOW TO INVESTIGATE IT: Clamp a spring vertically with a ruler beside it. Record its original length. Add known masses one at a time, wait for the spring to settle, record its new length, and subtract the original length to find each extension. Convert each mass to force using F = mg. Repeat readings and avoid overloading. Plot force F on the vertical axis and extension e on the horizontal axis.\nThe straight portion of a force–extension graph passes through the origin; its gradient is k. If the axes are reversed, the gradient is 1/k. At the limit of proportionality the graph stops being straight. The elastic limit concerns whether the material can still return to its original dimensions; the two limits need not be identical.\n\nExample (i)\nA spring has original length 12 cm and stretched length 17 cm. Find its extension.\nSolution\nFormula: e = final length − original length.\ne = 17 − 12 = 5 cm = 0.05 m.\n\nExample (ii)\nA force of 8 N produces an extension of 4 cm. Calculate the spring constant.\nSolution\nFormula: k = F/e.\nConvert 4 cm = 0.04 m.\nk = 8/0.04 = 200 N/m.\n\nExample (iii)\nA spring of constant 150 N/m carries a load of mass 0.30 kg. Calculate its extension. Take g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: F = mg; e = F/k.\nF = 0.30 × 10 = 3 N.\ne = 3/150 = 0.02 m = 2 cm.\n\nExample (iv)\nOn a force–extension graph, force increases from 2 N to 6 N while extension increases from 0.01 m to 0.03 m. Find the gradient.\nSolution\nFormula: gradient = change in force/change in extension.\nk = (6 − 2)/(0.03 − 0.01) = 4/0.02 = 200 N/m.\n\n3. ELASTIC POTENTIAL ENERGY AND WORK DONE\nWhen you stretch a spring, you do work against its restoring force. If it returns elastically, energy is stored in the spring and can be released when you let go. This stored energy is called ELASTIC POTENTIAL ENERGY.\nFor a spring obeying Hooke's law, force increases steadily from zero to its final value. The average force during gradual stretching is therefore half the final force. Work done equals the area under the force–extension graph:\nE = ½Fe = ½ke².\nE is measured in joules (J). The triangular area formula applies to the straight-line Hooke's-law region. If the graph is curved, find the actual area under the curve instead.\n\nExample (v)\nA spring of constant 100 N/m is extended by 0.20 m. Find the stored energy.\nSolution\nFormula: E = ½ke².\nE = ½ × 100 × (0.20)² = 2 J.\n\nExample (vi)\nA spring is gradually stretched through 0.10 m until the force reaches 20 N. Find the work done.\nSolution\nFormula: E = ½Fe.\nE = ½ × 20 × 0.10 = 1 J.\nThe factor ½ is essential because the force rises gradually from zero.\n\nExample (vii)\nIf the extension of a Hooke's-law spring doubles, what happens to its elastic energy?\nSolution\nFormula: E = ½ke².\nWith the same k, E is proportional to e². Doubling e gives 2² = 4 times the energy.\n\n4. STRESS, STRAIN AND YOUNG'S MODULUS\nA long thin wire stretches more easily than a short thick wire made of the same material. To compare materials fairly, we must account for the wire's dimensions.\nDEFINITION: Tensile stress is the stretching force divided by the wire's cross-sectional area.\nStress = F/A, unit pascal (Pa) or N/m².\nDEFINITION: Tensile strain is the extension divided by the original length.\nStrain = e/L; strain has no unit because it is a ratio of lengths.\nDEFINITION: Young's modulus is tensile stress divided by tensile strain in the linear elastic region.\nY = stress/strain = (F/A)/(e/L) = FL/(Ae).\nIts unit is Pa. A high Young's modulus means the material is stiff and resists stretching. Stiffness is not the same as breaking strength: a material may resist extension yet still fail under a sufficiently large force.\nThink of stress as how concentrated the pull is, strain as the fraction by which the wire lengthens, and Young's modulus as how much stress is needed to produce a given strain.\n\nExample (viii)\nA wire 2 m long extends by 1 mm. Find its tensile strain.\nSolution\nFormula: strain = e/L.\nConvert 1 mm = 0.001 m.\nStrain = 0.001/2 = 0.0005.\n\nExample (ix)\nA wire of cross-sectional area 2 × 10⁻⁶ m² is pulled by 40 N. Find its tensile stress.\nSolution\nFormula: stress = F/A.\nStress = 40/(2 × 10⁻⁶) = 2 × 10⁷ Pa.\n\nExample (x)\nA wire 2 m long with area 1 × 10⁻⁶ m² extends 1 mm under a force of 50 N. Find Young's modulus.\nSolution\nFormula: Y = FL/(Ae).\nConvert 1 mm = 0.001 m.\nY = (50 × 2)/[(1 × 10⁻⁶) × 0.001] = 1 × 10¹¹ Pa.\n\n5. ELASTIC AND PLASTIC BEHAVIOUR IN PRACTICE\nAn elastic deformation disappears after the load is removed; a plastic deformation remains. The limit of proportionality marks the end of a straight force–extension relationship. The elastic limit marks the greatest deformation from which the material can still recover fully. Stretching beyond the elastic limit may permanently lengthen a spring; a still greater force may cause breaking.\nExamples of elastic applications include spring balances, vehicle suspension and vibration absorbers. Springs should not be overloaded. In a laboratory graph, label both axes and units, mark the straight-line region, and do not assume that a curved region obeys F = ke.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nExtension is not stretched length. Convert cm and mm to m before calculating k, E or Y. A spring constant has units N/m, while Young's modulus has units Pa. Strain has no unit. A force–extension gradient is k only when force is on the vertical axis. Hooke's law applies within the proportional region, not indefinitely.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Define elasticity. Answer: The ability to regain original shape and size after unloading within the elastic limit.\n2. A spring changes from 8 cm to 11 cm. Find its extension. Answer: 3 cm.\n3. What does a large spring constant mean? Answer: A stiffer spring.\n4. Why is the elastic-energy graph area triangular in the Hooke's-law region? Answer: Force rises linearly from zero.\n5. What is the SI unit of Young's modulus? Answer: Pascal (Pa).\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw the force–extension graph with both axes and units. Explain its straight section, gradient and curved region. Then practise finding extension, spring constant, energy and Young's modulus without confusing their formulas."},"old":{"title":"9. Elasticity, Hooke's Law and Elastic Potential Energy","body":"New curriculum-first Physics lesson; verified year-by-year past-question mapping pending."}},{"id":"physics-pressure-in-solids-and-liquids","title":"10. Pressure in Solids and Liquids","body":"1. PRESSURE IN SOLIDS: WHY CONTACT AREA MATTERS\nPush the sharp end of a nail against wood, then imagine pushing its blunt end with the same force. The sharp end enters more easily because the force acts on a smaller area. Similarly, a schoolbag strap that is broad feels more comfortable than a thin strap carrying the same load because the force is spread over more area.\nDEFINITION: Pressure is the force acting normally (perpendicularly) per unit area of a surface.\nP = F/A.\nP is pressure in pascals (Pa), F is the perpendicular force in newtons (N), and A is contact area in square metres (m²). One pascal equals one newton per square metre. At constant area, increasing force increases pressure. At constant force, decreasing area increases pressure. Broad foundations, wide tyres on soft ground and snowshoes spread weight over a larger area.\n\nExample (i)\nA block exerts a force of 600 N on an area of 0.30 m². Find the pressure.\nSolution\nFormula: P = F/A.\nP = 600/0.30 = 2,000 Pa.\n\nExample (ii)\nA force of 120 N acts on 20 cm². Find the pressure.\nSolution\nFormula: P = F/A.\nConvert area: 20 cm² = 20/10,000 = 0.002 m².\nP = 120/0.002 = 60,000 Pa.\n\nExample (iii)\nA 200 N block has a base measuring 20 cm by 10 cm. Find its pressure when resting on that face.\nSolution\nFormula: A = length × breadth; P = F/A.\nA = 0.20 × 0.10 = 0.020 m².\nP = 200/0.020 = 10,000 Pa.\n\n2. PRESSURE IN LIQUIDS: DEPTH, DENSITY AND DIRECTION\nImagine three holes at different heights on the side of a water-filled container. The lowest hole generally sends water farther because the water pressure is greater at greater depth. A liquid at rest pushes on the walls and bottom of its container.\nDEFINITION: Hydrostatic pressure is the pressure exerted by a liquid at rest because of the weight of the liquid above a point. For a liquid open to the atmosphere, its GAUGE pressure at vertical depth h is:\nP = ρgh.\nρ is liquid density (kg/m³), g is gravitational field strength (m/s²), and h is vertical depth below the free surface (m). This formula gives the pressure due to the liquid alone. Absolute pressure = atmospheric pressure + ρgh.\nPressure increases with depth and density. At equal depths in the same connected stationary liquid, pressure is equal even if the container shapes differ. Pressure at a point acts in all directions; on a wall it acts perpendicular to that wall. Water behind a dam exerts more pressure near the bottom, which is one reason dams are often thicker there.\nThe formula depends on VERTICAL depth, not the distance measured along a sloping wall. It does not depend on the total amount of liquid in the container.\n\nExample (iv)\nFind the gauge pressure at a depth of 3 m in water. Take ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: P = ρgh.\nP = 1,000 × 10 × 3 = 30,000 Pa.\n\nExample (v)\nAt what depth in water is gauge pressure 50,000 Pa? Use ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: h = P/(ρg).\nh = 50,000/(1,000 × 10) = 5 m.\n\nExample (vi)\nOil has density 800 kg/m³. Find the gauge pressure 2 m below its surface. Take g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: P = ρgh.\nP = 800 × 10 × 2 = 16,000 Pa.\nAt the same depth, this is less than in water because oil is less dense.\n\n3. PASCAL'S PRINCIPLE AND HYDRAULIC MACHINES\nA mechanic can raise a heavy vehicle using a hydraulic jack by pushing a much smaller piston. The small piston creates a pressure increase in the enclosed liquid, and that pressure is transmitted to the larger piston.\nDEFINITION: Pascal's principle states that a change in pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished throughout the fluid.\nFor an ideal hydraulic press with pistons at the same level:\nF₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂.\nF₁ and A₁ refer to the small input piston; F₂ and A₂ to the large output piston. Because A₂ is larger, F₂ can be larger. But the input piston must move farther than the output piston:\nA₁d₁ = A₂d₂.\nFor an ideal machine, F₁d₁ = F₂d₂. A hydraulic machine multiplies force, not energy. In practice, friction and other losses reduce efficiency. Hydraulic brakes transmit pressure to wheel brakes; hydraulic lifts and jacks raise loads.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 50 N force acts on a piston of area 0.01 m². The output piston has area 0.20 m². Find the ideal output force.\nSolution\nFormula: F₂ = F₁A₂/A₁.\nF₂ = 50 × 0.20/0.01 = 1,000 N.\n\nExample (viii)\nThe large piston area is five times the small piston area. If the small piston moves 25 cm, find the large piston's movement.\nSolution\nFormula: A₁d₁ = A₂d₂.\nd₂ = (A₁/A₂)d₁ = 25/5 = 5 cm.\n\nExample (ix)\nAn ideal press has a small piston of area 4 cm² and a large piston of area 100 cm². What input force is needed to support 500 N?\nSolution\nFormula: F₁ = F₂A₁/A₂.\nF₁ = 500 × 4/100 = 20 N.\nThe areas may both remain in cm² because their ratio is dimensionless.\n\n4. ATMOSPHERIC PRESSURE, BAROMETERS AND MANOMETERS\nEven though we cannot see air, it has mass and weight. The atmosphere therefore exerts pressure on objects and on the surfaces of liquids.\nDEFINITION: Atmospheric pressure is the pressure exerted by the weight of the air above a surface. It generally decreases as altitude increases.\nA BAROMETER measures atmospheric pressure. In a simple mercury barometer, a long glass tube closed at one end is filled with mercury and inverted into a mercury reservoir. Atmospheric pressure acting on the reservoir supports a mercury column. The space above the column is approximately a vacuum. The vertical column height h gives:\nP_atm = ρmercury gh.\nA MANOMETER measures a pressure difference. In a simple U-tube manometer containing one liquid, the vertical difference Δh between the two liquid surfaces corresponds to ΔP = ρgΔh. If one arm is open to air, the level difference helps determine whether the gas pressure is above or below atmospheric pressure.\nA drinking straw works because drawing air out reduces the pressure inside the straw; atmospheric pressure on the drink's surface pushes the liquid upward. 'Suction' is not a separate pulling force.\n\nExample (x)\nA mercury barometer shows 0.76 m of mercury. Calculate atmospheric pressure if ρ = 13,600 kg/m³ and g = 9.8 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: P = ρgh.\nP = 13,600 × 9.8 × 0.76 = 101,292.8 Pa ≈ 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa.\n\nExample (xi)\nA U-tube manometer containing water shows a vertical level difference of 0.15 m. Find the pressure difference. Take ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: ΔP = ρgΔh.\nΔP = 1,000 × 10 × 0.15 = 1,500 Pa.\n\n5. APPLICATIONS AND COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nSharp tools produce high pressure because their contact area is small. Broad foundations lower pressure on soil. Dams must withstand greater pressure at greater depth. A hydraulic press produces more force at its large piston but less displacement. A barometer measures atmospheric pressure; a manometer measures a pressure difference.\nAlways distinguish gauge pressure from absolute pressure. For liquids, use density of the LIQUID and VERTICAL depth. Convert cm² to m² using 10,000, not 100. For manometers, use the vertical difference between levels, not the length of the U-shaped tube.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Define pressure. Answer: Normal force per unit area.\n2. Why does a sharp knife cut more easily? Answer: Smaller contact area gives greater pressure.\n3. What happens to hydrostatic pressure as depth increases? Answer: It increases.\n4. State Pascal's principle. Answer: A pressure change in an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished throughout it.\n5. Which instrument measures atmospheric pressure? Answer: Barometer.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nSketch a block resting on two different faces and compare its pressures. Draw a tank with two points at different depths and label ρ, g and h. For a hydraulic press, label both piston areas and forces. For a manometer, mark the VERTICAL level difference Δh.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"10. Pressure in Solids and Liquids","body":"1. PRESSURE IN SOLIDS: WHY CONTACT AREA MATTERS\nPush the sharp end of a nail against wood, then imagine pushing its blunt end with the same force. The sharp end enters more easily because the force acts on a smaller area. Similarly, a schoolbag strap that is broad feels more comfortable than a thin strap carrying the same load because the force is spread over more area.\nDEFINITION: Pressure is the force acting normally (perpendicularly) per unit area of a surface.\nP = F/A.\nP is pressure in pascals (Pa), F is the perpendicular force in newtons (N), and A is contact area in square metres (m²). One pascal equals one newton per square metre. At constant area, increasing force increases pressure. At constant force, decreasing area increases pressure. Broad foundations, wide tyres on soft ground and snowshoes spread weight over a larger area.\n\nExample (i)\nA block exerts a force of 600 N on an area of 0.30 m². Find the pressure.\nSolution\nFormula: P = F/A.\nP = 600/0.30 = 2,000 Pa.\n\nExample (ii)\nA force of 120 N acts on 20 cm². Find the pressure.\nSolution\nFormula: P = F/A.\nConvert area: 20 cm² = 20/10,000 = 0.002 m².\nP = 120/0.002 = 60,000 Pa.\n\nExample (iii)\nA 200 N block has a base measuring 20 cm by 10 cm. Find its pressure when resting on that face.\nSolution\nFormula: A = length × breadth; P = F/A.\nA = 0.20 × 0.10 = 0.020 m².\nP = 200/0.020 = 10,000 Pa.\n\n2. PRESSURE IN LIQUIDS: DEPTH, DENSITY AND DIRECTION\nImagine three holes at different heights on the side of a water-filled container. The lowest hole generally sends water farther because the water pressure is greater at greater depth. A liquid at rest pushes on the walls and bottom of its container.\nDEFINITION: Hydrostatic pressure is the pressure exerted by a liquid at rest because of the weight of the liquid above a point. For a liquid open to the atmosphere, its GAUGE pressure at vertical depth h is:\nP = ρgh.\nρ is liquid density (kg/m³), g is gravitational field strength (m/s²), and h is vertical depth below the free surface (m). This formula gives the pressure due to the liquid alone. Absolute pressure = atmospheric pressure + ρgh.\nPressure increases with depth and density. At equal depths in the same connected stationary liquid, pressure is equal even if the container shapes differ. Pressure at a point acts in all directions; on a wall it acts perpendicular to that wall. Water behind a dam exerts more pressure near the bottom, which is one reason dams are often thicker there.\nThe formula depends on VERTICAL depth, not the distance measured along a sloping wall. It does not depend on the total amount of liquid in the container.\n\nExample (iv)\nFind the gauge pressure at a depth of 3 m in water. Take ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: P = ρgh.\nP = 1,000 × 10 × 3 = 30,000 Pa.\n\nExample (v)\nAt what depth in water is gauge pressure 50,000 Pa? Use ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: h = P/(ρg).\nh = 50,000/(1,000 × 10) = 5 m.\n\nExample (vi)\nOil has density 800 kg/m³. Find the gauge pressure 2 m below its surface. Take g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: P = ρgh.\nP = 800 × 10 × 2 = 16,000 Pa.\nAt the same depth, this is less than in water because oil is less dense.\n\n3. PASCAL'S PRINCIPLE AND HYDRAULIC MACHINES\nA mechanic can raise a heavy vehicle using a hydraulic jack by pushing a much smaller piston. The small piston creates a pressure increase in the enclosed liquid, and that pressure is transmitted to the larger piston.\nDEFINITION: Pascal's principle states that a change in pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished throughout the fluid.\nFor an ideal hydraulic press with pistons at the same level:\nF₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂.\nF₁ and A₁ refer to the small input piston; F₂ and A₂ to the large output piston. Because A₂ is larger, F₂ can be larger. But the input piston must move farther than the output piston:\nA₁d₁ = A₂d₂.\nFor an ideal machine, F₁d₁ = F₂d₂. A hydraulic machine multiplies force, not energy. In practice, friction and other losses reduce efficiency. Hydraulic brakes transmit pressure to wheel brakes; hydraulic lifts and jacks raise loads.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 50 N force acts on a piston of area 0.01 m². The output piston has area 0.20 m². Find the ideal output force.\nSolution\nFormula: F₂ = F₁A₂/A₁.\nF₂ = 50 × 0.20/0.01 = 1,000 N.\n\nExample (viii)\nThe large piston area is five times the small piston area. If the small piston moves 25 cm, find the large piston's movement.\nSolution\nFormula: A₁d₁ = A₂d₂.\nd₂ = (A₁/A₂)d₁ = 25/5 = 5 cm.\n\nExample (ix)\nAn ideal press has a small piston of area 4 cm² and a large piston of area 100 cm². What input force is needed to support 500 N?\nSolution\nFormula: F₁ = F₂A₁/A₂.\nF₁ = 500 × 4/100 = 20 N.\nThe areas may both remain in cm² because their ratio is dimensionless.\n\n4. ATMOSPHERIC PRESSURE, BAROMETERS AND MANOMETERS\nEven though we cannot see air, it has mass and weight. The atmosphere therefore exerts pressure on objects and on the surfaces of liquids.\nDEFINITION: Atmospheric pressure is the pressure exerted by the weight of the air above a surface. It generally decreases as altitude increases.\nA BAROMETER measures atmospheric pressure. In a simple mercury barometer, a long glass tube closed at one end is filled with mercury and inverted into a mercury reservoir. Atmospheric pressure acting on the reservoir supports a mercury column. The space above the column is approximately a vacuum. The vertical column height h gives:\nP_atm = ρmercury gh.\nA MANOMETER measures a pressure difference. In a simple U-tube manometer containing one liquid, the vertical difference Δh between the two liquid surfaces corresponds to ΔP = ρgΔh. If one arm is open to air, the level difference helps determine whether the gas pressure is above or below atmospheric pressure.\nA drinking straw works because drawing air out reduces the pressure inside the straw; atmospheric pressure on the drink's surface pushes the liquid upward. 'Suction' is not a separate pulling force.\n\nExample (x)\nA mercury barometer shows 0.76 m of mercury. Calculate atmospheric pressure if ρ = 13,600 kg/m³ and g = 9.8 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: P = ρgh.\nP = 13,600 × 9.8 × 0.76 = 101,292.8 Pa ≈ 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa.\n\nExample (xi)\nA U-tube manometer containing water shows a vertical level difference of 0.15 m. Find the pressure difference. Take ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: ΔP = ρgΔh.\nΔP = 1,000 × 10 × 0.15 = 1,500 Pa.\n\n5. APPLICATIONS AND COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nSharp tools produce high pressure because their contact area is small. Broad foundations lower pressure on soil. Dams must withstand greater pressure at greater depth. A hydraulic press produces more force at its large piston but less displacement. A barometer measures atmospheric pressure; a manometer measures a pressure difference.\nAlways distinguish gauge pressure from absolute pressure. For liquids, use density of the LIQUID and VERTICAL depth. Convert cm² to m² using 10,000, not 100. For manometers, use the vertical difference between levels, not the length of the U-shaped tube.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Define pressure. Answer: Normal force per unit area.\n2. Why does a sharp knife cut more easily? Answer: Smaller contact area gives greater pressure.\n3. What happens to hydrostatic pressure as depth increases? Answer: It increases.\n4. State Pascal's principle. Answer: A pressure change in an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished throughout it.\n5. Which instrument measures atmospheric pressure? Answer: Barometer.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nSketch a block resting on two different faces and compare its pressures. Draw a tank with two points at different depths and label ρ, g and h. For a hydraulic press, label both piston areas and forces. For a manometer, mark the VERTICAL level difference Δh."},"old":{"title":"10. Pressure in Solids and Liquids","body":"New curriculum-first Physics lesson; verified year-by-year past-question mapping pending."}},{"id":"physics-upthrust-flotation-density-and-surface-tension","title":"11. Upthrust, Flotation, Density and Surface Tension","body":"1. UPTHRUST: WHY OBJECTS SEEM LIGHTER IN WATER\nImagine holding a heavy stone under water. It seems easier to support than when you lift it into air. The stone has not lost mass: water pushes it upwards and reduces the downward force your hand must supply.\nDEFINITION: Upthrust (buoyant force) is the upward resultant force exerted by a fluid on a body partly or fully immersed in it. Both liquids and gases exert upthrust.\nWHY IT HAPPENS: A fluid exerts pressure in every direction. Pressure is greater at greater depth, so for a submerged block the upward force on its lower face exceeds the downward force on its upper face. Horizontal pressure forces cancel for a symmetric block in still water. The remaining resultant is upwards. The greater the volume of fluid displaced, the greater the upthrust, if fluid density stays the same.\nAPPARENT WEIGHT: A spring balance reads the supporting force, not the body's mass. When a body hangs at rest fully immersed, apparent weight = true weight − upthrust. A spring balance may show 40 N in air and 28 N in water: upthrust = 12 N. This is not a loss of 12 kg.\n\n2. ARCHIMEDES' PRINCIPLE AND DISPLACED FLUID\nPut a stone into a brim-full container of water: some water overflows. The overflow is the displaced water. If a body is completely submerged, its displaced volume equals the volume of the body. If it is only partly submerged, displaced volume equals ONLY its submerged volume.\nDEFINITION: Archimedes' principle states that a body partly or wholly immersed in a fluid experiences an upthrust equal to the WEIGHT of the fluid displaced.\nU = ρ × g × V\nwhere ρ = density of the displaced fluid, g = gravitational field strength, and V = volume of fluid displaced.\nUpthrust U is measured in newtons (N). Density ρ is in kg/m³, g is in m/s², and displaced volume V is in m³.\nWHY THE FORMULA WORKS: Mass of displaced fluid = density × volume, or m = ρV. Its weight is mg = ρVg. By Archimedes' principle, that weight equals the upthrust. This also explains why the same object has a greater upthrust in denser salt water than in fresh water, if the submerged volume is unchanged.\nEXPERIMENT: Weigh a solid with a spring balance in air. Lower it fully into an overflow can of water without touching the bottom. Collect and weigh the displaced water. The loss of apparent weight should approximately equal the weight of the displaced water. For the most reliable comparison, ensure the solid is fully immersed and does not touch the container. Avoid confusing the MASS of the water collected with its WEIGHT.\n\n\nExample (i)\nA fully submerged object displaces 0.002 m³ of water. Calculate the upthrust. Take ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: U = ρgV.\nU = 1,000 × 10 × 0.002 = 20 N.\n\nExample (ii)\nA metal object weighs 50 N in air and 35 N when fully immersed in water. Find the upthrust.\nSolution\nFormula: U = true weight − apparent weight.\nU = 50 − 35 = 15 N.\n\nExample (iii)\nA fully submerged object experiences 30 N upthrust in water. Find its volume using ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: V = U/(ρg).\nV = 30/(1,000 × 10) = 0.003 m³.\n\n3. FLOTATION: FLOATING, SINKING AND NEUTRAL BUOYANCY\nA small iron nail sinks, yet a huge steel ship floats. The ship is hollow and contains air, so the average density of the entire ship is low enough to float while displacing sufficient water.\nDEFINITION: The principle of flotation states that a body floating at rest displaces a weight of fluid equal to its own weight. Thus U = W for a freely floating body in equilibrium.\nSINKING: If a fully submerged object's weight is greater than the available upthrust, the resultant force is downward and it sinks. RISING: If upthrust exceeds weight, the resultant force is upward and it rises. NEUTRAL BUOYANCY: If upthrust equals weight while fully submerged, the body can remain suspended without rising or sinking.\nFor a freely floating uniform body, weight equals upthrust. Therefore:\nρ(object) × V(total) × g = ρ(fluid) × V(submerged) × g.\nFraction submerged = submerged volume ÷ total volume = density of object ÷ density of fluid.\nThis fraction relationship assumes the body has a uniform average density and floats freely.\nA wooden block of density 600 kg/m³ floating in water of density 1,000 kg/m³ has 60% of its volume under water and 40% above it. In denser salt water, the same floating body needs to displace LESS volume to support its weight.\nAPPLICATIONS: Ships, submarines, life jackets and hydrometers all rely on buoyancy. A submarine changes its average density by taking water into or expelling water from ballast tanks.\n\n\nExample (iv)\nA wooden block has density 600 kg/m³ and floats in water of density 1,000 kg/m³. What fraction of its volume is submerged?\nSolution\nFormula: fraction submerged = ρobject/ρfluid.\nFraction = 600/1,000 = 0.60 = 60%.\n\nExample (v)\nA block of mass 4 kg floats in water. Find the volume of water displaced. Use ρ = 1,000 kg/m³.\nSolution\nFloating equilibrium: ρwater g V = mg.\nThus V = m/ρwater = 4/1,000 = 0.004 m³.\n\n4. DENSITY, RELATIVE DENSITY AND HYDROMETERS\nTwo objects can have the same size but different masses. Density tells us how much mass is packed into a given volume.\nDEFINITION: Density is mass per unit volume: ρ = m/V. SI unit: kg/m³. Water has a density of about 1,000 kg/m³ under ordinary school-level conditions.\nDEFINITION: Relative density (specific gravity) is the ratio of a substance's density to the density of water at the stated reference temperature. Relative density = ρsubstance/ρwater. Because it is a ratio of like quantities, it has NO unit.\nA HYDROMETER is a floating instrument used to compare or measure liquid density. In a dense liquid, less of the instrument needs to be submerged to displace enough weight, so it floats HIGHER. In a less dense liquid it sinks deeper. The scale must be read at the appropriate liquid level as specified for the instrument.\nExample: oil of density 800 kg/m³ has relative density 0.8; it is less dense than water and normally forms a layer above water if the liquids do not mix.\n\n\nExample (vi)\nA liquid has density 800 kg/m³. Find its relative density compared with water at 1,000 kg/m³.\nSolution\nFormula: relative density = ρliquid/ρwater.\nRelative density = 800/1,000 = 0.8.\n\n5. SURFACE TENSION, COHESION, ADHESION AND CAPILLARITY\nWatch a water droplet bead up on a waxy surface. Or notice how a tiny insect can stand on water without immediately breaking its surface. These are everyday effects of molecular forces.\nDEFINITION: Cohesion is attraction between molecules of the SAME substance; adhesion is attraction between molecules of DIFFERENT substances.\nDEFINITION: Surface tension is the force per unit length acting tangentially to a liquid surface and perpendicular to an imaginary line drawn on that surface. Its SI unit is N/m. Molecules inside a liquid are attracted from all sides; surface molecules experience an imbalance that makes the surface tend to minimise its area. Surface tension helps small drops become rounded and supports some small insects. Detergent generally reduces water's surface tension.\nDEFINITION: A meniscus is the curved surface of a liquid where it meets a container. Water in clean glass has a concave meniscus because adhesion to glass is strong; mercury in glass typically has a convex meniscus because cohesion dominates.\nDEFINITION: Capillarity is the rise or depression of a liquid in a narrow tube or fine pore caused by surface tension and adhesive/cohesive effects. Water rises in narrow clean glass tubes, whereas mercury is depressed. For the same liquid and tube material, a narrower tube generally produces a greater capillary rise.\nAPPLICATIONS: Water travels through narrow spaces in soil and porous materials; lamp wicks draw fuel by capillary action. These examples involve both adhesion and cohesion, not surface tension alone.\n\n\nExample (vii)\nWhy does a clean thin glass tube draw water higher than a wider tube?\nSolution\nPrinciple: Capillary rise increases as tube radius decreases, for the same liquid and surface conditions.\nTherefore the narrower tube has the greater rise.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nDo not confuse mass of displaced fluid with its weight. An object floating at rest has upthrust equal to its weight; a sinking object can still experience upthrust, but it is insufficient to balance its weight. Use the immersed volume when calculating displacement. Relative density has no unit.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. State Archimedes' principle. Answer: Upthrust equals the weight of fluid displaced.\n2. Why can a ship float? Answer: Its average density is sufficiently low and it displaces water whose weight equals the ship's weight.\n3. What is apparent weight? Answer: True weight minus upthrust.\n4. Does relative density have a unit? Answer: No.\n5. What happens to surface tension when detergent is added to water? Answer: It generally decreases.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw a floating block and label downward weight and upward upthrust. Draw a fully submerged block with the same forces. Practise identifying the displaced volume in each case.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"11. Upthrust, Flotation, Density and Surface Tension","body":"1. UPTHRUST: WHY OBJECTS SEEM LIGHTER IN WATER\nImagine holding a heavy stone under water. It seems easier to support than when you lift it into air. The stone has not lost mass: water pushes it upwards and reduces the downward force your hand must supply.\nDEFINITION: Upthrust (buoyant force) is the upward resultant force exerted by a fluid on a body partly or fully immersed in it. Both liquids and gases exert upthrust.\nWHY IT HAPPENS: A fluid exerts pressure in every direction. Pressure is greater at greater depth, so for a submerged block the upward force on its lower face exceeds the downward force on its upper face. Horizontal pressure forces cancel for a symmetric block in still water. The remaining resultant is upwards. The greater the volume of fluid displaced, the greater the upthrust, if fluid density stays the same.\nAPPARENT WEIGHT: A spring balance reads the supporting force, not the body's mass. When a body hangs at rest fully immersed, apparent weight = true weight − upthrust. A spring balance may show 40 N in air and 28 N in water: upthrust = 12 N. This is not a loss of 12 kg.\n\n2. ARCHIMEDES' PRINCIPLE AND DISPLACED FLUID\nPut a stone into a brim-full container of water: some water overflows. The overflow is the displaced water. If a body is completely submerged, its displaced volume equals the volume of the body. If it is only partly submerged, displaced volume equals ONLY its submerged volume.\nDEFINITION: Archimedes' principle states that a body partly or wholly immersed in a fluid experiences an upthrust equal to the WEIGHT of the fluid displaced.\nU = ρ × g × V\nwhere ρ = density of the displaced fluid, g = gravitational field strength, and V = volume of fluid displaced.\nUpthrust U is measured in newtons (N). Density ρ is in kg/m³, g is in m/s², and displaced volume V is in m³.\nWHY THE FORMULA WORKS: Mass of displaced fluid = density × volume, or m = ρV. Its weight is mg = ρVg. By Archimedes' principle, that weight equals the upthrust. This also explains why the same object has a greater upthrust in denser salt water than in fresh water, if the submerged volume is unchanged.\nEXPERIMENT: Weigh a solid with a spring balance in air. Lower it fully into an overflow can of water without touching the bottom. Collect and weigh the displaced water. The loss of apparent weight should approximately equal the weight of the displaced water. For the most reliable comparison, ensure the solid is fully immersed and does not touch the container. Avoid confusing the MASS of the water collected with its WEIGHT.\n\n\nExample (i)\nA fully submerged object displaces 0.002 m³ of water. Calculate the upthrust. Take ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: U = ρgV.\nU = 1,000 × 10 × 0.002 = 20 N.\n\nExample (ii)\nA metal object weighs 50 N in air and 35 N when fully immersed in water. Find the upthrust.\nSolution\nFormula: U = true weight − apparent weight.\nU = 50 − 35 = 15 N.\n\nExample (iii)\nA fully submerged object experiences 30 N upthrust in water. Find its volume using ρ = 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 10 m/s².\nSolution\nFormula: V = U/(ρg).\nV = 30/(1,000 × 10) = 0.003 m³.\n\n3. FLOTATION: FLOATING, SINKING AND NEUTRAL BUOYANCY\nA small iron nail sinks, yet a huge steel ship floats. The ship is hollow and contains air, so the average density of the entire ship is low enough to float while displacing sufficient water.\nDEFINITION: The principle of flotation states that a body floating at rest displaces a weight of fluid equal to its own weight. Thus U = W for a freely floating body in equilibrium.\nSINKING: If a fully submerged object's weight is greater than the available upthrust, the resultant force is downward and it sinks. RISING: If upthrust exceeds weight, the resultant force is upward and it rises. NEUTRAL BUOYANCY: If upthrust equals weight while fully submerged, the body can remain suspended without rising or sinking.\nFor a freely floating uniform body, weight equals upthrust. Therefore:\nρ(object) × V(total) × g = ρ(fluid) × V(submerged) × g.\nFraction submerged = submerged volume ÷ total volume = density of object ÷ density of fluid.\nThis fraction relationship assumes the body has a uniform average density and floats freely.\nA wooden block of density 600 kg/m³ floating in water of density 1,000 kg/m³ has 60% of its volume under water and 40% above it. In denser salt water, the same floating body needs to displace LESS volume to support its weight.\nAPPLICATIONS: Ships, submarines, life jackets and hydrometers all rely on buoyancy. A submarine changes its average density by taking water into or expelling water from ballast tanks.\n\n\nExample (iv)\nA wooden block has density 600 kg/m³ and floats in water of density 1,000 kg/m³. What fraction of its volume is submerged?\nSolution\nFormula: fraction submerged = ρobject/ρfluid.\nFraction = 600/1,000 = 0.60 = 60%.\n\nExample (v)\nA block of mass 4 kg floats in water. Find the volume of water displaced. Use ρ = 1,000 kg/m³.\nSolution\nFloating equilibrium: ρwater g V = mg.\nThus V = m/ρwater = 4/1,000 = 0.004 m³.\n\n4. DENSITY, RELATIVE DENSITY AND HYDROMETERS\nTwo objects can have the same size but different masses. Density tells us how much mass is packed into a given volume.\nDEFINITION: Density is mass per unit volume: ρ = m/V. SI unit: kg/m³. Water has a density of about 1,000 kg/m³ under ordinary school-level conditions.\nDEFINITION: Relative density (specific gravity) is the ratio of a substance's density to the density of water at the stated reference temperature. Relative density = ρsubstance/ρwater. Because it is a ratio of like quantities, it has NO unit.\nA HYDROMETER is a floating instrument used to compare or measure liquid density. In a dense liquid, less of the instrument needs to be submerged to displace enough weight, so it floats HIGHER. In a less dense liquid it sinks deeper. The scale must be read at the appropriate liquid level as specified for the instrument.\nExample: oil of density 800 kg/m³ has relative density 0.8; it is less dense than water and normally forms a layer above water if the liquids do not mix.\n\n\nExample (vi)\nA liquid has density 800 kg/m³. Find its relative density compared with water at 1,000 kg/m³.\nSolution\nFormula: relative density = ρliquid/ρwater.\nRelative density = 800/1,000 = 0.8.\n\n5. SURFACE TENSION, COHESION, ADHESION AND CAPILLARITY\nWatch a water droplet bead up on a waxy surface. Or notice how a tiny insect can stand on water without immediately breaking its surface. These are everyday effects of molecular forces.\nDEFINITION: Cohesion is attraction between molecules of the SAME substance; adhesion is attraction between molecules of DIFFERENT substances.\nDEFINITION: Surface tension is the force per unit length acting tangentially to a liquid surface and perpendicular to an imaginary line drawn on that surface. Its SI unit is N/m. Molecules inside a liquid are attracted from all sides; surface molecules experience an imbalance that makes the surface tend to minimise its area. Surface tension helps small drops become rounded and supports some small insects. Detergent generally reduces water's surface tension.\nDEFINITION: A meniscus is the curved surface of a liquid where it meets a container. Water in clean glass has a concave meniscus because adhesion to glass is strong; mercury in glass typically has a convex meniscus because cohesion dominates.\nDEFINITION: Capillarity is the rise or depression of a liquid in a narrow tube or fine pore caused by surface tension and adhesive/cohesive effects. Water rises in narrow clean glass tubes, whereas mercury is depressed. For the same liquid and tube material, a narrower tube generally produces a greater capillary rise.\nAPPLICATIONS: Water travels through narrow spaces in soil and porous materials; lamp wicks draw fuel by capillary action. These examples involve both adhesion and cohesion, not surface tension alone.\n\n\nExample (vii)\nWhy does a clean thin glass tube draw water higher than a wider tube?\nSolution\nPrinciple: Capillary rise increases as tube radius decreases, for the same liquid and surface conditions.\nTherefore the narrower tube has the greater rise.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nDo not confuse mass of displaced fluid with its weight. An object floating at rest has upthrust equal to its weight; a sinking object can still experience upthrust, but it is insufficient to balance its weight. Use the immersed volume when calculating displacement. Relative density has no unit.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. State Archimedes' principle. Answer: Upthrust equals the weight of fluid displaced.\n2. Why can a ship float? Answer: Its average density is sufficiently low and it displaces water whose weight equals the ship's weight.\n3. What is apparent weight? Answer: True weight minus upthrust.\n4. Does relative density have a unit? Answer: No.\n5. What happens to surface tension when detergent is added to water? Answer: It generally decreases.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw a floating block and label downward weight and upward upthrust. Draw a fully submerged block with the same forces. Practise identifying the displaced volume in each case."},"old":{"title":"11. Upthrust, Flotation, Density and Surface Tension","body":"New curriculum-first Physics lesson; verified year-by-year past-question mapping pending."}},{"id":"physics-temperature-thermometers-and-thermal-expansion","title":"12. Temperature, Thermometers and Thermal Expansion","body":"1. HEAT, TEMPERATURE AND THERMAL EQUILIBRIUM\nTouch a metal spoon and a wooden spoon left in the same room. The metal may feel colder because it transfers energy away from your skin faster, even when both spoons are at the same temperature. How something FEELS is not a reliable thermometer.\nDEFINITION: Temperature is a physical quantity indicating the thermal state of a body and determining the direction of net heat transfer. It is related to the average kinetic energy of particles in a substance, although the exact relationship depends on the material.\nDEFINITION: Heat is energy transferred from one body or system to another because of a temperature difference. Heat is measured in joules (J); temperature is measured in kelvin (K) or degrees Celsius (°C).\nIf a hot cup is placed in cooler surroundings, energy flows from the cup to its surroundings. This continues until they reach THERMAL EQUILIBRIUM: the state in which bodies in thermal contact have the same temperature and no net heat flows between them.\nA large bucket of warm water can contain more internal energy than a small cup of hotter water. Do not equate temperature with total heat energy. A difference of 1 °C is the same SIZE as a difference of 1 K, although their zero points differ.\n\n\n2. CELSIUS, KELVIN AND FAHRENHEIT SCALES\nDifferent thermometers may display the same temperature using different numbers. On the Celsius scale, pure water freezes at approximately 0 °C and boils at approximately 100 °C at standard atmospheric pressure. On the Kelvin scale, absolute zero is 0 K; its unit is kelvin, NOT degrees kelvin.\nDEFINITION: A temperature scale assigns numerical values to thermal states using specified reference points and intervals.\nCelsius to Kelvin: T(K) = θ(°C) + 273.15. Kelvin to Celsius: θ(°C) = T(K) − 273.15. For approximate JAMB questions, use 273 where instructed.\nFahrenheit: F = (9/5)C + 32; conversely C = (5/9)(F − 32). At 0 °C the Fahrenheit reading is 32 °F; at 100 °C it is 212 °F.\nIMPORTANT DISTINCTION: 30 °C is a temperature reading, while a RISE of 30 °C is a temperature difference. A rise of 30 °C equals a rise of 30 K; do not add 273 to a difference.\n\n\nExample (i)\nConvert 27 °C to kelvin.\nSolution\nFormula: T = θ + 273.15.\nT = 27 + 273.15 = 300.15 K (approximately 300 K).\n\nExample (ii)\nConvert 40 °C to Fahrenheit.\nSolution\nFormula: F = (9/5)C + 32.\nF = (9/5 × 40) + 32 = 104 °F.\n\nExample (iii)\nThe temperature rises from 20 °C to 50 °C. State the rise in kelvin.\nSolution\nTemperature change = 50 − 20 = 30 °C.\nA change of 30 °C equals a change of 30 K. Do not add 273 to a temperature difference.\n\n3. THERMOMETERS: PARTS, THERMOMETRIC PROPERTIES AND CALIBRATION\nA liquid-in-glass thermometer contains a bulb with a liquid and a narrow capillary tube marked with a scale. When the liquid warms, it generally expands and rises in the capillary. The scale converts column height into a temperature reading.\nDEFINITION: A thermometer is an instrument used to measure temperature. A thermometric property is a measurable property that changes predictably with temperature, such as liquid volume, electrical resistance, thermoelectric voltage or gas pressure at constant volume.\nCLINICAL AND LABORATORY THERMOMETERS: Clinical thermometers are designed for body-temperature measurements, usually over a relatively narrow range; laboratory thermometers measure a broader range of temperatures. Some modern thermometers use electronic sensors rather than liquid expansion.\nDEFINITION: Sensitivity is the change in a thermometric property per unit temperature change. Range is the span of temperatures an instrument can measure. Response time is how quickly it approaches the temperature of the object being measured. Accuracy concerns closeness to the true temperature; precision concerns consistency of repeated readings.\nCALIBRATION: For a thermometer whose property changes linearly, mark two known reference points and divide the interval proportionally. If the liquid column is L₀ at 0 °C and L₁₀₀ at 100 °C, a reading L corresponds to:\nθ/100 = (L − L₀)/(L₁₀₀ − L₀).\nThis interpolation assumes a linear scale. Read at eye level to avoid parallax and wait until the reading stabilises.\n\n\nExample (iv)\nA thermometer's liquid column is 2 cm at 0 °C and 12 cm at 100 °C. What temperature corresponds to 7 cm, assuming linear expansion?\nSolution\nFormula: θ/100 = (L − L₀)/(L₁₀₀ − L₀).\nθ/100 = (7 − 2)/(12 − 2) = 5/10.\nθ = 50 °C.\n\n4. THERMAL EXPANSION OF SOLIDS\nA metal jar lid can become easier to loosen when warmed. A railway track may buckle if there is no allowance for heating. These are effects of thermal expansion.\nDEFINITION: Thermal expansion is an increase in a material's dimensions caused by a rise in temperature. As a solid warms, its particles vibrate more and their average separation usually increases.\nLINEAR EXPANSION: Change in length ΔL = αL₀ΔT. Here α is the coefficient of linear expansion (K⁻¹), L₀ is the ORIGINAL length (m), and ΔT is the temperature rise (K or °C). Final length L = L₀ + ΔL. The coefficient α tells us the fractional increase in length per degree of temperature rise.\nAREA AND VOLUME EXPANSION: A heated plate expands in area and a block expands in volume. For small changes in an isotropic solid, the area expansion coefficient is approximately 2α and the volume expansion coefficient approximately 3α. A hole in a metal plate also becomes larger when the plate is heated uniformly.\nAPPLICATIONS: Expansion joints in bridges, gaps or engineered allowances in railway structures, sagging overhead wires and loosening metal lids. A BIMETALLIC STRIP contains two bonded metals with different expansion coefficients. On heating, it bends towards the metal that expands less, which is useful in thermostats.\n\n\nExample (v)\nA 2 m metal rod has α = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ K⁻¹. Find its increase in length for a 50 K rise.\nSolution\nFormula: ΔL = αL₀ΔT.\nΔL = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ × 2 × 50 = 0.0012 m = 1.2 mm.\n\nExample (vi)\nA rod is 1.5 m long and expands by 0.9 mm for a 40 °C rise. Find α.\nSolution\nFormula: α = ΔL/(L₀ΔT).\nConvert 0.9 mm = 0.0009 m.\nα = 0.0009/(1.5 × 40) = 1.5 × 10⁻⁵ °C⁻¹.\n\n5. LIQUID EXPANSION AND THE ANOMALOUS BEHAVIOUR OF WATER\nIf you warm a flask of coloured liquid fitted with a narrow tube, the liquid level generally rises. The liquid expands, but the glass container expands too, so the observed rise is not simply the liquid's entire expansion.\nDEFINITION: Real expansion of a liquid is its actual increase in volume on heating. Apparent expansion is the increase observed relative to the expanding container. For ordinary small expansions, real volume expansion = apparent expansion + expansion of the container.\nDEFINITION: The coefficient of volume expansion is the fractional change in volume per unit temperature change: ΔV = γV₀ΔT, where γ is in K⁻¹. Liquids usually expand more than solids for the same temperature rise.\nANOMALOUS EXPANSION OF WATER: Unlike most liquids, water contracts as it warms from 0 °C to about 4 °C, becoming denser. At about 4 °C its density is greatest. Above 4 °C, it expands normally on warming. On cooling from 4 °C towards 0 °C it expands, and ice is less dense than liquid water.\nWHY THIS MATTERS: In cold weather, water near 4 °C tends to sink because it is denser, while colder water remains near the surface and eventually freezes. Floating ice forms an insulating layer that can help deeper water remain liquid. Do not say water has its greatest density at 0 °C.\n\n\nExample (vii)\nWhy does a pond tend to freeze first at its surface?\nSolution\nWater near 4 °C is densest and sinks. Water cooled below 4 °C stays nearer the surface, where it can freeze. The ice then provides some insulation.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nHeat is energy, temperature is not. Use the ORIGINAL length in ΔL = αL₀ΔT. Convert mm to m when the other lengths are in metres. Kelvin temperatures have no degree sign. Do not confuse a 20 °C temperature with a 20 °C temperature rise. Water has maximum density near 4 °C, not 0 °C.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is the SI unit of temperature? Answer: Kelvin (K).\n2. What causes heat to flow between two bodies? Answer: A temperature difference.\n3. How many kelvin is a rise of 15 °C? Answer: 15 K.\n4. Why are gaps left in bridges? Answer: To allow thermal expansion.\n5. At about what temperature is pure water densest? Answer: 4 °C.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nSketch a thermometer with 0 °C and 100 °C marks and locate an intermediate reading by proportional distance. Practise converting temperature values separately from temperature changes.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"12. Temperature, Thermometers and Thermal Expansion","body":"1. HEAT, TEMPERATURE AND THERMAL EQUILIBRIUM\nTouch a metal spoon and a wooden spoon left in the same room. The metal may feel colder because it transfers energy away from your skin faster, even when both spoons are at the same temperature. How something FEELS is not a reliable thermometer.\nDEFINITION: Temperature is a physical quantity indicating the thermal state of a body and determining the direction of net heat transfer. It is related to the average kinetic energy of particles in a substance, although the exact relationship depends on the material.\nDEFINITION: Heat is energy transferred from one body or system to another because of a temperature difference. Heat is measured in joules (J); temperature is measured in kelvin (K) or degrees Celsius (°C).\nIf a hot cup is placed in cooler surroundings, energy flows from the cup to its surroundings. This continues until they reach THERMAL EQUILIBRIUM: the state in which bodies in thermal contact have the same temperature and no net heat flows between them.\nA large bucket of warm water can contain more internal energy than a small cup of hotter water. Do not equate temperature with total heat energy. A difference of 1 °C is the same SIZE as a difference of 1 K, although their zero points differ.\n\n\n2. CELSIUS, KELVIN AND FAHRENHEIT SCALES\nDifferent thermometers may display the same temperature using different numbers. On the Celsius scale, pure water freezes at approximately 0 °C and boils at approximately 100 °C at standard atmospheric pressure. On the Kelvin scale, absolute zero is 0 K; its unit is kelvin, NOT degrees kelvin.\nDEFINITION: A temperature scale assigns numerical values to thermal states using specified reference points and intervals.\nCelsius to Kelvin: T(K) = θ(°C) + 273.15. Kelvin to Celsius: θ(°C) = T(K) − 273.15. For approximate JAMB questions, use 273 where instructed.\nFahrenheit: F = (9/5)C + 32; conversely C = (5/9)(F − 32). At 0 °C the Fahrenheit reading is 32 °F; at 100 °C it is 212 °F.\nIMPORTANT DISTINCTION: 30 °C is a temperature reading, while a RISE of 30 °C is a temperature difference. A rise of 30 °C equals a rise of 30 K; do not add 273 to a difference.\n\n\nExample (i)\nConvert 27 °C to kelvin.\nSolution\nFormula: T = θ + 273.15.\nT = 27 + 273.15 = 300.15 K (approximately 300 K).\n\nExample (ii)\nConvert 40 °C to Fahrenheit.\nSolution\nFormula: F = (9/5)C + 32.\nF = (9/5 × 40) + 32 = 104 °F.\n\nExample (iii)\nThe temperature rises from 20 °C to 50 °C. State the rise in kelvin.\nSolution\nTemperature change = 50 − 20 = 30 °C.\nA change of 30 °C equals a change of 30 K. Do not add 273 to a temperature difference.\n\n3. THERMOMETERS: PARTS, THERMOMETRIC PROPERTIES AND CALIBRATION\nA liquid-in-glass thermometer contains a bulb with a liquid and a narrow capillary tube marked with a scale. When the liquid warms, it generally expands and rises in the capillary. The scale converts column height into a temperature reading.\nDEFINITION: A thermometer is an instrument used to measure temperature. A thermometric property is a measurable property that changes predictably with temperature, such as liquid volume, electrical resistance, thermoelectric voltage or gas pressure at constant volume.\nCLINICAL AND LABORATORY THERMOMETERS: Clinical thermometers are designed for body-temperature measurements, usually over a relatively narrow range; laboratory thermometers measure a broader range of temperatures. Some modern thermometers use electronic sensors rather than liquid expansion.\nDEFINITION: Sensitivity is the change in a thermometric property per unit temperature change. Range is the span of temperatures an instrument can measure. Response time is how quickly it approaches the temperature of the object being measured. Accuracy concerns closeness to the true temperature; precision concerns consistency of repeated readings.\nCALIBRATION: For a thermometer whose property changes linearly, mark two known reference points and divide the interval proportionally. If the liquid column is L₀ at 0 °C and L₁₀₀ at 100 °C, a reading L corresponds to:\nθ/100 = (L − L₀)/(L₁₀₀ − L₀).\nThis interpolation assumes a linear scale. Read at eye level to avoid parallax and wait until the reading stabilises.\n\n\nExample (iv)\nA thermometer's liquid column is 2 cm at 0 °C and 12 cm at 100 °C. What temperature corresponds to 7 cm, assuming linear expansion?\nSolution\nFormula: θ/100 = (L − L₀)/(L₁₀₀ − L₀).\nθ/100 = (7 − 2)/(12 − 2) = 5/10.\nθ = 50 °C.\n\n4. THERMAL EXPANSION OF SOLIDS\nA metal jar lid can become easier to loosen when warmed. A railway track may buckle if there is no allowance for heating. These are effects of thermal expansion.\nDEFINITION: Thermal expansion is an increase in a material's dimensions caused by a rise in temperature. As a solid warms, its particles vibrate more and their average separation usually increases.\nLINEAR EXPANSION: Change in length ΔL = αL₀ΔT. Here α is the coefficient of linear expansion (K⁻¹), L₀ is the ORIGINAL length (m), and ΔT is the temperature rise (K or °C). Final length L = L₀ + ΔL. The coefficient α tells us the fractional increase in length per degree of temperature rise.\nAREA AND VOLUME EXPANSION: A heated plate expands in area and a block expands in volume. For small changes in an isotropic solid, the area expansion coefficient is approximately 2α and the volume expansion coefficient approximately 3α. A hole in a metal plate also becomes larger when the plate is heated uniformly.\nAPPLICATIONS: Expansion joints in bridges, gaps or engineered allowances in railway structures, sagging overhead wires and loosening metal lids. A BIMETALLIC STRIP contains two bonded metals with different expansion coefficients. On heating, it bends towards the metal that expands less, which is useful in thermostats.\n\n\nExample (v)\nA 2 m metal rod has α = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ K⁻¹. Find its increase in length for a 50 K rise.\nSolution\nFormula: ΔL = αL₀ΔT.\nΔL = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ × 2 × 50 = 0.0012 m = 1.2 mm.\n\nExample (vi)\nA rod is 1.5 m long and expands by 0.9 mm for a 40 °C rise. Find α.\nSolution\nFormula: α = ΔL/(L₀ΔT).\nConvert 0.9 mm = 0.0009 m.\nα = 0.0009/(1.5 × 40) = 1.5 × 10⁻⁵ °C⁻¹.\n\n5. LIQUID EXPANSION AND THE ANOMALOUS BEHAVIOUR OF WATER\nIf you warm a flask of coloured liquid fitted with a narrow tube, the liquid level generally rises. The liquid expands, but the glass container expands too, so the observed rise is not simply the liquid's entire expansion.\nDEFINITION: Real expansion of a liquid is its actual increase in volume on heating. Apparent expansion is the increase observed relative to the expanding container. For ordinary small expansions, real volume expansion = apparent expansion + expansion of the container.\nDEFINITION: The coefficient of volume expansion is the fractional change in volume per unit temperature change: ΔV = γV₀ΔT, where γ is in K⁻¹. Liquids usually expand more than solids for the same temperature rise.\nANOMALOUS EXPANSION OF WATER: Unlike most liquids, water contracts as it warms from 0 °C to about 4 °C, becoming denser. At about 4 °C its density is greatest. Above 4 °C, it expands normally on warming. On cooling from 4 °C towards 0 °C it expands, and ice is less dense than liquid water.\nWHY THIS MATTERS: In cold weather, water near 4 °C tends to sink because it is denser, while colder water remains near the surface and eventually freezes. Floating ice forms an insulating layer that can help deeper water remain liquid. Do not say water has its greatest density at 0 °C.\n\n\nExample (vii)\nWhy does a pond tend to freeze first at its surface?\nSolution\nWater near 4 °C is densest and sinks. Water cooled below 4 °C stays nearer the surface, where it can freeze. The ice then provides some insulation.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nHeat is energy, temperature is not. Use the ORIGINAL length in ΔL = αL₀ΔT. Convert mm to m when the other lengths are in metres. Kelvin temperatures have no degree sign. Do not confuse a 20 °C temperature with a 20 °C temperature rise. Water has maximum density near 4 °C, not 0 °C.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What is the SI unit of temperature? Answer: Kelvin (K).\n2. What causes heat to flow between two bodies? Answer: A temperature difference.\n3. How many kelvin is a rise of 15 °C? Answer: 15 K.\n4. Why are gaps left in bridges? Answer: To allow thermal expansion.\n5. At about what temperature is pure water densest? Answer: 4 °C.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nSketch a thermometer with 0 °C and 100 °C marks and locate an intermediate reading by proportional distance. Practise converting temperature values separately from temperature changes."},"old":{"title":"12. Temperature, Thermometers and Thermal Expansion","body":"New curriculum-first Physics lesson; verified year-by-year past-question mapping pending."}},{"id":"physics-heat-capacity-calorimetry","title":"13. Heat Capacity, Specific Heat Capacity and Calorimetry","body":"1. WHY DIFFERENT MATERIALS WARM AT DIFFERENT RATES\nPlace equal masses of water and cooking oil in separate containers and supply approximately the same heat. Their temperatures may rise by different amounts. Materials require different amounts of energy to achieve the same temperature rise. This is why a metal spoon heats up quickly while a large pot of water can take longer.\nDEFINITION: Heat capacity C is the heat energy required to raise the temperature of an ENTIRE body by 1 K (or 1 °C), without a change of state. C = Q/ΔT, in J/K.\nDEFINITION: Specific heat capacity c is the heat energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 K (or 1 °C), without a change of state. c = Q/(mΔT), in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nThus Q = mcΔT and C = mc. Heat capacity depends on the mass of the body; specific heat capacity is a property of the substance under the stated conditions. For water, a commonly used school value is 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nThe symbol ΔT means FINAL temperature minus INITIAL temperature. For cooling, the heat lost is often calculated using the positive magnitude of the temperature fall. A temperature DIFFERENCE of 20 °C equals 20 K; do not add 273 to a difference.\n\n2. HEATING CURVES AND ENERGY BALANCE\nOn a temperature–time graph for a substance heated at constant power, the slope tells us how quickly its temperature changes. A steeper temperature rise means a smaller total heat capacity when the power input is the same and heat losses are negligible. During a change of state, temperature can stay approximately constant while energy changes the arrangement of particles; that energy is latent heat, treated in the next lesson.\nDEFINITION: Calorimetry is the measurement of heat transferred during physical or chemical processes.\nIn an ideally insulated experiment, total heat lost by hotter bodies equals total heat gained by cooler bodies. This is the principle of heat exchange. In a real experiment, some heat is lost to the surroundings or absorbed by the container, thermometer and stirrer.\n\nExample (i)\nFind the heat needed to raise 2 kg of water from 20 °C to 50 °C. Take c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nSolution\nFormula: Q = mcΔT.\nΔT = 50 − 20 = 30 K.\nQ = 2 × 4,200 × 30 = 252,000 J.\n\nExample (ii)\nA 0.50 kg metal block absorbs 9,000 J and warms from 25 °C to 65 °C. Find its specific heat capacity.\nSolution\nFormula: c = Q/(mΔT).\nΔT = 65 − 25 = 40 K.\nc = 9,000/(0.50 × 40) = 450 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n\nExample (iii)\nA body requires 3,600 J for a 12 K rise. Find its heat capacity.\nSolution\nFormula: C = Q/ΔT.\nC = 3,600/12 = 300 J/K.\n\n3. THE METHOD OF MIXTURES\nImagine pouring hot water into cooler water in a well-insulated container. Energy moves from the hotter water to the cooler water until both reach a common final temperature. Neither water sample 'gives' temperature; it transfers heat energy.\nIf both samples are water and the container absorbs negligible heat:\nm_hot c(T_hot − T_final) = m_cold c(T_final − T_cold).\nThe c values cancel ONLY if both samples are the same substance. If the substances differ, retain their different specific heat capacities. If the calorimeter absorbs heat, add C_calorimeter(T_final − T_initial) to the heat-gained side.\nEXPERIMENT: Measure masses and starting temperatures. Mix quickly in an insulated vessel, stir gently, and record the highest or stable common temperature promptly. Covering the container reduces heat exchange with air.\n\nExample (iv)\nMix 0.20 kg of water at 80 °C with 0.30 kg of water at 20 °C in an insulated container. Find the final temperature.\nSolution\nFormula: m₁c(T₁ − T) = m₂c(T − T₂).\nSince both are water, cancel c:\n0.20(80 − T) = 0.30(T − 20).\n16 − 0.20T = 0.30T − 6.\n22 = 0.50T, so T = 44 °C.\n\nExample (v)\nA 0.10 kg hot metal at 100 °C is placed in 0.20 kg water at 20 °C. Final temperature is 25 °C. Find the metal's specific heat capacity. Take c_water = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹; ignore heat loss.\nSolution\nFormula: heat lost by metal = heat gained by water.\n0.10 × c_metal × (100 − 25) = 0.20 × 4,200 × (25 − 20).\n7.5c_metal = 4,200.\nc_metal = 560 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n\n4. ELECTRICAL HEATING AND EXPERIMENTAL ERRORS\nAn electric immersion heater converts electrical energy into thermal energy. If voltage V, current I and heating time t are known, electrical energy supplied is E = VIt. If the heater is ideal and all energy heats the sample, VIt = mcΔT. Real experiments may need to account for heat lost to air and heat absorbed by the container.\nEXPERIMENT: Weigh the water, measure its initial temperature, insert the heater and thermometer without letting them touch dangerously, measure V and I, heat for a measured time, stir, then record the temperature rise. Follow laboratory electrical safety procedures; do not operate an exposed heater outside its intended liquid.\nExample (vi)\nA 12 V heater draws 2 A for 5 minutes. Find the electrical energy supplied.\nSolution\nFormula: E = VIt.\nConvert 5 minutes = 300 s.\nE = 12 × 2 × 300 = 7,200 J.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 100 W heater supplies energy to 0.50 kg of water for 2 minutes. Assuming no heat loss, find the rise in temperature. Take c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nSolution\nFormulas: Q = Pt and ΔT = Q/(mc).\nQ = 100 × 120 = 12,000 J.\nΔT = 12,000/(0.50 × 4,200) ≈ 5.71 K.\n\n5. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nHeat capacity C (J/K) is not specific heat capacity c (J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹). Use mass in kg, not grams. A temperature change does not need a 273 conversion. In mixing problems the final temperature must lie between the initial temperatures when no other heat source or phase change is involved. Heat lost = heat gained only when losses and container effects are neglected or accounted for.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Define specific heat capacity. Answer: Energy needed to raise 1 kg of a substance by 1 K without change of state.\n2. What is the SI unit of heat capacity? Answer: J/K.\n3. Why does a larger mass of the same material need more heat for the same rise? Answer: Q = mcΔT.\n4. What is the principle of mixtures in an insulated system? Answer: Heat lost equals heat gained.\n5. Convert a rise of 25 °C to kelvin. Answer: 25 K.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nSketch a temperature–time graph for constant heating without phase change, identify its slope, and practise calculating Q, c, C and a final mixture temperature.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"13. Heat Capacity, Specific Heat Capacity and Calorimetry","body":"1. WHY DIFFERENT MATERIALS WARM AT DIFFERENT RATES\nPlace equal masses of water and cooking oil in separate containers and supply approximately the same heat. Their temperatures may rise by different amounts. Materials require different amounts of energy to achieve the same temperature rise. This is why a metal spoon heats up quickly while a large pot of water can take longer.\nDEFINITION: Heat capacity C is the heat energy required to raise the temperature of an ENTIRE body by 1 K (or 1 °C), without a change of state. C = Q/ΔT, in J/K.\nDEFINITION: Specific heat capacity c is the heat energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 K (or 1 °C), without a change of state. c = Q/(mΔT), in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nThus Q = mcΔT and C = mc. Heat capacity depends on the mass of the body; specific heat capacity is a property of the substance under the stated conditions. For water, a commonly used school value is 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nThe symbol ΔT means FINAL temperature minus INITIAL temperature. For cooling, the heat lost is often calculated using the positive magnitude of the temperature fall. A temperature DIFFERENCE of 20 °C equals 20 K; do not add 273 to a difference.\n\n2. HEATING CURVES AND ENERGY BALANCE\nOn a temperature–time graph for a substance heated at constant power, the slope tells us how quickly its temperature changes. A steeper temperature rise means a smaller total heat capacity when the power input is the same and heat losses are negligible. During a change of state, temperature can stay approximately constant while energy changes the arrangement of particles; that energy is latent heat, treated in the next lesson.\nDEFINITION: Calorimetry is the measurement of heat transferred during physical or chemical processes.\nIn an ideally insulated experiment, total heat lost by hotter bodies equals total heat gained by cooler bodies. This is the principle of heat exchange. In a real experiment, some heat is lost to the surroundings or absorbed by the container, thermometer and stirrer.\n\nExample (i)\nFind the heat needed to raise 2 kg of water from 20 °C to 50 °C. Take c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nSolution\nFormula: Q = mcΔT.\nΔT = 50 − 20 = 30 K.\nQ = 2 × 4,200 × 30 = 252,000 J.\n\nExample (ii)\nA 0.50 kg metal block absorbs 9,000 J and warms from 25 °C to 65 °C. Find its specific heat capacity.\nSolution\nFormula: c = Q/(mΔT).\nΔT = 65 − 25 = 40 K.\nc = 9,000/(0.50 × 40) = 450 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n\nExample (iii)\nA body requires 3,600 J for a 12 K rise. Find its heat capacity.\nSolution\nFormula: C = Q/ΔT.\nC = 3,600/12 = 300 J/K.\n\n3. THE METHOD OF MIXTURES\nImagine pouring hot water into cooler water in a well-insulated container. Energy moves from the hotter water to the cooler water until both reach a common final temperature. Neither water sample 'gives' temperature; it transfers heat energy.\nIf both samples are water and the container absorbs negligible heat:\nm_hot c(T_hot − T_final) = m_cold c(T_final − T_cold).\nThe c values cancel ONLY if both samples are the same substance. If the substances differ, retain their different specific heat capacities. If the calorimeter absorbs heat, add C_calorimeter(T_final − T_initial) to the heat-gained side.\nEXPERIMENT: Measure masses and starting temperatures. Mix quickly in an insulated vessel, stir gently, and record the highest or stable common temperature promptly. Covering the container reduces heat exchange with air.\n\nExample (iv)\nMix 0.20 kg of water at 80 °C with 0.30 kg of water at 20 °C in an insulated container. Find the final temperature.\nSolution\nFormula: m₁c(T₁ − T) = m₂c(T − T₂).\nSince both are water, cancel c:\n0.20(80 − T) = 0.30(T − 20).\n16 − 0.20T = 0.30T − 6.\n22 = 0.50T, so T = 44 °C.\n\nExample (v)\nA 0.10 kg hot metal at 100 °C is placed in 0.20 kg water at 20 °C. Final temperature is 25 °C. Find the metal's specific heat capacity. Take c_water = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹; ignore heat loss.\nSolution\nFormula: heat lost by metal = heat gained by water.\n0.10 × c_metal × (100 − 25) = 0.20 × 4,200 × (25 − 20).\n7.5c_metal = 4,200.\nc_metal = 560 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\n\n4. ELECTRICAL HEATING AND EXPERIMENTAL ERRORS\nAn electric immersion heater converts electrical energy into thermal energy. If voltage V, current I and heating time t are known, electrical energy supplied is E = VIt. If the heater is ideal and all energy heats the sample, VIt = mcΔT. Real experiments may need to account for heat lost to air and heat absorbed by the container.\nEXPERIMENT: Weigh the water, measure its initial temperature, insert the heater and thermometer without letting them touch dangerously, measure V and I, heat for a measured time, stir, then record the temperature rise. Follow laboratory electrical safety procedures; do not operate an exposed heater outside its intended liquid.\nExample (vi)\nA 12 V heater draws 2 A for 5 minutes. Find the electrical energy supplied.\nSolution\nFormula: E = VIt.\nConvert 5 minutes = 300 s.\nE = 12 × 2 × 300 = 7,200 J.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 100 W heater supplies energy to 0.50 kg of water for 2 minutes. Assuming no heat loss, find the rise in temperature. Take c = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nSolution\nFormulas: Q = Pt and ΔT = Q/(mc).\nQ = 100 × 120 = 12,000 J.\nΔT = 12,000/(0.50 × 4,200) ≈ 5.71 K.\n\n5. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nHeat capacity C (J/K) is not specific heat capacity c (J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹). Use mass in kg, not grams. A temperature change does not need a 273 conversion. In mixing problems the final temperature must lie between the initial temperatures when no other heat source or phase change is involved. Heat lost = heat gained only when losses and container effects are neglected or accounted for.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Define specific heat capacity. Answer: Energy needed to raise 1 kg of a substance by 1 K without change of state.\n2. What is the SI unit of heat capacity? Answer: J/K.\n3. Why does a larger mass of the same material need more heat for the same rise? Answer: Q = mcΔT.\n4. What is the principle of mixtures in an insulated system? Answer: Heat lost equals heat gained.\n5. Convert a rise of 25 °C to kelvin. Answer: 25 K.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nSketch a temperature–time graph for constant heating without phase change, identify its slope, and practise calculating Q, c, C and a final mixture temperature."},"old":{"title":"13. Heat Capacity, Specific Heat Capacity and Calorimetry","body":"This lesson has been developed in the revised Physics preview. Past-question mapping will follow when the Physics question bank is uploaded."}},{"id":"physics-change-of-state-latent-heat","title":"14. Changes of State, Latent Heat and Evaporation","body":"1. WHY ICE CAN MELT WITHOUT GETTING WARMER\nPlace crushed ice in a beaker and warm it gently. While ice is melting at its melting point under steady pressure, the temperature remains approximately constant even though heat is entering. Energy is being used to change the arrangement of particles, rather than to increase their average kinetic energy.\nDEFINITION: A change of state is a physical change between solid, liquid and gas without changing the substance's chemical identity. Melting: solid → liquid; freezing: liquid → solid; boiling/evaporation: liquid → gas; condensation: gas → liquid; sublimation: solid → gas; deposition: gas → solid.\nDEFINITION: Latent heat is energy absorbed or released during a change of state without a temperature change at the transition temperature.\nDEFINITION: Specific latent heat L is the energy needed to change the state of 1 kg of a substance without a temperature change, measured in J/kg. Q = mL.\nSpecific latent heat of fusion Lf refers to melting/freezing; specific latent heat of vaporisation Lv refers to boiling/condensation. The reverse changes release the same magnitude of energy under equivalent conditions.\n\n2. HEATING AND COOLING CURVES\nImagine steadily heating ice below 0 °C until it becomes steam above 100 °C at standard atmospheric pressure. The graph contains sloping parts (temperature changes within one state) and flat parts (changes of state at nearly constant temperature).\nOn sloping sections use Q = mcΔT, with the correct specific heat capacity for that state. On flat sections use Q = mL. If a question includes several stages, calculate each stage separately and add the energies. During condensation and freezing, energy is released rather than absorbed.\nThe melting and boiling points depend on pressure and purity. Water's familiar 0 °C and 100 °C values are approximate reference values at standard atmospheric pressure, not universal values in all conditions.\n\nExample (i)\nFind the energy required to melt 0.25 kg of ice at 0 °C. Take Lf = 3.34 × 10⁵ J/kg.\nSolution\nFormula: Q = mLf.\nQ = 0.25 × 3.34 × 10⁵ = 83,500 J.\n\nExample (ii)\nCalculate the heat needed to vaporise 0.10 kg of water at its boiling point. Take Lv = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg.\nSolution\nFormula: Q = mLv.\nQ = 0.10 × 2.26 × 10⁶ = 226,000 J.\n\nExample (iii)\nHow much energy is released when 0.50 kg of steam condenses at its boiling point? Take Lv = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg.\nSolution\nFormula: Q = mLv (magnitude).\nQ = 0.50 × 2.26 × 10⁶ = 1.13 × 10⁶ J released.\n\n3. MULTISTAGE HEATING: SENSIBLE HEAT AND LATENT HEAT\nHeating ice from −10 °C to liquid water at 20 °C requires three distinct stages: first warm the ice to 0 °C, then melt it at 0 °C, then warm the liquid water to 20 °C. Do not apply Q = mcΔT across the melting stage because temperature is not rising there.\nExample (iv)\nCalculate the heat required to convert 0.20 kg of ice at −10 °C to water at 20 °C. Take c_ice = 2,100 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, Lf = 3.34 × 10⁵ J/kg and c_water = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nSolution\nStage 1 formula: Q₁ = mc_iceΔT = 0.20 × 2,100 × 10 = 4,200 J.\nStage 2 formula: Q₂ = mLf = 0.20 × 334,000 = 66,800 J.\nStage 3 formula: Q₃ = mc_waterΔT = 0.20 × 4,200 × 20 = 16,800 J.\nTotal Q = Q₁ + Q₂ + Q₃ = 87,800 J.\n\nExample (v)\nA heater supplies 150 W for 8 minutes to ice already at its melting point. Assuming all the energy melts ice, find the mass melted. Take Lf = 3.0 × 10⁵ J/kg.\nSolution\nFormulas: Q = Pt; m = Q/Lf.\nQ = 150 × (8 × 60) = 72,000 J.\nm = 72,000/300,000 = 0.24 kg.\n\n4. EVAPORATION VERSUS BOILING\nWet clothes can dry without the water reaching 100 °C. Some fast-moving molecules escape from the surface of a liquid at ordinary temperatures.\nDEFINITION: Evaporation is the escape of molecules from a liquid's SURFACE into the gaseous state at temperatures below its boiling point as well as at other temperatures. It is generally faster when temperature and exposed surface area increase, when air moves faster over the surface, and when surrounding humidity is lower.\nDEFINITION: Boiling is rapid vaporisation throughout a liquid, with bubbles forming when its vapour pressure matches the surrounding pressure. At a fixed external pressure, a pure liquid boils at a characteristic temperature.\nEvaporation often cools the remaining liquid because faster molecules preferentially escape, lowering its average kinetic energy unless energy is supplied. Sweating cools the skin; a porous water pot can cool water through evaporation.\nIncreasing atmospheric pressure generally raises the boiling point; reducing pressure lowers it. A pressure cooker uses higher pressure to cook at higher temperatures.\n\nExample (vi)\nWhy does rubbing alcohol feel cool on the skin?\nSolution\nIt evaporates readily. The escaping molecules take energy from the liquid and surrounding skin, producing cooling.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 1.5 kW heater is used to boil water already at 100 °C for 10 minutes. If 60% of the electrical energy vaporises water, find the mass vaporised. Take Lv = 2.25 × 10⁶ J/kg.\nSolution\nFormula: useful energy = efficiency × Pt; m = useful energy/Lv.\nUseful energy = 0.60 × 1,500 × 600 = 540,000 J.\nm = 540,000/(2.25 × 10⁶) = 0.24 kg.\n\n5. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nDo not confuse evaporation (surface, at many temperatures) with boiling (bubbles throughout at boiling point). Use Q = mL only during a phase change and Q = mcΔT during a temperature rise or fall. A flat heating-curve section does not mean no energy is entering. Steam can cause severe burns because it releases latent heat on condensation.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Define specific latent heat. Answer: Energy to change the state of 1 kg without temperature change.\n2. Which change releases latent heat: condensation or evaporation? Answer: Condensation.\n3. Does evaporation require boiling? Answer: No.\n4. What formula applies while ice melts at 0 °C? Answer: Q = mLf.\n5. Why does a wet cloth cool as it dries? Answer: Evaporation removes energy.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw and label a heating curve for ice → water → steam. Write Q = mcΔT on each sloping segment and Q = mL on each plateau; then solve a multistage heating question.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"14. Changes of State, Latent Heat and Evaporation","body":"1. WHY ICE CAN MELT WITHOUT GETTING WARMER\nPlace crushed ice in a beaker and warm it gently. While ice is melting at its melting point under steady pressure, the temperature remains approximately constant even though heat is entering. Energy is being used to change the arrangement of particles, rather than to increase their average kinetic energy.\nDEFINITION: A change of state is a physical change between solid, liquid and gas without changing the substance's chemical identity. Melting: solid → liquid; freezing: liquid → solid; boiling/evaporation: liquid → gas; condensation: gas → liquid; sublimation: solid → gas; deposition: gas → solid.\nDEFINITION: Latent heat is energy absorbed or released during a change of state without a temperature change at the transition temperature.\nDEFINITION: Specific latent heat L is the energy needed to change the state of 1 kg of a substance without a temperature change, measured in J/kg. Q = mL.\nSpecific latent heat of fusion Lf refers to melting/freezing; specific latent heat of vaporisation Lv refers to boiling/condensation. The reverse changes release the same magnitude of energy under equivalent conditions.\n\n2. HEATING AND COOLING CURVES\nImagine steadily heating ice below 0 °C until it becomes steam above 100 °C at standard atmospheric pressure. The graph contains sloping parts (temperature changes within one state) and flat parts (changes of state at nearly constant temperature).\nOn sloping sections use Q = mcΔT, with the correct specific heat capacity for that state. On flat sections use Q = mL. If a question includes several stages, calculate each stage separately and add the energies. During condensation and freezing, energy is released rather than absorbed.\nThe melting and boiling points depend on pressure and purity. Water's familiar 0 °C and 100 °C values are approximate reference values at standard atmospheric pressure, not universal values in all conditions.\n\nExample (i)\nFind the energy required to melt 0.25 kg of ice at 0 °C. Take Lf = 3.34 × 10⁵ J/kg.\nSolution\nFormula: Q = mLf.\nQ = 0.25 × 3.34 × 10⁵ = 83,500 J.\n\nExample (ii)\nCalculate the heat needed to vaporise 0.10 kg of water at its boiling point. Take Lv = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg.\nSolution\nFormula: Q = mLv.\nQ = 0.10 × 2.26 × 10⁶ = 226,000 J.\n\nExample (iii)\nHow much energy is released when 0.50 kg of steam condenses at its boiling point? Take Lv = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg.\nSolution\nFormula: Q = mLv (magnitude).\nQ = 0.50 × 2.26 × 10⁶ = 1.13 × 10⁶ J released.\n\n3. MULTISTAGE HEATING: SENSIBLE HEAT AND LATENT HEAT\nHeating ice from −10 °C to liquid water at 20 °C requires three distinct stages: first warm the ice to 0 °C, then melt it at 0 °C, then warm the liquid water to 20 °C. Do not apply Q = mcΔT across the melting stage because temperature is not rising there.\nExample (iv)\nCalculate the heat required to convert 0.20 kg of ice at −10 °C to water at 20 °C. Take c_ice = 2,100 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, Lf = 3.34 × 10⁵ J/kg and c_water = 4,200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nSolution\nStage 1 formula: Q₁ = mc_iceΔT = 0.20 × 2,100 × 10 = 4,200 J.\nStage 2 formula: Q₂ = mLf = 0.20 × 334,000 = 66,800 J.\nStage 3 formula: Q₃ = mc_waterΔT = 0.20 × 4,200 × 20 = 16,800 J.\nTotal Q = Q₁ + Q₂ + Q₃ = 87,800 J.\n\nExample (v)\nA heater supplies 150 W for 8 minutes to ice already at its melting point. Assuming all the energy melts ice, find the mass melted. Take Lf = 3.0 × 10⁵ J/kg.\nSolution\nFormulas: Q = Pt; m = Q/Lf.\nQ = 150 × (8 × 60) = 72,000 J.\nm = 72,000/300,000 = 0.24 kg.\n\n4. EVAPORATION VERSUS BOILING\nWet clothes can dry without the water reaching 100 °C. Some fast-moving molecules escape from the surface of a liquid at ordinary temperatures.\nDEFINITION: Evaporation is the escape of molecules from a liquid's SURFACE into the gaseous state at temperatures below its boiling point as well as at other temperatures. It is generally faster when temperature and exposed surface area increase, when air moves faster over the surface, and when surrounding humidity is lower.\nDEFINITION: Boiling is rapid vaporisation throughout a liquid, with bubbles forming when its vapour pressure matches the surrounding pressure. At a fixed external pressure, a pure liquid boils at a characteristic temperature.\nEvaporation often cools the remaining liquid because faster molecules preferentially escape, lowering its average kinetic energy unless energy is supplied. Sweating cools the skin; a porous water pot can cool water through evaporation.\nIncreasing atmospheric pressure generally raises the boiling point; reducing pressure lowers it. A pressure cooker uses higher pressure to cook at higher temperatures.\n\nExample (vi)\nWhy does rubbing alcohol feel cool on the skin?\nSolution\nIt evaporates readily. The escaping molecules take energy from the liquid and surrounding skin, producing cooling.\n\nExample (vii)\nA 1.5 kW heater is used to boil water already at 100 °C for 10 minutes. If 60% of the electrical energy vaporises water, find the mass vaporised. Take Lv = 2.25 × 10⁶ J/kg.\nSolution\nFormula: useful energy = efficiency × Pt; m = useful energy/Lv.\nUseful energy = 0.60 × 1,500 × 600 = 540,000 J.\nm = 540,000/(2.25 × 10⁶) = 0.24 kg.\n\n5. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nDo not confuse evaporation (surface, at many temperatures) with boiling (bubbles throughout at boiling point). Use Q = mL only during a phase change and Q = mcΔT during a temperature rise or fall. A flat heating-curve section does not mean no energy is entering. Steam can cause severe burns because it releases latent heat on condensation.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Define specific latent heat. Answer: Energy to change the state of 1 kg without temperature change.\n2. Which change releases latent heat: condensation or evaporation? Answer: Condensation.\n3. Does evaporation require boiling? Answer: No.\n4. What formula applies while ice melts at 0 °C? Answer: Q = mLf.\n5. Why does a wet cloth cool as it dries? Answer: Evaporation removes energy.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw and label a heating curve for ice → water → steam. Write Q = mcΔT on each sloping segment and Q = mL on each plateau; then solve a multistage heating question."},"old":{"title":"14. Changes of State, Latent Heat and Evaporation","body":"This lesson has been developed in the revised Physics preview. Past-question mapping will follow when the Physics question bank is uploaded."}},{"id":"physics-heat-transfer","title":"15. Heat Transfer: Conduction, Convection and Radiation","body":"1. THREE WAYS HEAT MOVES\nHold one end of a metal spoon in warm water and the handle may soon become hot. Watch warm water circulate in a pot. Feel sunlight on your skin even though space is nearly a vacuum. These are three different ways energy is transferred because of a temperature difference.\nDEFINITION: Heat transfer is the movement of thermal energy from a hotter region to a cooler region. It occurs by conduction, convection or radiation, sometimes simultaneously. Heat is energy in transit, not a substance stored inside a body.\n\n2. CONDUCTION: TRANSFER WITHOUT BULK MOVEMENT\nA metal rod becomes hot along its length even though the rod does not flow from one place to another.\nDEFINITION: Conduction is the transfer of thermal energy through a material or between bodies in contact without bulk movement of the material. In solids, vibrating particles transfer energy to neighbours; in metals, mobile electrons also contribute strongly.\nMetals such as copper and aluminium are usually good conductors. Wood, plastics and trapped air are generally poorer conductors. Poor conductors are called thermal insulators; they reduce the RATE of heat transfer but do not stop it completely.\nFor a uniform slab in steady conditions, heat-transfer rate is:\nQ/t = kA(T_hot − T_cold)/L.\nHere k = thermal conductivity (W m⁻¹ K⁻¹), A = area (m²), L = thickness (m). Greater area and temperature difference increase the rate; greater thickness reduces it. This relation assumes steady conduction and a constant k.\nAPPLICATIONS: metal cooking pots transfer heat to food; plastic handles reduce heat flow to hands; insulated coolers use poor conductors.\n\nExample (i)\nA wall of area 4 m² and thickness 0.20 m has conductivity 0.50 W m⁻¹ K⁻¹. Its faces differ by 10 K. Find the steady heat-transfer rate.\nSolution\nFormula: Q/t = kAΔT/L.\nQ/t = 0.50 × 4 × 10 / 0.20 = 100 W.\n\nExample (ii)\nIf a slab's thickness doubles while all other quantities remain unchanged, what happens to its steady conduction rate?\nSolution\nFormula: Q/t = kAΔT/L.\nRate is inversely proportional to thickness. Doubling L halves the rate.\n\n3. CONVECTION: TRANSFER BY MOVING FLUID\nWhen water at the bottom of a pot is heated, it generally expands, becomes less dense and rises. Cooler, denser water sinks to replace it, producing circulation.\nDEFINITION: Convection is heat transfer by bulk movement of a fluid (liquid or gas). The circulating movement is a convection current.\nNATURAL CONVECTION arises from density differences under gravity. FORCED CONVECTION occurs when a fan or pump moves the fluid. Convection cannot occur in a solid because the material does not flow as a bulk fluid.\nSEA BREEZE: During the day, land heats faster than the sea. Air over land warms and rises; cooler air from the sea moves inland near the surface. LAND BREEZE: At night, land cools faster, and near-surface air can move from land towards the warmer sea. Real weather also depends on other factors.\nAPPLICATIONS: ventilation openings high on walls allow warm air to escape; chimneys promote upward movement of warm gases; room heaters warm surrounding air.\n\nExample (iii)\nWhy are ventilation openings often placed near a ceiling?\nSolution\nWarm air tends to become less dense and rise. High vents allow it to escape, supporting convection and replacement by cooler air.\n\nExample (iv)\nWhy does the sea breeze usually blow from sea to land during the day?\nSolution\nLand warms more quickly, so air above it rises. Cooler, denser air from over the sea flows towards land to replace it.\n\n4. RADIATION: TRANSFER WITHOUT A MEDIUM\nThe Sun heats Earth across space. This cannot be explained by conduction or convection across the near-vacuum of space.\nDEFINITION: Thermal radiation is the transfer of energy by electromagnetic waves emitted by matter because of its temperature. It does not require a material medium.\nAll objects above absolute zero emit thermal radiation. Hotter objects generally emit more radiation. Dull black surfaces are usually better absorbers and emitters than shiny polished surfaces under comparable conditions. A shiny surface reflects much incident radiation and can reduce radiative heat transfer.\nFor an idealised surface, radiated power follows P = εσAT⁴, where ε is emissivity (0 to 1), σ is the Stefan–Boltzmann constant, A is area, and T is ABSOLUTE temperature in kelvin. Net radiation to surroundings at temperature Ts is P_net = εσA(T⁴ − Ts⁴), under the usual simplifying assumptions.\nA vacuum flask reduces heat transfer through several mechanisms: vacuum between walls limits conduction and convection; reflective surfaces reduce radiation; an insulating stopper limits transfer through the neck.\n\nExample (v)\nTwo identical objects have the same area and emissivity. One is at 600 K and the other at 300 K. Compare their total emitted radiant powers, ignoring surroundings.\nSolution\nFormula: P ∝ T⁴.\nP₆₀₀/P₃₀₀ = (600/300)⁴ = 2⁴ = 16.\nThe hotter object emits 16 times as much power under these assumptions.\n\nExample (vi)\nWhy does a polished silvered surface help a vacuum flask retain heat?\nSolution\nIt reflects much thermal radiation and is a relatively poor emitter and absorber, reducing radiative exchange.\n\n5. COMBINED HEAT TRANSFER AND PRACTICAL CHOICES\nA hot cooking pot conducts heat through its metal base, convection moves hot water inside it, and radiation transfers energy between the pot and surroundings. These mechanisms often act together. Insulation slows heat flow by reducing one or more mechanisms.\nExample (vii)\nWhy is a woollen blanket useful on a cold night?\nSolution\nIts fibres trap pockets of air, reducing conduction and suppressing large-scale air circulation. It does not create heat; it reduces the body's heat loss.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nConvection occurs in fluids, not solids. Radiation can cross a vacuum; conduction and convection cannot cross a perfect vacuum. A black surface is generally a good emitter AND absorber. In radiation formulas T must be in kelvin. A vacuum flask is not perfectly insulating; heat can still pass through the stopper and by radiation.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Name the heat-transfer method that works through vacuum. Answer: Radiation.\n2. Can convection occur in a rigid solid? Answer: No.\n3. Why do metals conduct heat well? Answer: Particle interactions and mobile electrons transfer energy.\n4. What happens to steady conduction rate if thickness doubles? Answer: It halves, all else equal.\n5. Why are vacuum flask walls silvered? Answer: To reduce radiative heat transfer.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw a pot heating water and label conduction through the base, convection in the water and radiation from hot surfaces. Sketch a vacuum flask and explain each insulating feature.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"15. Heat Transfer: Conduction, Convection and Radiation","body":"1. THREE WAYS HEAT MOVES\nHold one end of a metal spoon in warm water and the handle may soon become hot. Watch warm water circulate in a pot. Feel sunlight on your skin even though space is nearly a vacuum. These are three different ways energy is transferred because of a temperature difference.\nDEFINITION: Heat transfer is the movement of thermal energy from a hotter region to a cooler region. It occurs by conduction, convection or radiation, sometimes simultaneously. Heat is energy in transit, not a substance stored inside a body.\n\n2. CONDUCTION: TRANSFER WITHOUT BULK MOVEMENT\nA metal rod becomes hot along its length even though the rod does not flow from one place to another.\nDEFINITION: Conduction is the transfer of thermal energy through a material or between bodies in contact without bulk movement of the material. In solids, vibrating particles transfer energy to neighbours; in metals, mobile electrons also contribute strongly.\nMetals such as copper and aluminium are usually good conductors. Wood, plastics and trapped air are generally poorer conductors. Poor conductors are called thermal insulators; they reduce the RATE of heat transfer but do not stop it completely.\nFor a uniform slab in steady conditions, heat-transfer rate is:\nQ/t = kA(T_hot − T_cold)/L.\nHere k = thermal conductivity (W m⁻¹ K⁻¹), A = area (m²), L = thickness (m). Greater area and temperature difference increase the rate; greater thickness reduces it. This relation assumes steady conduction and a constant k.\nAPPLICATIONS: metal cooking pots transfer heat to food; plastic handles reduce heat flow to hands; insulated coolers use poor conductors.\n\nExample (i)\nA wall of area 4 m² and thickness 0.20 m has conductivity 0.50 W m⁻¹ K⁻¹. Its faces differ by 10 K. Find the steady heat-transfer rate.\nSolution\nFormula: Q/t = kAΔT/L.\nQ/t = 0.50 × 4 × 10 / 0.20 = 100 W.\n\nExample (ii)\nIf a slab's thickness doubles while all other quantities remain unchanged, what happens to its steady conduction rate?\nSolution\nFormula: Q/t = kAΔT/L.\nRate is inversely proportional to thickness. Doubling L halves the rate.\n\n3. CONVECTION: TRANSFER BY MOVING FLUID\nWhen water at the bottom of a pot is heated, it generally expands, becomes less dense and rises. Cooler, denser water sinks to replace it, producing circulation.\nDEFINITION: Convection is heat transfer by bulk movement of a fluid (liquid or gas). The circulating movement is a convection current.\nNATURAL CONVECTION arises from density differences under gravity. FORCED CONVECTION occurs when a fan or pump moves the fluid. Convection cannot occur in a solid because the material does not flow as a bulk fluid.\nSEA BREEZE: During the day, land heats faster than the sea. Air over land warms and rises; cooler air from the sea moves inland near the surface. LAND BREEZE: At night, land cools faster, and near-surface air can move from land towards the warmer sea. Real weather also depends on other factors.\nAPPLICATIONS: ventilation openings high on walls allow warm air to escape; chimneys promote upward movement of warm gases; room heaters warm surrounding air.\n\nExample (iii)\nWhy are ventilation openings often placed near a ceiling?\nSolution\nWarm air tends to become less dense and rise. High vents allow it to escape, supporting convection and replacement by cooler air.\n\nExample (iv)\nWhy does the sea breeze usually blow from sea to land during the day?\nSolution\nLand warms more quickly, so air above it rises. Cooler, denser air from over the sea flows towards land to replace it.\n\n4. RADIATION: TRANSFER WITHOUT A MEDIUM\nThe Sun heats Earth across space. This cannot be explained by conduction or convection across the near-vacuum of space.\nDEFINITION: Thermal radiation is the transfer of energy by electromagnetic waves emitted by matter because of its temperature. It does not require a material medium.\nAll objects above absolute zero emit thermal radiation. Hotter objects generally emit more radiation. Dull black surfaces are usually better absorbers and emitters than shiny polished surfaces under comparable conditions. A shiny surface reflects much incident radiation and can reduce radiative heat transfer.\nFor an idealised surface, radiated power follows P = εσAT⁴, where ε is emissivity (0 to 1), σ is the Stefan–Boltzmann constant, A is area, and T is ABSOLUTE temperature in kelvin. Net radiation to surroundings at temperature Ts is P_net = εσA(T⁴ − Ts⁴), under the usual simplifying assumptions.\nA vacuum flask reduces heat transfer through several mechanisms: vacuum between walls limits conduction and convection; reflective surfaces reduce radiation; an insulating stopper limits transfer through the neck.\n\nExample (v)\nTwo identical objects have the same area and emissivity. One is at 600 K and the other at 300 K. Compare their total emitted radiant powers, ignoring surroundings.\nSolution\nFormula: P ∝ T⁴.\nP₆₀₀/P₃₀₀ = (600/300)⁴ = 2⁴ = 16.\nThe hotter object emits 16 times as much power under these assumptions.\n\nExample (vi)\nWhy does a polished silvered surface help a vacuum flask retain heat?\nSolution\nIt reflects much thermal radiation and is a relatively poor emitter and absorber, reducing radiative exchange.\n\n5. COMBINED HEAT TRANSFER AND PRACTICAL CHOICES\nA hot cooking pot conducts heat through its metal base, convection moves hot water inside it, and radiation transfers energy between the pot and surroundings. These mechanisms often act together. Insulation slows heat flow by reducing one or more mechanisms.\nExample (vii)\nWhy is a woollen blanket useful on a cold night?\nSolution\nIts fibres trap pockets of air, reducing conduction and suppressing large-scale air circulation. It does not create heat; it reduces the body's heat loss.\n\n6. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nConvection occurs in fluids, not solids. Radiation can cross a vacuum; conduction and convection cannot cross a perfect vacuum. A black surface is generally a good emitter AND absorber. In radiation formulas T must be in kelvin. A vacuum flask is not perfectly insulating; heat can still pass through the stopper and by radiation.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. Name the heat-transfer method that works through vacuum. Answer: Radiation.\n2. Can convection occur in a rigid solid? Answer: No.\n3. Why do metals conduct heat well? Answer: Particle interactions and mobile electrons transfer energy.\n4. What happens to steady conduction rate if thickness doubles? Answer: It halves, all else equal.\n5. Why are vacuum flask walls silvered? Answer: To reduce radiative heat transfer.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nDraw a pot heating water and label conduction through the base, convection in the water and radiation from hot surfaces. Sketch a vacuum flask and explain each insulating feature."},"old":{"title":"15. Heat Transfer: Conduction, Convection and Radiation","body":"This lesson has been developed in the revised Physics preview. Past-question mapping will follow when the Physics question bank is uploaded."}},{"id":"physics-gas-laws-kinetic-theory","title":"16. Gas Laws and the Kinetic Theory of Matter","body":"1. WHY A GAS PUSHES ON ITS CONTAINER\nPump air into a bicycle tyre. The tyre becomes firmer because gas molecules move randomly and collide with its inner walls, exerting pressure. Heating a sealed gas container often raises its pressure because the molecules strike its walls more vigorously and frequently.\nDEFINITION: The kinetic theory of gases models a gas as many tiny particles moving randomly and continuously, with pressure arising from collisions with container walls. In the ideal-gas model, particles have negligible volume compared with the container and intermolecular forces are ignored except during collisions.\nA gas has no fixed shape or volume: it fills its container. A liquid has nearly fixed volume but takes the container's shape. A solid has relatively fixed shape and volume. Temperature is related to average molecular kinetic energy; gas-law calculations must use ABSOLUTE temperature in kelvin.\n\n2. BOYLE'S LAW: PRESSURE AND VOLUME\nBlock the outlet of a syringe and gently push its plunger. The trapped air becomes harder to compress. At approximately constant temperature, a smaller volume gives greater pressure.\nDEFINITION: Boyle's law states that the pressure of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume at constant temperature.\nP ∝ 1/V, so PV = constant and P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.\nThe graph of pressure against volume is a decreasing curve (hyperbola), not a straight line. A graph of pressure against 1/V is a straight line through the origin under ideal conditions. Use absolute pressure, not gauge pressure, in gas laws.\n\nExample (i)\nA gas occupies 300 cm³ at 100 kPa. Find its volume at 150 kPa if temperature stays constant.\nSolution\nFormula: P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.\nV₂ = (100 × 300)/150 = 200 cm³.\n\nExample (ii)\nA gas has volume 2.0 L at 80 kPa. It is compressed to 0.50 L at constant temperature. Find its final pressure.\nSolution\nFormula: P₂ = P₁V₁/V₂.\nP₂ = 80 × 2.0/0.50 = 320 kPa.\n\n3. CHARLES'S LAW: VOLUME AND TEMPERATURE\nImagine a flexible balloon warmed gently in a safe environment. If external pressure stays approximately constant, the gas can expand as its absolute temperature rises.\nDEFINITION: Charles's law states that the volume of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature at constant pressure.\nV ∝ T, so V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂, with T in K. A graph of V against T in kelvin is a straight line through the origin in the ideal-gas model. In Celsius, extrapolating the line reaches zero volume near −273.15 °C, but real gases condense before reaching that point.\n\nExample (iii)\nA gas occupies 250 cm³ at 27 °C. Find its volume at 87 °C at constant pressure. Use 273 for conversion.\nSolution\nFormula: V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂.\nT₁ = 27 + 273 = 300 K; T₂ = 87 + 273 = 360 K.\nV₂ = 250 × 360/300 = 300 cm³.\n\n4. PRESSURE LAW: PRESSURE AND TEMPERATURE\nA sealed rigid container cannot expand appreciably. Heating the gas inside therefore increases its pressure.\nDEFINITION: The pressure law (Gay-Lussac's law) states that the pressure of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature at constant volume.\nP ∝ T, so P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂.\nDo not heat sealed containers as an experiment without proper laboratory safety arrangements.\n\nExample (iv)\nA rigid vessel contains gas at 120 kPa and 20 °C. Find its pressure at 80 °C. Use 273 for conversion.\nSolution\nFormula: P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂.\nT₁ = 293 K; T₂ = 353 K.\nP₂ = 120 × 353/293 ≈ 144.6 kPa.\n\n5. COMBINED GAS LAW AND IDEAL-GAS EQUATION\nIf pressure, volume and temperature all change for a fixed mass of gas, combine the simple laws:\nP₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂.\nFor an ideal gas containing n moles, PV = nRT, where R ≈ 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ when P is in Pa and V in m³. The amount of gas must remain constant for the combined-gas-law form.\nA useful problem-solving method: identify which quantities are constant, convert temperatures to kelvin, write the correct law, substitute matching initial and final values, then check whether the answer makes physical sense.\n\nExample (v)\nA gas occupies 2.0 L at 100 kPa and 300 K. Find its volume at 150 kPa and 450 K.\nSolution\nFormula: P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂.\nV₂ = (P₁V₁T₂)/(P₂T₁) = (100 × 2.0 × 450)/(150 × 300) = 2.0 L.\n\nExample (vi)\nFind the number of moles in a gas at pressure 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, volume 0.0249 m³ and temperature 300 K. Take R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nSolution\nFormula: n = PV/(RT).\nn = (100,000 × 0.0249)/(8.31 × 300) ≈ 0.999 ≈ 1.00 mol.\n\n6. GAS MOLECULES, DIFFUSION AND LIMITATIONS\nOpen a bottle of perfume and its smell spreads through the room. This illustrates diffusion: the net spreading of particles from a region of higher concentration towards lower concentration because of random molecular motion. Gases diffuse relatively quickly because their particles move freely and mix. Diffusion is not the same as a bulk wind or convection current, though those can speed mixing.\nDEFINITION: Brownian motion is the irregular movement of tiny particles suspended in a fluid, caused by uneven collisions with surrounding molecules. It is evidence of molecular motion.\nIdeal-gas laws are approximations. Real gases deviate more at high pressures and low temperatures, when particle volume and attractions become important. Gas laws do not justify claims that a real gas can reach zero volume.\n\nExample (vii)\nWhy does the smell of perfume spread across a room even when there is no strong breeze?\nSolution\nPerfume vapour molecules move randomly and diffuse from regions of higher concentration to lower concentration. Air movement may also assist spreading.\n\n7. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nConvert Celsius to kelvin BEFORE applying Charles's law, the pressure law or combined gas law. Boyle's law requires constant temperature, Charles's law constant pressure, and the pressure law constant volume. Use ABSOLUTE gas pressure. Keep volume units consistent on both sides; for PV = nRT with R = 8.31, use Pa and m³.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What causes gas pressure? Answer: Collisions of moving molecules with the container walls.\n2. State Boyle's law. Answer: At constant temperature, P is inversely proportional to V for a fixed mass.\n3. What is 27 °C in kelvin using 273? Answer: 300 K.\n4. Which law applies at constant volume? Answer: Pressure law.\n5. Why must gas-law temperatures be in kelvin? Answer: The laws relate to absolute temperature.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nSketch P against V, V against absolute T, and P against absolute T with correctly labelled axes. Solve one example under each constant condition and one combined-law example.","revised":true,"new":{"title":"16. Gas Laws and the Kinetic Theory of Matter","body":"1. WHY A GAS PUSHES ON ITS CONTAINER\nPump air into a bicycle tyre. The tyre becomes firmer because gas molecules move randomly and collide with its inner walls, exerting pressure. Heating a sealed gas container often raises its pressure because the molecules strike its walls more vigorously and frequently.\nDEFINITION: The kinetic theory of gases models a gas as many tiny particles moving randomly and continuously, with pressure arising from collisions with container walls. In the ideal-gas model, particles have negligible volume compared with the container and intermolecular forces are ignored except during collisions.\nA gas has no fixed shape or volume: it fills its container. A liquid has nearly fixed volume but takes the container's shape. A solid has relatively fixed shape and volume. Temperature is related to average molecular kinetic energy; gas-law calculations must use ABSOLUTE temperature in kelvin.\n\n2. BOYLE'S LAW: PRESSURE AND VOLUME\nBlock the outlet of a syringe and gently push its plunger. The trapped air becomes harder to compress. At approximately constant temperature, a smaller volume gives greater pressure.\nDEFINITION: Boyle's law states that the pressure of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to its volume at constant temperature.\nP ∝ 1/V, so PV = constant and P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.\nThe graph of pressure against volume is a decreasing curve (hyperbola), not a straight line. A graph of pressure against 1/V is a straight line through the origin under ideal conditions. Use absolute pressure, not gauge pressure, in gas laws.\n\nExample (i)\nA gas occupies 300 cm³ at 100 kPa. Find its volume at 150 kPa if temperature stays constant.\nSolution\nFormula: P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.\nV₂ = (100 × 300)/150 = 200 cm³.\n\nExample (ii)\nA gas has volume 2.0 L at 80 kPa. It is compressed to 0.50 L at constant temperature. Find its final pressure.\nSolution\nFormula: P₂ = P₁V₁/V₂.\nP₂ = 80 × 2.0/0.50 = 320 kPa.\n\n3. CHARLES'S LAW: VOLUME AND TEMPERATURE\nImagine a flexible balloon warmed gently in a safe environment. If external pressure stays approximately constant, the gas can expand as its absolute temperature rises.\nDEFINITION: Charles's law states that the volume of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature at constant pressure.\nV ∝ T, so V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂, with T in K. A graph of V against T in kelvin is a straight line through the origin in the ideal-gas model. In Celsius, extrapolating the line reaches zero volume near −273.15 °C, but real gases condense before reaching that point.\n\nExample (iii)\nA gas occupies 250 cm³ at 27 °C. Find its volume at 87 °C at constant pressure. Use 273 for conversion.\nSolution\nFormula: V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂.\nT₁ = 27 + 273 = 300 K; T₂ = 87 + 273 = 360 K.\nV₂ = 250 × 360/300 = 300 cm³.\n\n4. PRESSURE LAW: PRESSURE AND TEMPERATURE\nA sealed rigid container cannot expand appreciably. Heating the gas inside therefore increases its pressure.\nDEFINITION: The pressure law (Gay-Lussac's law) states that the pressure of a fixed mass of gas is directly proportional to its absolute temperature at constant volume.\nP ∝ T, so P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂.\nDo not heat sealed containers as an experiment without proper laboratory safety arrangements.\n\nExample (iv)\nA rigid vessel contains gas at 120 kPa and 20 °C. Find its pressure at 80 °C. Use 273 for conversion.\nSolution\nFormula: P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂.\nT₁ = 293 K; T₂ = 353 K.\nP₂ = 120 × 353/293 ≈ 144.6 kPa.\n\n5. COMBINED GAS LAW AND IDEAL-GAS EQUATION\nIf pressure, volume and temperature all change for a fixed mass of gas, combine the simple laws:\nP₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂.\nFor an ideal gas containing n moles, PV = nRT, where R ≈ 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ when P is in Pa and V in m³. The amount of gas must remain constant for the combined-gas-law form.\nA useful problem-solving method: identify which quantities are constant, convert temperatures to kelvin, write the correct law, substitute matching initial and final values, then check whether the answer makes physical sense.\n\nExample (v)\nA gas occupies 2.0 L at 100 kPa and 300 K. Find its volume at 150 kPa and 450 K.\nSolution\nFormula: P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂.\nV₂ = (P₁V₁T₂)/(P₂T₁) = (100 × 2.0 × 450)/(150 × 300) = 2.0 L.\n\nExample (vi)\nFind the number of moles in a gas at pressure 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, volume 0.0249 m³ and temperature 300 K. Take R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.\nSolution\nFormula: n = PV/(RT).\nn = (100,000 × 0.0249)/(8.31 × 300) ≈ 0.999 ≈ 1.00 mol.\n\n6. GAS MOLECULES, DIFFUSION AND LIMITATIONS\nOpen a bottle of perfume and its smell spreads through the room. This illustrates diffusion: the net spreading of particles from a region of higher concentration towards lower concentration because of random molecular motion. Gases diffuse relatively quickly because their particles move freely and mix. Diffusion is not the same as a bulk wind or convection current, though those can speed mixing.\nDEFINITION: Brownian motion is the irregular movement of tiny particles suspended in a fluid, caused by uneven collisions with surrounding molecules. It is evidence of molecular motion.\nIdeal-gas laws are approximations. Real gases deviate more at high pressures and low temperatures, when particle volume and attractions become important. Gas laws do not justify claims that a real gas can reach zero volume.\n\nExample (vii)\nWhy does the smell of perfume spread across a room even when there is no strong breeze?\nSolution\nPerfume vapour molecules move randomly and diffuse from regions of higher concentration to lower concentration. Air movement may also assist spreading.\n\n7. COMMON JAMB TRAPS\nConvert Celsius to kelvin BEFORE applying Charles's law, the pressure law or combined gas law. Boyle's law requires constant temperature, Charles's law constant pressure, and the pressure law constant volume. Use ABSOLUTE gas pressure. Keep volume units consistent on both sides; for PV = nRT with R = 8.31, use Pa and m³.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\n1. What causes gas pressure? Answer: Collisions of moving molecules with the container walls.\n2. State Boyle's law. Answer: At constant temperature, P is inversely proportional to V for a fixed mass.\n3. What is 27 °C in kelvin using 273? Answer: 300 K.\n4. Which law applies at constant volume? Answer: Pressure law.\n5. Why must gas-law temperatures be in kelvin? Answer: The laws relate to absolute temperature.\n\nPRACTICE CONNECTION\nSketch P against V, V against absolute T, and P against absolute T with correctly labelled axes. Solve one example under each constant condition and one combined-law example."},"old":{"title":"16. Gas Laws and the Kinetic Theory of Matter","body":"This lesson has been developed in the revised Physics preview. Past-question mapping will follow when the Physics question bank is uploaded."}}];
